/* ÇEVİRİ en · ekokuma — arac/ceviri.py ile YAZILDI, elle düzenleme: py arac/ceviri.py uygula.
   Kayıt: id → [metin, kaynak_ozet] (olayda alan başına). Tasarım: denetim/CEVIRI-1103/TASARIM.md */
Ceviri.ekle("en","ekokuma",{
"iktisat-uc-ilke-provizyonizm":{"baslik": ["What did the Ottoman economy rest on? Three principles: provisioning, the treasury, tradition", "f3759c46"], "metin": ["■ First principle: need comes first (provisionism)\nAccording to Mehmet Genç, writing in TDV, the economic outlook of the Ottoman administrator in the classical period rested on the concept of 'need'. The task of the economy was to ensure that goods within the country were plentiful, of good quality and cheap. The basic unit was the kaza (judicial district): a town and the dozens of villages attached to it, headed by a kadı (judge). Goods produced in a kaza went first to meet the needs of the kaza, then to the army and the palace, then to crowded Istanbul, then to other regions. Only if something was left over from all this was export permitted.\n\n■ Second principle: the treasury (fiscalism)\nThe second aim was to protect the revenue of the treasury. But in an economy with low productivity and very expensive transport it was hard to increase revenue. Genç writes that Ottoman budgets, measured in gold and silver value, did not grow much from the 1580s to the 1780s and were almost always in deficit. When the two principles clashed, it was generally the treasury that was sacrificed and the people's provisioning that was protected.\n\n■ Third principle: tradition\nWhen the institutions matured in the mid-16th century, a third principle was added: preserving the old balance. The same formula was always used in disputes: nothing was to be done contrary to 'what has been done since time immemorial' (kadimden olagelene). One kanunname (law code) defines 'kadim' (ancient) as that whose beginning no one remembers.\n\n■ The difference from Europe: mercantilism\nIn the same centuries, according to Kemal Beydilli's article on Europe in TDV, mercantilism prevailed in Europe: preventing precious metal from flowing out by means of an active foreign trade, and protecting domestic industry behind high customs walls. The aim was to increase the power of the state. The Ottomans did the opposite: they restricted and taxed exports and let imports in freely at low duties. According to Genç, the capitulations too were nourished by this outlook. That is why, even when it was at its strongest, the state did not curtail them but on the contrary extended them.", "154437a7"]},
"iltizam-malikane-esham-zinciri":{"oneri": ["The state redesigned the right to collect taxes three times", "4065c421"], "sebep.b": ["Under the tax-farming (iltizam) system, the sale of the right to collect taxes at auction for short terms (one to three years) drove the tax farmer to overtax the reaya (tax-paying subjects) for quick profit and to exhaust the productive capacity of the revenue source (mukataa) — a fiscal vicious circle that repeated itself every time the tax farmer changed", "e05e4ab1"], "sonuc.b": ["With the introduction of the malikâne system, revenue sources (mukataas) were granted to the tax farmer (malikâne holder) for life at a fixed tax — an attempt to encourage the tax farmer from then on to invest in the reaya, since the tax was fixed while the profit was left free", "99c06f82"], "bag": ["According to TDV's article on the malikâne, in the last quarter of the seventeenth century rising war expenses and falling tax revenues had created a vicious circle; the malikâne 'was conceived as an institution that would make it possible to overcome this paradox'. The difference: in ordinary tax farming the auction was held on the amount of the annual tax (mal), whereas in the malikâne the amount of the annual tax was fixed in advance by the treasury, and the malikâne holder acquired his right by paying a lump sum down (muaccele). According to TDV's article on the esham, demand for the malikâne was limited to a narrow military class (women and non-Muslims were excluded); when, after the Ottoman-Russian War of 1768-74, the obligation arose to pay Russia an indemnity of 7.5 million kuruş, the state had to go beyond this limit and devised the esham system: the revenue of a mukataa was sliced into shares (sehim) and sold in return for a down payment (muaccele); the buyer did not interfere in management and only received an annual cash income in proportion to the amount he had invested.", "117f867a"], "metin": ["All three systems tried to solve the same problem — meeting the state's ever-growing fiscal needs without the capacity to collect taxes directly — in different ways. In tax farming, the short term (one to three years) pushed the tax farmer into exploitation; the malikâne softened this with a fixed tax for life, but it was open only to the military class, which limited demand; the esham widened demand by dividing the malikâne right into shares and making it — in theory — saleable to anyone. But the esham also bred its own crisis: according to TDV's article on the esham, the system grew rapidly (the annual interest payment rose from 400,000 kuruş to 2 million kuruş in ten years), and when the finance officials did the calculations in the 1780s, they found that the income from reselling the shares of deceased shareholders covered only a small part of the annual interest paid; the treasury came under a growing interest burden, and from 1792 new sales were halted. Malikâne sales themselves ended completely in the 1840s. Taken together, the three show the cycle of Ottoman finance in the seventeenth and eighteenth centuries of 'inventing ever more complex financial instruments to raise revenue, with each instrument breeding its own crisis'.", "cdd1a571"]},
"isyan-fenerli-voyvodalar":{"baslik": ["The Phanariot voivodes: why did Greeks from Istanbul rule Wallachia and Moldavia for 110 years?", "99330a42"], "metin": ["① The cause — the Pruth, 1711. The voivode of Moldavia, Dimitrie Cantemir (Kantemiroğlu), was a man who had lived in Istanbul for many years and had written an Ottoman history and a book on Turkish music; during his time as voivode he signed an alliance at Jassy with the Russian Tsar Peter. After the Ottoman forces had surrounded the tsar's army on the Pruth and Baltacı Mehmed Pasha had signed a treaty, Cantemir fled to Russia and became the tsar's adviser. The voivode of Wallachia, Constantin Brâncoveanu, on the other hand, was brought to Istanbul on the allegation that he had helped Peter on the Pruth, and was executed together with his four sons (1714) — curiously, it was Cantemir who had denounced him to the Sublime Porte, although it was he himself who had given the real help. The decision taken in the reign of Ahmed III arose from these two: no longer would native princes be appointed to the voivodeships of Moldavia and Wallachia, but Greek beys who could remain permanently under the control of the Sublime Porte. The first appointee was Nicholas Mavrocordatos, son of Alexander Mavrocordatos, dragoman of the Imperial Council: to Moldavia in 1711, and to Wallachia in 1716 after Stefan Cantacuzino. In the same years, in the face of the 18th-century Russian danger and the Austrian threat, Wallachia had begun to be regarded as the principal line of defence; Khotyn too was detached from the voivodeship in 1713 and made directly an Ottoman sanjak. Thus the logic of the system was one: internal administration to a trustworthy official, the frontier fortresses to the state.\n② WHO WERE THE PHANARIOTS — Fener (Phanar) is a district of Istanbul on the southern shore of the Golden Horn; when the seat of the Orthodox patriarchate settled there at the end of the 16th century, the district acquired its distinctive character, and wealthy Greek aristocratic families settled around the patriarchate. These families, called Fanariot in Western sources, sent their children to study in Italy; these “Phanariot gentlemen”, who spoke many European languages besides Turkish and Arabic and followed international politics, were, on returning to Istanbul, employed as dragomans of the Imperial Council and of the Imperial Fleet. The families listed by TDV are: Ghika, Callimachi, Caradja, Soutzo, Ypsilanti, Mourouzis, Ralli, Handjeri and Rosetti. These princes, called “hospodar” (gospodar) in Western sources, each became an official of the sultan and were appointed for three years at most. Nor was their influence confined to the two countries: until the Tanzimat, especially during the wars with Austria and Russia, they were active in the politics of the capital.\n   THE EXAMPLE OF ONE LIFE — Alexandre Handjéri (Hançerli). He was born in Istanbul; the family's nickname comes from a dagger (hançer) given as a reward for their service as the palace's private physicians in the time of Mehmed IV. In Ottoman sources his name appears as “Hançerî Bey, one of the beys of Bucharest”. Besides Western languages, he learned Turkish, Arabic and Persian; between August 1806 and February 1807 he was chief dragoman of the Sublime Porte, and between 7 March and 24 July 1807 voivode of Moldavia. After the deposition of Selim III he was forced to return to Istanbul; although he sought the voivodeship of Wallachia in 1815-1816 and in 1818, a new regulation made in 1819 deprived the Handjéri family of this right — the reason given being that they had established secret relations with the Filiki Eteria and helped Alexander Ypsilanti. When the revolt broke out in 1821 he fled to Odessa with his family, worked in Russia as a translator of Oriental languages, settled in Moscow and died there on 12 June 1854; he is known for his French-Arabic-Persian-Turkish dictionary (Moscow 1840-1841). 📌 The DATE in this biography is important: the Sublime Porte's distrust of a Phanariot family was set down in a DOCUMENT in 1819 — that is, two years before the revolt.\n③ HOW THE ERA PASSED — Two TDV articles describe the same era in different tones, and both are worth reading. The article on Moldavia is measured: in the time of these voivodes the moral condition of the Principalities was shaken and bribery and intrigue increased; nevertheless serfdom (bondage to the soil) was abolished, some personal liberties were granted to the peasants, and hospitals and schools were opened in many places. The article on Wallachia is much harsher: the era of the Phanariot Greek beys, which lasted more than 100 years, “is a period still remembered with hatred in Wallachia”; the beys supported by the patriarchate exploited the local people together with the relatives and Greek business partners they brought with them. The same article also adds a political dimension: after 1750 these beys, in cooperation with the patriarchate of Istanbul, sought a way to revive the old Byzantine State and spread Byzantine nationalism; Wallachia became the economic source feeding this nationalism and its training ground. TDV nevertheless records the positive side: in the Phanariot era Wallachia opened up to the West, established schools and hospitals, and even took forward-looking steps such as the abolition of slavery — but the people lived amid great hardship.\n④ THE END — 1821, THE PHANARIOTS THEMSELVES ENDED THE SYSTEM. According to TDV's article on Wallachia, Tudor Vladimirescu prepared a revolt in 1821 with the aim of getting rid of the Phanariot Greeks who were grievously exploiting the people; this coincided in date with Alexander Ypsilanti's Eteria movement. The two movements DID NOT UNITE: to Ypsilanti's call to fight together against the Turks, Vladimirescu replied, “I rose up not against the Turks but against the Greeks”; his soldiers clashed with Ypsilanti's soldiers near Bucharest, and the Wallachian commander was betrayed and killed by Ypsilanti. TDV also notes that Russian historians wished to present these two movements as a single movement directed against the Turks in the Balkans. On the Moldavian side, by contrast, the voivode himself was part of the revolt: the movement organised by Ypsilanti was supported by the voivode of Moldavia, Michael Soutzo (Mihail Suça), who was of Greek origin. Thereupon Mahmud II abandoned the appointment of Phanariot beys; in 1822 he appointed to Moldavia one of the native princes, Ion Sandu Sturdza, and in Wallachia too he set about bringing the native families to power as before. The 110-year system was closed not by a reform of the Sublime Porte but by the joining of the revolt by the system's own personnel.\n   ⇒ WHAT APPEARS ON THE MAP — Throughout these 110 years Wallachia and Moldavia remain on the map in the VASSAL colour; what changes is not the owner of the land but who the voivode is. The shell around them, however, is dark, and that shell was built up stage by stage: Kiliia (Kili) and Akkerman have been direct Ottoman sanjaks since 1484, Bender-Budjak since 1538, and Khotyn since 1713. In other words, this card appears NOT as a change of colour on the map but in the chronology — the administrative personnel changed in 1821 did not move the borders.", "8a50d36c"]},
"isyan-sirp-milli-anlati":{"baslik": ["The Serbian uprisings in the Serbian narrative: not a “revolt” but the “Serbian Revolution”", "dbc95e39"], "metin": ["① The contemporary narrative of the other side — According to Dušan T. Bataković, the movement that began in 1804 was at first a peasant uprising against the local Janissaries, and after 1805 it gradually acquired a national character. The rebels donned the coat of arms of the medieval Nemanjić dynasty; the Governing Council (Praviteljstvujušči sovjet), established in 1805, held its sessions in Smederevo beneath a portrait of Emperor Stefan Dušan. The letter of authority that Karađorđe gave in 1806 to the delegation that was to go to the Habsburg and Russian emperors was written with the phrase “on behalf of the whole Serbian nation” and with the signature “Karađorđe Petrović, commander-in-chief in Serbia”. The report of an Ottoman official held captive in Serbia in 1806 lays bare the connection in the rebels' minds: they always keep with them Jovan Rajić's history book telling of Knez Lazar, and it is this that drove them to revolt. In other words, on the Serbian side 1804 was constructed as the continuation of Kosovo in 1389 (for the Kosovo epic itself: karsi-kosova-1389). Intellectual support came from the other side of the border: Dositej Obradović's ode beginning “Arise, Serbia” became the rebels' anthem, and in 1805 Count Sava Tekelija sent the first 500 copies of the map of Serbia, Bosnia, Dubrovnik and Montenegro that he had printed in Vienna directly to the rebel leadership. An interesting detail: the first historian of the Serbian revolution was a Greek — Triantafillos Doukas printed his History of the Slavo-Serbs in Budapest as early as 1807. The first great narrative in Serbian is Vuk Stefanović Karadžić's book Miloš Obrenović, knjaz Serbii, ili gradja za srpsku istoriju našega vremena, printed in Buda in 1828; in today's academic studies the scenes of 1815 are still based on it page by page.\n② TODAY'S MEMORY AND HISTORIOGRAPHY — The one who gave it its name was the German historian Leopold von Ranke: because of the long-term effect it left on the political and social order of the region, he likened the uprising of 1804-1813 to the French example and described it as the “Serbian Revolution” (History of Servia and the Servian Revolution, London 1848). Today's Serbian historiography has broadened this name: Srpska revolucija 1804-1835 is divided in two — the period of war (1804-1815) and the period of peace (1815-1835); the milestone separating the two is the Second Serbian Uprising (Radoš Ljušić's periodisation). That is why, in Serbian memory, 1815 is the date not of a firman but OF AN ASSEMBLY: on 23 April 1815, on Palm Sunday (Cveti), the people's assembly that gathered beside the church in Takovo elected Miloš Obrenović (1783-1860), obor-knez of the nahiyes of Kragujevac, Rudnik and Čačak, as vožd (leader) of the uprising. In Serbian sources the end of the uprising too is not a surrender but an agreement: the VERBAL agreement reached in Belgrade on 6 November 1815 between Maraşlı Ali Pasha and knez Miloš. With it, dual administration (dvovlašće) was established in the pashalik: alongside the Ottoman administration there were Serbian organs — the Narodna kancelarija, the highest judicial and administrative body, the obor-knezes of the nahiyes and the village kmets — and the Serbs collected the tax. The verbal agreement was given legal form by eight firmans issued by the Sublime Porte in the winter of 1815/16; the jizya was fixed at the lowest bracket, 3 kuruş, and in the pashalik of Belgrade it was never raised until 1833. And within the same narrative stands a FOUNDING CRIME: on 25 July 1817, on the knez's orders, Đorđe Petrović Karađorđe, vožd of the First Serbian Uprising, was killed; two days later his severed head was delivered to the pasha of Belgrade, and the pasha sent it on to Istanbul at the beginning of August. Miloš, in his own words, never felt any remorse for this murder; on the contrary, he used the situation to strengthen his position. On 18 November of the same year the Narodna skupština that met in Belgrade proclaimed him — at his own wish — HEREDITARY knez. Nor was he the only rival to die: in 1817 knez Sima Marković and captain Dragić Gorunović, who opposed Miloš, were handed over to the pasha and beheaded on 21 March, and the knez also had their “accomplices”, voyvoda Pavle Cukić and pop Sava, killed. The Obrenović dynasty, that is, the Obrenović-Karađorđević feud that would divide Serbian politics until 1903, was built upon these purges.\n③ THE OTTOMAN NARRATIVE — According to TDV's article on Serbia, the Janissaries, who increased their influence in the sanjak of Smederevo after 1739, came to be the masters of Belgrade, levied heavy taxes on the people and forced the Serbian peasant into corvée; “the ever-increasing oppression by the Janissaries called dayıs and yamaks” brought about the revolt, which broke out in 1804 under the leadership of Karađorđe. In this narrative TDV regards the national character as acquired later, and also writes that the Serbian units that took Belgrade carried out a great massacre of the Muslims in the city. The Treaty of Bucharest of 1812 brought partial autonomy; in 1813 the revolt was completely suppressed and Karađorđe fled to Austria. For 1815, TDV's sentence is short and sets up an entirely different framework: “The second Serbian revolt broke out in 1815 under the leadership of a Serbian knez named Miloš Obrenović. Although the revolt was suppressed in the same year, from this date onwards the privileges granted to the Serbs began to be extended. Miloš Obrenović was appointed chief knez.” The real story behind the Ottoman documents lies between the two: when news of the revolt reached Istanbul, Mahmud II issued a firman on 25 May 1815 ordering the governor of Bosnia, Hurşid Ahmed Pasha, and the governor of Rumelia, Maraşlı Ali Pasha, to “root out the rebels and force them into obedience” (in Serbian historiography it is referred to as the “death firman”). The assembling of the army took a long time, and the units reached the border only in July; in the meantime Waterloo took place on 18 June, the international situation changed, and an order went to the governors to pacify the rebels by peaceful means. Maraşlı made better use of this interval than Hurşid Ahmed Pasha: he did not impose on the Serbs the condition of laying down their arms, contented himself with “obedience to the sultan”, in return carried the rebels' complaint against Süleyman Pasha Skopljak to Istanbul, and by a firman of 22 November 1815 himself took over the post of guardian (muhafız) of Belgrade.\n④ WHERE THEY DIVERGE — The name: on one side a “revolt” (and two separate revolts), on the other a single “revolution” (and a single process spanning 1804-1835). The ending: TDV says “the revolt was suppressed in the same year, Miloš WAS APPOINTED chief knez”; the Serbian academic narrative says “the uprising ended with the verbal agreement of Belgrade on 6 November 1815 and dual administration was established” — the two are describing the same months. The hero: Serbian memory has two founders, and each undermines the other; the heroic rebel Karađorđe and the pragmatist Miloš who had him killed. In the Ottoman source, by contrast, there is only “an appointed chief knez”, not the founder of a dynasty. What is not in dispute? The NUMBERS of the agreement: a jizya of 3 kuruş, the Narodna kancelarija, the collection of the tax by the Serbs — these rest on Ottoman firmans and are recorded in the same way on both sides. What they differ over is not the facts but the story into which the facts are placed. (For the language of Serbian textbooks today and the formula of the “five-hundred-year yoke”: bakis-sirp. For the question of the link between the Serbian revolt and the French Revolution: the relevant further-reading card.)", "c96163aa"], "kisa": ["In the Ottoman source, 1804 and 1815 are two separate revolts: the first was suppressed, and after the second Miloš Obrenović was appointed chief knez. In Serbian historiography, however, the two are two phases of a single event, and its name is the “Serbian Revolution” (1804-1835) — the name Ranke gave it by likening it to the French Revolution. This is why it is regarded as foundational: the modern Serbian state was born of this revolution. But the same narrative also carries a founding crime — Miloš's having Karađorđe killed.", "0127f90a"]},
"isyan-ypsilanti":{"baslik": ["Alexander Ypsilanti: the tsar's aide-de-camp, head of the Eteria, the man who crossed the Pruth", "86d9f4c5"], "metin": ["① Do not confuse the three Alexanders — The Ypsilanti family is one of the Phanariot families listed by TDV and produced three Alexanders in three generations. The grandfather, Alexandros Ypsilanti, was voivode in Wallachia and Moldavia at the end of the 18th century; TDV's article on Moldavia writes that in his time some obligations previously imposed on the people were lifted by Abdülhamid I (1774) — that is, half a century before the revolt the family belonged to the personnel the state trusted. The father, Constantine Ypsilanti, was voivode of Wallachia, and in 1804, immediately after Karađorđe's revolt, encouraged by the Russian foreign minister Count Czartoryski, he developed the idea of founding a great Christian state in the Balkans and sent the Serbian rebels arms, provisions and a small military unit. The son, Alexander Ypsilanti (1792-1828), is the man of 1821. This card is about him.\n② BIOGRAPHY — Alexander Ypsilanti held the rank of major general in the Russian army and was aide-de-camp to Tsar Alexander I. The Filiki Eteria (Society of Friends) was a secret organisation founded in 1814 by Greek merchants and politicians in Odessa, an important Russian port city on the Black Sea. The organisation first offered the leadership to Ioannis Kapodistrias; Kapodistrias refused and warned that an uprising would be folly. Thereupon the leadership was given to Ypsilanti in April 1820.\n③ THE SERBIAN CONNECTION — The Eteria's representative in Serbia was Georgios Olympios, who had taken part in the First Serbian Uprising and was known among the Serbs as “Kapetan Jorgaç”. In June 1817 the exiled Karađorđe was made to swear the oath of the Eteria in a garden at Galata near Jassy — at Constantine Ypsilanti's house; he was then taken to the Serbian border and was killed a few days after entering Serbia (on Miloš's orders; see isyan-sirp-milli-anlati). In January 1821 Alexander Ypsilanti sent knez Miloš a draft alliance of ten articles; the Eterist carrying the draft was caught at Ada Kaleh, taken to Istanbul and executed. Miloš did not rise up during the whole of the Greek war.\n④ THE PRUTH — In February 1821 Ypsilanti crossed the river Pruth from Russian soil, in the uniform of the Russian army, entered Moldavia and reached Jassy. His proclamation called for a fight for “faith and fatherland”, stressed the natural right to liberty and invited the Greeks to emulate the peoples of Europe who were rising up for “liberty and happiness”. The Russian support he expected DID NOT COME: Tsar Alexander I ordered him to lay down his arms and expelled him from the Russian army; the Congress of Laibach (Ljubljana), meeting at the time, condemned the uprising; in Istanbul the patriarch excommunicated him. Ottoman forces suppressed the movement in June. THE SOURCES CONTRADICT EACH OTHER AT THIS POINT, and the card gives both: TDV's article on Wallachia describes Ypsilanti as having “gone into action in 1821 in the Crimea with the support of the Russian tsar”; the academic source consulted (Heraclides-Dialla), however, says that the tsar rejected him. TDV's article on Greece, too, gives Odessa, not the Crimea, as the place where the organisation was founded. The text of the card took the academic side as its basis and did not hide TDV's wording.\n⑤ VLADIMIRESCU — When Ypsilanti came down into Wallachia, there was already another uprising there: that of Tudor Vladimirescu. According to TDV, Vladimirescu's revolt was against Phanariot Greek rule; to Ypsilanti's call to fight together against the Turks he replied, “I rose up not against the Turks but against the Greeks.” The two forces clashed near Bucharest, and the Wallachian commander was betrayed and killed by Ypsilanti. This is the critical sentence for Ottoman chronology: in the Wallachia of 1821 there was no SINGLE front against the Ottomans; there were TWO separate movements, one against Phanariot Greek rule and one against the Ottomans, and they destroyed each other.\n⑥ HIS END — The course Ypsilanti took in the Principalities is severely criticised in academic assessment: Heraclides and Dialla describe his conduct as a mixture of arrogance, cruelty and incompetence. The campaign ended in rout; Ypsilanti fled to the Austrian Empire, and the remaining Eterists (Iordache Olimpiotul, Iane Farmache and others) resisted the Ottoman army for some time longer, using churches and monasteries as their headquarters. Alexander Ypsilanti died in 1828. The real consequence of the movement, however, lies elsewhere: in the words of TDV's article on Moldavia, “the movement produced results in Greece before Wallachia” — the revolt defeated in Wallachia and Moldavia turned into a war of independence that was won in the Morea (see the entry of 25 March 1821 and, for the end of the Phanariot system: isyan-fenerli-voyvodalar).", "b26a94d0"], "kisa": ["The son of a Phanariot voivodal family, a major general in the Russian army and aide-de-camp to Tsar Alexander I. In April 1820 he took the head of the Filiki Eteria, in February 1821 he crossed the Pruth in Russian uniform, entered Jassy and started the Greek War of Independence. The tsar ordered him to lay down his arms and expelled him from the army, the patriarch excommunicated him, and Vladimirescu said, “I rose up not against the Turks but against the Greeks”; by June it was all over. He himself fled to Austria and died in 1828.", "753dbcc5"]},
"kabakci-isyani-niye-cikti":{"sebep.b": ["The incitement claiming that the yamaks (auxiliary garrison troops) in the Bosphorus forts were to be brought into the Nizam-i Cedid (New Order) system and made to wear Nizam-i Cedid uniforms; the killing of Mahmud Raif Efendi, Superintendent of the Bosphorus, and Halil Haseki", "cf38e5a2"], "sonuc.b": ["Selim III's abolition of the Nizam-i Cedid with all its institutions and practices, his deposition and the accession of Mustafa IV", "4e31054c"], "bag": ["The visible face of the revolt was Kabakçı Mustafa, a Janissary from Kastamonu; the TDV Encyclopaedia of Islam writes that the “real architects behind the scenes” of the uprising were Köse Musa Pasha, the deputy grand vizier (kaymakam), and the Shaykh al-Islam Topal Ataullah Mehmed Efendi.", "8f1c1945"], "metin": ["The setting. The Nizam-i Cedid had been launched after the Treaty of Jassy of 1792 and had carried out important reforms, but from 1805 onward it began to be shaken by reactions and acts of protest. The Janissaries and members of the ulema who were dissatisfied with its measures found an opportunity to widen the opposition front by taking a common stand. The attempt to apply the Nizam-i Cedid in Rumelia as well came to nothing because of the resistance of the ayan (provincial notables) and armed clashes (the Second Edirne Incident, 1806) — the TDV article `selim-iii` regards this retreat as the turning point of Selim III's reign. The closure of the pilgrimage routes owing to the Wahhabi revolts and the arrival of the British fleet off Istanbul in February 1807 dealt a heavy blow to the sultan's authority; the fleet's blockade caused scarcity and high prices in the city, bringing the accumulated anger to the point of explosion.\n\nTHE SPARK. Because of the war with Russia, the army had left Istanbul on 12 April 1807. When the forts on the Bosphorus had to be reinforced against the Russian threat, the intention of bringing the yamaks there into the Nizam-i Cedid system became an opportunity for Köse Musa Pasha's covert agitation. The incitement to the effect that the yamaks would be made to wear Nizam-i Cedid uniforms, and that Mahmud Raif Efendi, Superintendent of the Bosphorus, and Halil Haseki, commander of the Hungarian battery, had been assigned to this task, resulted in the killing of these two men — this is the starting point of the revolt (25 May 1807).\n\nWHY it could not be suppressed. When the uprising became known in Istanbul, Köse Musa Pasha presented it as a mere outburst of indignation; the possibility of intervention by the drilled troops at Levent Farm and the initiative of the chief of the artillery were likewise eliminated by his schemes. Selim III was being given misleading information. The figure the TDV Encyclopaedia of Islam underlines is this: the sultan, who failed to show the foresight to use the Nizam-i Cedid soldiers in Istanbul, who numbered more than 10,000, first sacrificed the officials demanded of him and then agreed to the abolition of the Nizam-i Cedid. Even this did not end the revolt; he himself was deposed, and on 21 Rabi al-Awwal 1222 / 29 May 1807 Mustafa IV was placed on the throne.\n\nTHE AFTERMATH. On 31 May 1807 a “hüccet-i şer'iyye” (a sharia court deed) was drawn up: in return for the soldiers' undertaking not to interfere in affairs of state from then on, they would not be called to account for the revolt. The deed did not hold. After the revolt Kabakçı Mustafa obtained the post of Superintendent of the Bosphorus, and fifteen days before Mahmud II was placed on the throne he was killed by Ali Agha, the ayan of Pınarhisar, sent by Alemdar Mustafa Pasha. The verdict of the TDV Encyclopaedia of Islam is severe: with this revolt the era of renewal was closed until 1826, and the state's “great opportunity to reform itself in a way that would keep pace with the developments of the age” was thwarted.", "58445285"]},
"kapitulasyon-diplomatik-araçtan-mali-bagimliliga":{"oneri": ["It began 562 years earlier as the gift of an alliance and ended by taking away control over customs.", "31efcffe"], "sebep.b": ["The first commercial privilege that Orhan Gazi signed with the Genoese — a unilateral and revocable grant, designed as a diplomatic and military instrument of alliance of the period", "b804b273"], "sonuc.b": ["The unilateral abolition of the capitulations by the Ottoman government on the eve of the First World War — the end of a regime of privileges lasting four centuries, which by then had turned into a web of financial dependence structurally limiting sovereignty over customs and justice", "0a38b258"], "bag": ["The chronology's entry for 18 February 1536 sums up this transformation in a single sentence: 'the capitulations, a diplomatic instrument that day, would over the centuries turn into the gateway to financial dependence.' TDV's article on the ahidname confirms the intermediate link: after France (1536), England (1580) and the Netherlands (1612) received similar privileges; in the eighteenth century several European states attained 'most-favored-nation' status, and after the British first succeeded in lowering the customs duty to 3%, others who paid 5% also began to trade under the British flag — in other words, there was a competitive downward dynamic among the capitulations. The entry for 30 May 1740 marks the turning point: the privileges ceased to be limited to a single sultan and were tied to a permanent treaty, and 'the regime of capitulations, which became impossible to terminate unilaterally, could be abolished only at Lausanne' — even the abolition of 1914 was a unilateral declaration made under wartime conditions, and it became legally permanent only at Lausanne in 1923.", "f7594cf7"], "metin": ["At first, capitulation meant 'privilege': it was a grant that a Muslim state made unilaterally to Christian states so that they could trade in safety — the sultan could revoke it if he wished, and the foreign merchant was subject not to the court of the Ottoman qadi but to his own consul (judicial capitulation). Over time it changed along three axes: ① Duration — from a grant that in 1352 could be considered void on the sultan's death, it turned after 1740 into a permanent treaty; ② Scope — from only customs and judicial privileges at first, it expanded with Baltalimanı in 1838 into the legal basis for opening the whole Ottoman market (including domestic trade) to free competition; ③ Number — limited to one or two states in the sixteenth century, in the nineteenth it spread automatically, through the 'most-favored-nation' clause, to almost all the great European states. When these three axes came together, the capitulation turned from the 'diplomatic gesture' of the beginning into a structural constraint under which the Ottoman government could not determine its own customs tariff or its jurisdiction over foreigners on its own territory. Even its abolition shows this: the government could do it only by a unilateral declaration, at a moment when Europe was occupied with the First World War.", "9a4f8e0e"]},
"kimdir-celali-reisleri":{"oneri": ["Celal (1519)", "f6855d6d"], "ad": ["Who were the great Celali chiefs, and when did they rise in revolt?", "ec99ec53"], "metin": ["• Sheikh Celal of Bozok (1519) — proclaimed himself the Mahdi around Tokat; his revolt was harshly suppressed, and his name was left to the whole movement.\n• Karayazıcı Abdülhalim (1599-1602) — from the Kılıçlı tribe in Urfa; he was called 'Karayazıcı' because he had served as a clerk (yazıcı) of the sekbans. In November 1599 he took the citadel of Urfa and sent out decrees bearing the tughra of 'Halim Shah'. When he was promised a sanjak, he handed over Hüseyin Pasha, who was with him (Hüseyin Pasha was executed in Istanbul on 7 February 1600). On 23 September 1600 he defeated government forces on the plain of Kayseri; on 12 August 1601 he was defeated by Sokulluzade Hasan Pasha at Sepetli near Elbistan; news of his death reached Istanbul in February-March 1602.\n• Deli Hasan (1602-1603) — Karayazıcı's brother. On 20 April 1602 he had Sokulluzade Hasan Pasha, the commander-in-chief against the Celalis, killed at Tokat; he overran Kütahya and marched on Afyonkarahisar; in March 1603 he asked for pardon and was made beylerbey of Bosnia.\n• Tavil Ahmed (1604-1607) — rose from the ranks of the sekbans; he defeated Nasuh Pasha, the commander-in-chief against the Celalis. Although he was given the beylerbeylik of Shahrizor in 1605, he continued his revolt and took Harput; his son Mehmed seized the governorship of Baghdad with a forged firman, and Baghdad was recovered only in 1607.\n• Canbolatoğlu Ali Pasha (1606-1607) — sanjak governor of Kilis. He rebelled after his uncle Hüseyin Pasha was executed by Cigalazade in 1604; he took Aleppo, had the Friday sermon read and coins struck in his own name, and made contact with Tuscany. On 24 October 1607 he was defeated by Kuyucu Murad Pasha on the plain of Oruç.\n• Kalenderoğlu Mehmed (1604-1608) — plundered the districts of Manisa, Aydın and Bursa; the townspeople did not let him into the sanjak of Ankara given to him in 1607. He was defeated on 9 August 1608 at the Göksun Pass (Alaçayır), fled to Iran and was never heard of again.\n• The later generation — Abaza Mehmed Pasha, beylerbey of Erzurum, who took up the cause of avenging the blood of Osman II; Varvar Ali Pasha, governor of Sivas in the reign of Sultan İbrahim; Kara Haydar, who held up the Isparta-Uluborlu road, and his son Haydaroğlu; Gürcü Abdünnebi and Katırcıoğlu Mehmed (dispersed at Üsküdar-Bulgurlu on 7 July 1649); and Abaza Hasan Pasha, whom Köprülü Mehmed Pasha suppressed with difficulty.", "50dbcca6"], "kisa": ["From a sheikh to a clerk of the sekbans, from a bey of Kilis to a muleteer: the rebels who filled a century, only as far as the sources date them.", "a2a71098"], "not": ["Most of the dates are at the level of the year or month; a day has been written only where the source gives one. The sources are divided between 23 and 24 October 1607 as the day of the battle on the plain of Oruç (the fighting began on the 23rd, and the main battle took place the next day). Kalenderoğlu's defeat appears in one TDV article only as 'August 1608' and in another as 9 August 1608.", "4fd12094"]},
"kimdir-dorduncu-mustafa":{"baslik": ["Who Was Mustafa IV? A Reign That Lasted Fourteen Months", "b59dbb07"], "metin": ["Birth and years as a prince. He was born on 26 Sha'ban 1193 / 8 September 1779. His father was Abdülhamid I, his mother Ayşe Sineperver Sultan. He was brought up in the palace with a classical education; together with his brother Mahmud he accompanied his father on his tours of inspection in the city. Because his cousin Selim III was childless, he received affection from him and led a comfortable life as a prince.\n\nACCESSION. When Selim III, having put an end to the Nizam-i Cedid, was deposed at the close of the Kabakçı Mustafa revolt, he became sultan on 21 Rabi al-Awwal 1222 / 29 May 1807. He ratified the “hüccet-i şer'iyye” (sharia court deed) drawn up on 31 May 1807, stating that the rebels would not be called to account, and gave an assurance under oath. The document was of no use: those who had brought about the change of reign could not be prevented from interfering in affairs of state and forcing through whatever they wanted.\n\nTHE REIGN. The TDV Encyclopaedia of Islam describes these fourteen months as “such great turmoil, disorder and disintegration of the structure of the state that it would soon lead to regret over Selim's deposition”. The war with Russia that had begun in 1806 was continuing; the events in Istanbul spread to the front as well, the Janissaries revolted, the grand vizier was chased away, and the agha of the Janissaries, Pehlivan Agha, was killed by his own comrades. On 24 August 1807 a one-year armistice was signed at Slobozia, but this period was spent not in preparing for war but in settling internal scores. When, upon the resignation of the deputy grand vizier Köse Musa Pasha, Tayyar Mahmud Pasha, a mortal enemy of Selim III, was appointed in his place (November 1807), the Rusçuk Committee (Rusçuk yaranı), who wanted to put Selim back on the throne, gathered around Alemdar Mustafa Pasha.\n\nTHE FALL. Alemdar came to Istanbul with an army on 25 Jumada al-Ula 1223 / 19 July 1808; order was restored in the city, Kabakçı Mustafa was killed on his orders, and the Shaykh al-Islam Ataullah Mehmed Efendi was dismissed. When Alemdar stormed the Sublime Porte and headed for the palace to put Selim on the throne, Mustafa IV had the palace gates shut and ordered that his cousin Selim and his brother Mahmud be killed. Selim III was murdered; Mahmud escaped wounded and, as the last surviving member of the dynasty, was placed on the throne on 4 Jumada al-Akhira 1223 / 28 July 1808. It is related that Mustafa IV greeted the coup with the words “I have not stepped down from the throne; who has enthroned Mahmud?”, and that the sultan's imam Ahmed Kamil Efendi calmed him and saved him from Alemdar's wrath.\n\nDEATH. After being confined to his apartment in the palace, he did not refrain from sending word outside so that he might be restored to the throne. When about three months later Alemdar was attacked and killed and the rebels wished to march on the palace, he was strangled with the sash from his own waist on the order of his brother Mahmud II (28 Ramadan 1223 / 17 November 1808). His body was buried at Bahçekapı in the mausoleum of his father Abdülhamid I. Rumors spread among the rebels that the coffin was empty.\n\nFor the point on which historians differ regarding his personality, see the card `dorduncu-mustafa-sevilen-mi`.", "d41175c7"]},
"kimdir-hurrem-sultan":{"oneri": ["Selim (later Selim II)", "1ea175ea"], "ad": ["Hürrem Sultan", "091becb1"], "metin": ["While a concubine of Sultan Suleiman the Lawgiver, she was formally married to him around 1533-34, creating a break with the past not seen under any earlier sultan and in effect inaugurating the institution of “Haseki Sultan”. She pursued a long-term policy in the palace to bring her son Prince Selim (later Selim II) to the throne: TDV reports that she had a hand in the fall of Grand Vizier İbrahim Pasha in 1536 and in the execution of the rival heir, Prince Mustafa, in 1553 — the full extent of this latter claim is disputed among historians. By marrying her daughter Mihrimah to Rüstem Pasha (1539), who thus became an imperial son-in-law, she built a broad alliance in the palace; this alliance also had a share in Rüstem's return to the grand vizierate for a second time in 1555. Besides the Haseki Complex in Istanbul, she is also known for her charitable work through the foundations she established in Mecca, Medina and Jerusalem. She died on 15 April 1558 and is buried in the Süleymaniye Complex.", "b6e2678f"], "kisa": ["From concubine to wedded Haseki Sultan: Suleiman the Lawgiver's first and only formally married wife, she became the most powerful woman in the palace for the sake of her son's throne.", "6dc1f90c"], "not": ["The extent of her part in the execution of Prince Mustafa is disputed; the dates of her marriage, foundations and death are certain.", "8b616b6d"]},
"kimdir-kanuni-tahta-cikis":{"oneri": ["Suleiman's Accession to the Throne", "c82a04d6"], "ad": ["Suleiman I (the Lawgiver) — accession to the throne", "90e2583e"], "metin": ["He was born on 6 November 1494 in Trebizond, where his father, Prince Selim, was sanjak governor; his mother was Hafsa Sultan. He spent his childhood in the palace at Trebizond; according to Evliya Çelebi, it was here that he learned the goldsmith's craft from a Greek master, together with his foster brother Yahyâ. Because of Bayezid II's hesitations, he received a sanjak late and was sent to Caffa in 1509; from there he followed and supported his father's struggle for the throne. After Selim took the throne and eliminated his brothers who claimed it, Suleiman, as sole heir, was sent to Manisa in 1513 and stayed there for about seven years. The news of his father's death on 21-22 September 1520 arrived with a messenger from Grand Vizier Pîrî Mehmed Pasha; travelling overland, he reached Üsküdar on 30 September, crossed to the palace and ascended the throne, and the next day he received his father's body at Edirnekapı. His first acts were read as signs proclaiming the style of the new reign: he allowed some 600-800 artisans and notables whom Selim had deported from Tabriz and Cairo to return to their homelands, lifted the ban on the silk trade with Iran and compensated the merchants whose goods had been seized, punished officials and soldiers who had oppressed the people, and consented to the return to Egypt of the last Abbasid caliph, who had been brought to Istanbul. Before the second month of his reign was out, Janbirdi al-Ghazali, the beylerbey of Damascus, rebelled; the revolt was crushed in January 1521 and turned the new sultan toward a great campaign, against Belgrade. The Venetian envoy who saw him in 1520 described him as a tall, slender but hardy and amiable young man whose beard and moustache were barely visible.", "7446ca21"], "kisa": ["A prince who came to the throne without a rival: his father had done away with Suleiman's uncles for the sake of the throne, while Suleiman's first act was to send home those his father had exiled.", "1318839e"], "not": ["The title \"Kanûnî\" (the Lawgiver) is not contemporary with him: according to TDV, it first appears in the eighteenth century in Dimitrie Cantemir's Ottoman history and became widespread in the nineteenth century; Westerners of his time called him \"the Magnificent\" or \"the Great Turk\". The sources give differing dates for his birth (Mecdî says April-May 1496).", "5e07e638"]},
"kimdir-kosem-sultan":{"oneri": ["Kosem Sultan", "1409ad69"], "ad": ["Kösem Sultan", "afe2d0c1"], "metin": ["She was the haseki of Ahmed I, the mother of Murad IV and Sultan Ibrahim, and the grandmother of Mehmed IV. When Murad IV came to the throne at the age of eleven or twelve (1623), it was she who in effect conducted state affairs. Because of Ibrahim's mental health problems she maintained her influence in his reign too (1640-48). After the accession of Mehmed IV at the age of seven in 1648, she acted as regent with the title “Grand Valide”, but this time she entered into a power struggle with the young sultan's own mother, Turhan Hatice Sultan. This rivalry ended on 2 September 1651, when a group of aghas siding with Turhan killed Kösem inside the harem — it is the only instance in Ottoman history of a valide sultan being killed in this way.", "8fde078f"], "kisa": ["The only valide sultan who in effect ruled the state during the reigns of two sons and a grandson and was finally strangled to death in the harem.", "1abfcddf"]},
"kimdir-kuyucu-murad-pasa":{"oneri": ["Kuyucu Murad Pasha", "ea29cc25"], "ad": ["Kuyucu Murad Pasha — how did he suppress the Celalis?", "a6f77930"], "metin": ["He was a devshirme (child-levy recruit) of Croatian origin; he is estimated to have been born around 1522. He was dismissed from the beylerbeylik of Yemen on rumours of ill-gotten wealth and shut up in Yedikule, and was later pardoned. While retreating from a night engagement during the Tabriz campaign of 1585, he fell with his horse into a deep well, was taken prisoner by the Safavids and was held at Alamut until the peace of 1590. He distinguished himself at Haçova, conducted the peace of Zsitvatorok through his son-in-law Kadızade Ali Pasha and became grand vizier on 11 December 1606.\n\nHis campaign consisted of three moves. The first move (1607): When he set out from Üsküdar at the beginning of summer, he gave the governorship of the sanjak of Ankara to Kalenderoğlu, who was threatening Bursa — his aim was to keep him occupied and secure his own rear. At Konya and Adana he eliminated minor chiefs (Saraçoğlu, Cemşid). When Canbolatoğlu laid an ambush at the Bakras Pass, he took a long way round and came down by way of Belen to the plain of Oruç. Canbolatoğlu, who had more than thirty thousand musket-bearing soldiers, at first had the upper hand; through Tiryaki Hasan Pasha's tactic of hiding the guns on the slopes of the pass and drawing the enemy within cannon range, the Celali army broke up (24 October 1607). He spent the winter in Aleppo.\n\nThe second move (1608): He forestalled an alliance by giving the sanjak of İçel to Muslu Çavuş, a possible ally of Kalenderoğlu, who had begun plundering again after being refused entry to Ankara, and defeated Kalenderoğlu in August 1608 on the Göksun uplands (Alaçayır). With Janissaries carrying a week's provisions, he pursued Tavil Halil, who was fleeing toward Iran, and his brother Meymun for six days and six nights and caught them at Karahisarışarkî. On 18 December 1608 he returned to Istanbul with some 400 Celali banners.\n\nThe third move (1609): Concealing it even from the sultan, he spread the rumour that a campaign against Iran would be opened, assembled the army at Üsküdar and, when the rebel chiefs he had summoned to join the army arrived — for example Yusuf Pasha, who had rebelled around Aydın — he eliminated them (the 'Üsküdar campaign'). That same winter he issued a justice decree (adaletname) forbidding officials to oppress the people and tried to secure the return of those who had abandoned their homes.\n\nThe price: According to the TDV, Celali pressure in Anatolia was broken above all by his harsh and bloody measures; in his inquiries thousands of people were killed on the grounds that they were Celalis. In 1610 he marched on Iran, spent the winter in Diyarbekir and, while preparing a new campaign, died at Cülek on 5 August 1611; his body was transferred to his tomb at Vezneciler in Istanbul.", "1b7ea3fd"], "kisa": ["A grand vizier past eighty broke up the great Celali armies of Anatolia in two years: with bargaining, trickery and blood.", "d72133a7"], "not": ["Tradition: According to an account going back to Peçuylu İbrahim, he killed some 60-70,000 Celalis and filled the wells he had dug at his halting places with their corpses. According to another tradition, his rival Nasuh Pasha had him poisoned at a banquet. Neither is a fact confirmed by contemporary documents. The starting day of the campaign differs in the sources (15 June, 2 July or only 'July 1607').", "1b9cb7d0"]},
"kimdir-mihrimah-sultan":{"oneri": ["Mihrimah Sultan", "f7be1265"], "ad": ["Mihrimah Sultan", "f7be1265"], "metin": ["She was the daughter of Sultan Suleiman the Lawgiver and Hürrem Sultan, and the sultan's only surviving daughter. In 1539 she was married to Rüstem Pasha; it is reported that, together with her mother, she was instrumental in her husband's twice being appointed grand vizier, in 1544 and in 1555 — at first she supported her brother Prince Bayezid, but after the struggle for the succession intensified she supported Prince Selim. She was a figure whom her father consulted on important matters in his old age and who preserved her influence in the palace until her death. She had a complex built in Üsküdar and another, designed by Mimar Sinan, at Edirnekapı, and made a large donation for the repair of the water channels in Mecca (the “Mihrimah Water”). She died on 25 January 1578.", "7b625637"], "kisa": ["Suleiman the Lawgiver's only surviving daughter; the woman her father consulted on state affairs in his old age.", "994041b8"]},
"kimdir-nurbanu-sultan":{"oneri": ["the Atik Valide Complex in Üsküdar (mosque, madrasa, soup kitchen, library)", "36dacb68"], "ad": ["Nurbanu Sultan", "a8d4b91a"], "metin": ["She was the haseki of Selim II and the mother of Murad III. With her son's accession in 1574 she became the first person to bear the title “Valide Sultan” (queen mother) officially, and she was influential in state affairs and appointments. According to TDV, the close diplomatic contact she maintained with Venice contributed to easing the Ottoman-Venetian tensions of the period; it is also reported that she corresponded with Queen Catherine de' Medici of France and played a role in the development of trade relations. She built the Atik Valide Complex in Üsküdar (mosque, madrasa, soup kitchen, library) — the library in the complex is cited as the first library founded with a woman as its patron. She died on 7 December 1583, and her son Murad III attended her funeral in person.", "8b346af3"], "kisa": ["The first official “Valide Sultan” in 1574: the woman who in effect began the institution.", "21402780"]},
"kimdir-orhan-gazi":{"oneri": ["Bursa (1326)", "c250031d"], "ad": ["Orhan Gazi", "de9c91f8"], "metin": ["He was the son of Osman Gazi; the year of his birth is not certain. From the fact that he was referred to as a \"yiğit\" (valiant young man) when he married Nilüfer Hatun in 1299, the TDV infers that he was about eighteen at the time. According to tradition, he was married to Nilüfer, the daughter of the tekfur of Yarhisar; Süleyman and Murad were born of this marriage. During his father's lifetime he was sent on campaigns alongside experienced commanders such as Akça Koca, Konuralp and Köse Mihal; since he had been at the head of the army since 1305, the beylik passed to him without any struggle for the throne. Bursa (1326), Iznik (1331) and Izmit (1337) were taken in his time, and the beylik of Karesi was added to his realm. Ibn Battuta, who saw him, writes that he was the most powerful of the Turkmen sultans in wealth, territory and soldiers, and that he constantly made the round of his hundred or so fortresses and stayed no more than a month in any city. In an inscription of 1337 he is referred to by the title \"sultan al-ghuzat\" (sultan of the ghazis). His eldest son Süleyman Pasha, who carried out the conquests in Rumelia, died before him; Edirne was taken in 1361 by Prince Murad and his tutor (lala) Şahin. Shortly after the capture of Edirne, when he was already well advanced in years, he lost his life in March 1362 (Jumada al-Awwal 763) in a plague epidemic in Bursa; it is known that as early as 1354 he was suffering from a liver ailment and was treated by a Greek physician named Taronites. His tomb is beside his father's. When he died, only Murad, İbrahim and Halil of his six sons were still alive; he was succeeded by Murad I.", "d88b6b11"], "kisa": ["He took over a frontier beylik from his father and left his son a state that had established a bridgehead in Rumelia; a plague epidemic in Bursa ended his life.", "047f2d2d"], "not": ["For the information that plague was the cause of death, the TDV relies on the edition of the Byzantine short chronicles (Schreiner).", "c8a165e1"]},
"kimdir-safiye-sultan":{"oneri": ["Safiye Sultan", "cad11635"], "ad": ["Safiye Sultan", "cad11635"], "metin": ["She was the haseki of Murad III and the mother of Mehmed III. She became valide sultan on her son's accession in 1595; in TDV's words, she had a say in many appointments, from the grand vizier to the Shaykh al-Islam. She is the only Ottoman valide sultan to have corresponded directly and exchanged gifts with Queen Elizabeth I (in 1593 she sent embroidered fabrics in return for a jewelled portrait) — this correspondence was part of the Anglo-Ottoman commercial diplomacy of the period. In 1598 she began the construction of the New Mosque (Yeni Cami) at Eminönü, but when she was removed from the palace on her son's death in 1603, the construction was left unfinished; the building would be completed only more than sixty years later, in 1665, by another valide sultan, Turhan Hatice. She died in January 1619.", "15189a20"], "kisa": ["The only Ottoman valide sultan to correspond directly with Queen Elizabeth I of England.", "4fa8a360"]},
"kimdir-turhan-hatice-sultan":{"oneri": ["Turhan Hatice Sultan", "89c0cc74"], "ad": ["Turhan Hatice Sultan", "89c0cc74"], "metin": ["She was the mother of Mehmed IV. She became valide sultan when her son came to the throne at the age of seven in 1648, but for the first three years she remained in the shadow of her mother-in-law, Kösem Sultan. In 1651, when a group of aghas loyal to her killed Kösem, she came to power alone. In 1656, on the advice of her counsellor Kasım Agha, she brought to the grand vizierate Köprülü Mehmed Pasha, an elderly provincial pasha who laid down four conditions, and left the administration of the state to him; this appointment began the Köprülü era, which would last nearly half a century. In 1665 she had the New Mosque (Yeni Cami), which her mother Safiye Sultan had left unfinished, completed. She died in Edirne on 5 July 1683 and is buried in the New Mosque Complex in Istanbul.", "88180f76"], "kisa": ["The valide sultan who, having eliminated her rival Kösem and entrusted the state to Köprülü Mehmed Pasha, opened an era that would last nearly half a century.", "17493198"]},
"kinalizade-ahlak-i-alai":{"baslik": ["Kınalızâde Ali Çelebi and the Ahlâk-ı Alâî", "db54bc77"], "metin": ["Kınalızâde wrote the Ahlâk-ı Alâî in 1564, while he was qadi of Damascus, and presented it to the beylerbey (governor-general) of Damascus, Semiz Ali Pasha; the word \"Alâî\" in its title also refers to this dedication. The book consists of an introduction and three books: individual ethics (temperament, virtues and vices, the etiquette of speech), family ethics (the Turkish-Islamic family, the duties of the head of the household, the upbringing of children) and political ethics (the administration of the state, the qualities of the ruler, the central place of justice). According to TDV, the work is neither a translation nor a compilation of old Persian sources; it draws on transmission-based, philosophical and Sufi conceptions of ethics. It was read as a textbook in the madrasas, served as a source for later Turkish books on ethics, and there are more than seventy copies of it in the libraries of Istanbul.", "6bb3b8aa"], "sanatci.ad": ["Kınalızâde Ali Efendi (Ali Çelebi)", "bc5ad7ba"], "sanatci.hayat": ["Born in Isparta in 916 (1510); in Istanbul he studied under Kara Sâlih and Çivizâde Muhyiddin Mehmed Efendi. In a career that began in 950 (1543) as professor at the Hüsâmiye Madrasa in Edirne, he served as qadi of Damascus, Cairo, Aleppo, Bursa, Edirne and Istanbul; in Muharram 979 he became kazasker (chief military judge) of Anatolia. He fell ill at Edirne while in the retinue of Selim II and died on 6 Ramadan 979 (22 January 1572).", "0dedab6c"], "sanatci.onem": ["The author of the best-known Ottoman book on ethics written in Turkish; his work was long taught in the madrasas.", "874b4c3c"]},
"kirim-eminek-kefe-1475":{"oneri": ["What brought the Crimea to the Ottomans was not a pitched battle but a tribal bey whom the Genoese had had removed from office…", "d82dcdca"], "sebep.b": ["The struggle for the throne between Hacı Giray's sons Nur Devlet and Mengli Giray after his death (1466); the Genoese of Caffa stoking this struggle and having Eminek, the bey of the Shirin, removed from office", "9a2ffdd0"], "sonuc.b": ["The taking of Caffa and all the Genoese ports by the fleet of Gedik Ahmed Pasha, and Mengli Giray's acceptance of Ottoman protection", "6414d820"], "bag": ["According to the TDV article on the Crimea, the khanate's greatest weakness was that the real masters of the state were the hereditary tribal beys; every rivalry within the khan's family easily turned into a civil war. Toward 1475 Eminek, the tudun (governor) of Caffa and bey of the Shirin, was removed from his office at the insistence of the Genoese on the charge of having come to an understanding with the Ottomans. Eminek assembled the tribes, rose in revolt and forced Mengli Giray to flee. Mengli, who took refuge with the Genoese, was this time imprisoned by the Genoese, who had come to terms with Nur Devlet. Eminek, who also fell out with Nur Devlet, appealed to the sultan against the Genoese. According to the article on Caffa, in the talks between the Conqueror and Eminek it had been agreed that, if the operation succeeded, Caffa would be left to the Ottomans.", "c6daa065"], "metin": ["Seizing this opportunity, the Conqueror sent a fleet of 100 vessels to the Crimea under the command of Gedik Ahmed Pasha; together with Caffa the whole coastal strip was taken in Safar 880 / June 1475. The Pasha set the imprisoned Mengli Giray free; Mengli took the khanate back from Nur Devlet, a friend of the Genoese, and agreed to be a friend to the sultan's friend and an enemy to his enemy. A month and a half later he confirmed this position in a letter he wrote to the sultan. Here the TDV also corrects a widespread belief: contrary to what is supposed, there is no separate document of vassalage; the bond rests on the agreement made with Gedik Ahmed Pasha and on the khan's letter. On the coastal strip stretching from west to east, from Sarıkirman to Sarıgöl, the sanjak of Caffa was established, while the interior remained with the khanate. From then on Caffa became the main base of Ottoman policy in the north and was also used as a prince's sanjak: Mehmed, the son of Bayezid II, and Suleiman — the future Lawgiver — son of Selim the Grim, served there as sanjak governors.", "ed385b93"]},
"kitapcik-istanbul-fethi-1453":{"baslik": ["The Conquest of Istanbul — a booklet on the siege: walls, cannon, chain and ships", "9ba20f4d"], "kisa": ["Read the 54-day siege with the plans, weapons and positions of both sides together: walls approaching 22 km in length, the chain across the Golden Horn, an army pressing from four directions, ships brought down overland and the order of the final assault.", "dee23e5e"], "bolumler.0.baslik": ["The city and its walls", "abfc8a02"], "bolumler.0.metin": ["According to TDV, the total length of the land and sea walls approached 22 km. There were about 5.5 km of walls along the shore of the Golden Horn and about 8 km from Seraglio Point (Sarayburnu) along the Marmara shore to Yedikule; these walls on the seaward side were single, and in places rose straight out of the sea. Along the land walls ran moats filled with sea water. In some places the walls were double, but they had not been modernised to meet the conditions of the age; because of Byzantium's financial straits they had not been properly repaired for a long time. Even so, they are regarded as an obstacle that was hard to overcome. The great length of the walls was a disadvantage for the small number of defenders facing a large army, while their thickness and height made the defence easier.", "53c35915"], "bolumler.1.baslik": ["Preparations before the conquest: Boğazkesen and the Rumelian shore", "279c5be2"], "bolumler.1.metin": ["Mehmed II planned the siege in person; it is related that he read books on siege tactics and worked on plans. The military side of the preparations was carried out by the team of Şehâbeddin, Saruca and Zağanos Pashas. In April 1452 construction of a new fortress began on the Rumelian shore of the Bosphorus at its narrowest point; completed in August 1452, the Boğazkesen Fortress was to cut Istanbul off from the supply routes coming from the Black Sea, secure the passage of fleets between Anatolia and Rumelia, and serve when necessary as a base for the besieging army. Immediately after the fortress was finished, the sultan suddenly appeared before the walls with a force of 50,000 and carried out a reconnaissance lasting three days. The preparations stretched financial resources to the limit, and new taxes caused unrest in society.", "74deab46"], "bolumler.2.baslik": ["Strategy: not a long blockade but a swift capture", "a5c96931"], "bolumler.2.metin": ["According to TDV's reading, Mehmed II calculated that a prolonged blockade would give time to any help that might come from Europe, and he aimed to take the city as soon as possible. The fact that a city taken by the sword had a different status in Islamic tradition, giving the sultan full right of disposal over it, is also reckoned to have entered into the calculation. According to the same source, the conquest was also seen as a requirement of the tradition of holy war (gaza) and, because Istanbul lay in a position that prevented uninterrupted communication between Rumelia and Anatolia in the middle of the two, as essential for the future of the state. Two independent sources reporting the great council held in Edirne (Tâcîzâde Câfer Çelebi and Kritovoulos) also record the objection of the cautious viziers concerning the strength of the walls.", "bc81a3a9"], "bolumler.3.baslik": ["The Ottoman army: numbers and components", "392e7045"], "bolumler.3.metin": ["Contemporary sources put the Ottoman forces at between 100,000 and 300,000; TDV says that those who took part in the siege numbered around 100,000 or slightly more, and writes that it counts a quarter of these as Janissaries and standing army (kapıkulu) troops. The main strength consisted of the timar-holding soldiers of Anatolia and Rumelia; it assigns the most effective role in the fighting to the kapıkulu, who were infantry, and to the azebs who fought in the front line. Among those digging mines, it is stated, there were about 300 Serbian miners as well as German, Bohemian and Hungarian masters. The army also included volunteers, irregulars (başıbozuk), and sheikhs and dervishes of the Sufi lodges; scholars such as Akşemseddin, Molla Gürânî and Molla Hüsrev gave the sultan and the commanders moral support.", "c65c6497"], "bolumler.4.baslik": ["The defence: how many men, under whose command", "8656730f"], "bolumler.4.metin": ["According to contemporary Byzantine and Latin sources, the military force in the city was about 5,000, or 8-9,000 with the auxiliary units; the civilian population is estimated at about 30,000. Emperor Constantine XI set up his headquarters with his best units between Topkapı and Edirnekapı. The Genoese Giustiniani, who arrived in January 1453 with 700 men (400 of them Genoese), held the right wing and was later shifted to the centre. The Venetian bailo Girolamo Minotto guarded the sector of the Tekfur Palace, Teodor Caristino the line from Eğrikapı to the Xyloporta on the Golden Horn, and Filippo Contarini the stretch from the Marmara shore to the Golden Gate (Yaldızlıkapı); Alvisio Diedo took charge of the fleet, Loukas Notaras of the shore of the harbour on the Golden Horn, and Cardinal Isidore, with 200 men, of Seraglio Point. In the sector of the Tekfur Palace, the moat, which had filled up, was dug out again during preparations for the defence. Relying on Dolfin, TDV reports that although there were many Greeks in the city, those able to wield a sword were few and not skilled, whereas civilians played an important role in repairing the walls and supplying materials.", "5a765ccc"], "bolumler.5.baslik": ["The chain across the Golden Horn", "6c0024a7"], "bolumler.5.gun": ["1453-04-02", "ee0ef198"], "bolumler.5.metin": ["On 2 April the Venetian Bartolomeo Soligo stretched a chain across the Golden Horn and closed its entrance. The chain was drawn between the Tower of Saint Eugenius, near present-day Sirkeci, and the walls of Galata at Tophane (the Mumhâne point); behind the line Byzantine and Italian ships were ranged. The walls along the Golden Horn, however, are considered weaker than those on the Marmara and on the landward side; according to TDV, forcing the chain and entering the Golden Horn was one of the objectives of the Ottoman side.", "c2fdf153"], "bolumler.6.baslik": ["The Ottoman camp and the deployment of the corps", "ead2c086"], "bolumler.6.gun": ["1453-04-05", "f50efc9d"], "bolumler.6.metin": ["On 5 April Mehmed II pitched his imperial tent on the hill (Maltepe) to the left of the Lycus (Bayrampaşa) valley, and then moved the units a little forward to form a front. Zağanos Pasha, with the part of the army on the ridges of Beyoğlu and Kasımpaşa, kept the northern shores from Galata to Kâğıthane under pressure; it was decided that a bridge should be built between Hasköy and the land walls. Karaca Pasha, beylerbeyi of Rumelia, held the front along the walls from the Golden Horn to the area of the Tekfur Palace and Eğrikapı, while İshak Pasha, beylerbeyi of Anatolia, and Mahmud Pasha held the sector from Topkapı to the Golden Gate and the Marble Tower. In the centre, on the Edirnekapı-Topkapı line, were the sultan himself and the grand vizier; in this sector, considered the weakest part of the walls, the army's choicest soldiers and heavy cannon were concentrated. The fleet of Baltaoğlu Süleyman Bey prevented foreign ships from passing along the Marmara shores.", "9dd1d038"], "bolumler.7.baslik": ["Cannon and batteries", "d1ed0f69"], "bolumler.7.gun": ["1453-04-11", "751204b4"], "bolumler.7.metin": ["The most effective weapon of the Ottoman units before the walls was their cannon. TDV writes that the batteries were carefully sited and changed position as needed during the siege. According to Kritovoulos, the cannon stood in three emplacements in front of the walls at Edirnekapı, at the Tekfur Palace, between the Bayrampaşa stream and Topkapı, and at Eğrikapı; some Byzantine and Italian authors count fourteen batteries (according to some, nine), each with four large cannon. According to Barbaro, on 11 April the cannon were drawn up before the walls considered weak and placed at four positions. The breaches opened by the cannon were for the most part repaired immediately by the defenders. For the start of actual fighting TDV gives two dates: some researchers take 6 April as the basis, others 12 April (the firing of the great cannon).", "1d6f5163"], "bolumler.8.baslik": ["The first assault and the fleet put to the test", "8dce8f8e"], "bolumler.8.gun": ["1453-04-18", "6e11f9af"], "bolumler.8.metin": ["The fleet that assembled off Beşiktaş and Kabataş on 12 April is given in TDV's Istanbul article as 145 vessels (galleys, galleons and small craft); the article on Baltaoğlu Süleyman Bey, however, says about 400 ships, and the two articles diverge on this number. Between 12 and 18 April no serious assault was made apart from cannon fire. The first assault, launched on 18 April against the outer walls in the direction of the Bayrampaşa stream, came to nothing because of Giustiniani's effective defence; Barbaro puts the Turkish losses at 200.", "c45995c7"], "bolumler.9.baslik": ["The Genoese relief ships entered the Golden Horn", "4d59007c"], "bolumler.9.gun": ["1453-04-20", "e40a64ac"], "bolumler.9.metin": ["On 20 April three Genoese ships bringing provisions and aid appeared off Istanbul, together with a Byzantine transport that had joined them at the entrance to the Dardanelles. The Ottoman fleet met them off Yenikapı; a strong southwesterly wind prevented the oared Ottoman ships from manoeuvring, while the high-sided Genoese vessels easily broke through the blockade and entered the Golden Horn. This failure broke the army's morale and caused unrest among the ulema and the commanders: Akşemseddin openly reproached the sultan in a letter; while Çandarlı Halil Pasha favoured accepting the Byzantine peace offer, Akşemseddin, Zağanos Pasha and Molla Gürânî argued for continuing the siege. According to TDV, Mehmed II hesitated, but in the end decided to carry on the siege.", "ea3efc6a"], "bolumler.10.baslik": ["The ships brought down into the Golden Horn overland", "127c84e8"], "bolumler.10.gun": ["1453-04-21", "e50a663f"], "bolumler.10.metin": ["After the decision to continue the siege, the plan was put into effect to haul part of the fleet overland and launch it into the Golden Horn, in order to put pressure on the walls along the Golden Horn, which were weaker than the land walls. TDV supposes that this was not done all at once but required long preparation, and that the ships were drawn up with slipways and machines (‘cerr-i eskāl’ in Tursun Bey, the art of moving heavy weights), lined up on the ridges of Kasımpaşa and suddenly launched at night. On the night of 21 to 22 April some sixty to seventy ships, large and small, suddenly appeared in the Golden Horn; this both restored the soldiers' morale and caused great astonishment among the Byzantines. The route is disputed: some researchers argue that the ships were taken from Tophane by way of Humbaracıbaşı Yokuşu and Asmalı Mescid to Kasımpaşa, others that they were brought down from Dolmabahçe or Beşiktaş by way of Harbiye to Kasımpaşa or opposite Eyüp; the expression common to the Ottoman sources is ‘over / behind Galata’. The artillery of Zağanos Pasha on the ridges of Kasımpaşa and Okmeydanı fired so as to prevent the Byzantine and Italian ships in the Golden Horn from attacking; two ships of the Byzantine fleet were sunk by cannon fire. The bridge built close to the walls, which made it possible to cross from one side to the other, was also under the protection of this fleet.", "d283803c"], "bolumler.11.baslik": ["The May assaults, the tunnels, the siege tower", "71144ceb"], "bolumler.11.gun": ["1453-05-06", "83b0bcd5"], "bolumler.11.metin": ["On 6 May the siege intensified again with a general assault on the walls at the Bayrampaşa stream; on 12 May the attack was pressed between the Tekfur Palace and Edirnekapı, against the Edirnekapı-Eğrikapı walls that had been brought down by cannon fire, but no result was achieved. On 16 May the great tunnel that had been extended beneath the walls into the city was discovered by the Byzantines and collapsed with a countermine; later tunnels met the same fate. On 18 May an assault with a large siege tower failed, and the tower was burned and destroyed. According to Barbaro, a hide-covered passage had been made between the tower and the camp, and soldiers could advance to the walls without losses; those inside it had filled the moat in front of the walls and piled up the earth along the walls.", "6ee3e273"], "bolumler.12.baslik": ["The final offer of surrender and the decision for a general assault", "f7016b0f"], "bolumler.12.gun": ["1453-05-23", "0eab8778"], "bolumler.12.metin": ["In the fourth week of May news spread through the army that the Hungarians and a Crusader fleet had set out; the sultan's attempts to take the city peacefully were creating impatience and anxiety among the soldiers. On 23 May Mehmed II sent İsfendiyaroğlu İsmail Bey as envoy and announced that, if the city surrendered, the lives and property of its people would not be touched, the emperor would be given the Despotate of the Morea, and whoever wished could go wherever he liked; the reply was to the effect that, if the siege were lifted, there could be vassalage and tribute as before. In the council of war Zağanos Pasha opposed Çandarlı Halil Pasha's call for a compromise; the decision for a general attack was taken, and the choice of the day was left to Zağanos Pasha.", "72e97526"], "bolumler.13.baslik": ["The plan of the final assault and the crack in the defence", "f6764100"], "bolumler.13.metin": ["In the two or three days after 23 May great preparations were made for the final assault. The plan was this: Zağanos Pasha would attack the walls along the Golden Horn; Karaca Pasha, on his right, the area from the centre to the Golden Horn walls; İshak and Mahmud Pashas the sector extending from the centre to the Marmara shore; and the central forces, with which the sultan was, the walls above the Bayrampaşa stream; the centre of gravity would be between Topkapı and Edirnekapı. When the news reached the Byzantines, Giustiniani undertook to have the cannon under Notaras's command moved to the central sector; Notaras objected, thinking that the attack would come against the walls along the Golden Horn, but by the emperor's order the cannon were sent where Giustiniani wanted them. The population, Greek and Italian, Orthodox and Catholic, attended religious ceremonies together in Hagia Sophia; the emperor sent the commanders to their posts on the outer walls and had the gates of the inner walls closed behind them.", "329f7d7b"], "bolumler.14.baslik": ["The night of candles: 28/29 May", "b4189b68"], "bolumler.14.gun": ["1453-05-28", "09ab7f99"], "bolumler.14.metin": ["On the night of 28 to 29 May the Ottoman army held festivities and lit up the surroundings with candles; towards midnight the walls, which had been as bright as day, suddenly went dark, and the army completed its final preparations. TDV writes that these lights struck terror into the people of Byzantium.", "a3f4bfbf"], "bolumler.15.baslik": ["29 May: assault on all fronts", "ae9d0a52"], "bolumler.15.gun": ["1453-05-29", "08ab7e06"], "bolumler.15.metin": ["The final attack began on all fronts at once before dawn on Tuesday, 29 May. The fleet blockaded the Marmara walls as far as Samatya and in places landed azeb troops; between Edirnekapı and Topkapı, along the Bayrampaşa valley, the walls, which had been knocked down and in places were being repaired, were attacked in great strength. According to Barbaro, three successive assaults were made; an hour before daybreak the great cannon began firing at the repaired walls. 300 Turkish soldiers got over the second walls but, not receiving support in time, were driven back; immediately afterwards a large force got over the second walls (Barbaro writes 70,000; TDV says this figure is exaggerated). When Giustiniani was wounded he was carried to his ship, and the people, supposing that the city had fallen, rushed in panic to the harbour on the Golden Horn. The attacking columns at the breach at Topkapı and between Edirnekapı and the Canbazhâne Gate joined up and scattered the defenders at three points. According to Barbaro, the fleet under the command of Zağanos Pasha landed troops at Fener. Emperor Constantine XI, who had gone to fill the place left by Giustiniani, died fighting.", "4d4288ef"], "bolumler.16.baslik": ["The accounts diverge: who entered first, how the emperor died", "866bb41b"], "bolumler.16.gun": ["1453-05-29", "08ab7e06"], "bolumler.16.metin": ["TDV shows that the sources diverge on this point. Phrantzes (Sphrantzes) writes that a Janissary named Hasan climbed to the top at a ruined spot in the wall, got over the walls with some thirty Janissaries beside him and died from the blow of a stone, and that the emperor was wounded while fighting and died. Barbaro records that no information could be obtained about the emperor's death and that there were reports that he had killed himself by strangling. Doukas writes that a group of fifty entered through a gate that had been ‘left open’, while Zorzi Dolfin writes that the great cannon, moved opposite Topkapı, brought down the tower and filled the moat, and that the emperor was crushed to death. Among the Ottoman sources, Tursun Bey states that the emperor's horse stumbled and fell and that he was killed by an azeb who did not know who he was, while Kemalpaşazâde speaks of the entry through the breach at Topkapı and of the killing by an azeb of the emperor as he fled towards Yedikule, and notes that there are many accounts of this. On those who first mounted the walls the Ottoman sources generally give no information; only Bihiştî writes that the first to enter the city was his father, Süleyman Bey. Selâhattin Tansel argues that the first entry into the city may have been through the weak walls on the side of the Golden Horn.", "5ded8195"], "bolumler.17.baslik": ["What could not be found for this booklet", "bf830a8f"], "bolumler.17.metin": ["The following were searched for in TDV's Istanbul and Mehmed II articles and not found, and have therefore not been written: any statement that Greek fire was used in the siege; the name of the cannon founder Orban/Urban and the dimensions of the ‘Şâhî’ cannon; a day-by-day account of the remaining days of the siege (the days not included in the detailed chronology). The primary basis for the latter is the diary that Barbaro kept day by day (Diary of the Siege of Constantinople, Kostantıniyye Muhâsarası Ruznâmesi; TDV: barbaro-niccolo), and no online text of it was found. As for the length of the siege, TDV's Mehmed II article says fifty-four days, between 26 Rabi al-Awwal and 20 Jumada al-Awwal 857 (6 April - 29 May 1453).", "b3c1d646"]},
"liste-hanedan-ici-katl-1-1374-1562":{"oneri": ["Cases in which one member of the dynasty was killed or blinded on the ORDER of another member of the dynasty, in TDV…", "e0846a34"], "ad": ["Killings and executions within the dynasty (1) — from Murad I to Suleiman the Lawgiver, 1374-1562", "1a570d9d"], "metin": ["① c. 1374 · Murad I → his son Savcı Bey · father-son · the eyes of the prince, who rebelled together with the Byzantine heir to the throne, were put out with a hot iron (not killing but blinding; TDV dates the clash to spring 1374) · TDV murad-i. ② 1389 · Bayezid I → his brother Yakub · brother · as soon as he was raised to the throne at Kosovo, he had him killed so that he would not cause a civil war · TDV bayezid-i. ③ 1405-1406 · Mehmed Çelebi → his brother İsa Çelebi · brother · he had him caught at Eskişehir and strangled · TDV isa-celebi, fetret-devri (TDV's article 'Mehmed I' separately reports Zachariadou's view that İsa was killed by Süleyman in 1403). ④ 17 February 1411 · Musa Çelebi → his elder brother Emîr Süleyman · brother · he had him caught while fleeing and did away with him · TDV musa-celebi. ⑤ 5 July 1413 · Mehmed Çelebi → his brother Musa Çelebi · brother · he had him caught as he fled wounded from Çamurlu and had him strangled · TDV musa-celebi, fetret-devri. ⑥ Winter 1422 · Murad II → his uncle Mustafa Çelebi (‘Düzme’, the ‘False’) · uncle-nephew · he was caught at Kızılağaç Yenicesi and executed in Edirne (TDV also records another account that he managed to escape) · TDV murad-ii. ⑦ 20 February 1423 · Murad II → his brother Küçük Mustafa · brother · after İznik was taken, the prince, who was thirteen in 1422, was handed over by İlyas Bey and executed · TDV murad-ii. ⑧ 1451 · Mehmed II → his infant brother Ahmed · brother · he had him strangled as soon as he came to the throne; the provision of the law code permitting fratricide is also this sultan's · TDV mehmed-ii. ⑨ End of 1512 · Selim I → his nephews Musa, Orhan and Emîr (sons of Prince Mahmud), Osman (son of Âlemşah) and Mehmed (son of Şehinşah) · uncle-nephew · in Bursa, on the grounds of ‘the survival of the sultanate’ · TDV selim-i. ⑩ 1513 · Selim I → his brother Korkud · brother · he had him caught at Manisa and killed · TDV selim-i. ⑪ 1513 · Selim I → his elder brother Ahmed · brother · caught while fleeing from the battle of Yenişehir of 15 April 1513 and killed · TDV selim-i. ⑫ 1522 · Reign of Suleiman I → the son of Cem Sultan living on Rhodes · the Cem branch of the dynasty · when Rhodes was surrendered he was found and put to death (TDV does not give his name) · TDV suleyman-i. ⑬ 6 October 1553 · Suleiman I → his son Prince Mustafa · father-son · on the fatwa of Ebussuûd Efendi he was strangled in the army camp near Konya Ereğlisi; his young son Mehmed, who was with him, was also killed (grandfather-grandson) · TDV mustafa-celebi. ⑭ 23 July 1562 · Prince Bayezid, the son whom Suleiman I did not forgive → the prince, handed over by Iran, was strangled at Qazvin the moment he was delivered to Selim's chief messenger (çavuşbaşı); then his four sons, and also his three-year-old fifth son in Bursa, were killed · father-son, grandfather-grandson · TDV bayezid-sehzade.", "20d234ff"], "kisa": ["Events explicitly recorded in TDV in which one member of the dynasty was killed or blinded on the order of another member of the dynasty. Reports and suspicious deaths are in the third list.", "89443b7b"], "not": ["In lines without a day, only the year has been written; the date is the one given by TDV, not by the atlas.", "f29b9dc4"]},
"liste-hanedan-ici-katl-2-1574-1808":{"oneri": ["It is said that ‘fratricide was abolished’ after 1603; this list shows that the practice continued at intervals until 1808.", "d51c740a"], "ad": ["Killings and executions within the dynasty (2) — from Murad III to Mahmud II, 1574-1808", "f8dfe15b"], "metin": ["① 22 December 1574 · Murad III → his brothers Osman, Mustafa, Süleyman, Cihangir and Abdullah · brother · they were strangled on the night of the accession; they were buried on the same day as their father Selim II · TDV murad-iii, selim-ii. ② 27 January 1595 · Mehmed III → nineteen brothers, four of them grown (Mustafa, Bayezid, Osman, Abdullah) · brother · they were strangled on the night of the accession · TDV mehmed-iii. ③ 1603 · Mehmed III → his elder son Prince Mahmud · father-son · he had the son killed whom the soldiers supported and whom he suspected of coveting his throne (TDV gives no day) · TDV mehmed-iii. ④ 12 January 1621 · Osman II → his brother Prince Mehmed · brother · before setting out on the Khotyn campaign; the Shaykh al-Islam Esad Efendi refused to give a fatwa, and the fatwa was given by the military judge (kazasker) of Rumelia · TDV osman-ii. ⑤ 27 August 1635 · Murad IV → his half-brothers Bayezid and Süleyman · brother · they were killed on the night of the festivities for the Yerevan (Revan) victory; TDV writes that the event aroused sorrow and hatred · TDV murad-iv. ⑥ February 1638 · Murad IV → his full brother Kasım · brother · he had him executed before setting out on the Baghdad campaign because he considered him dangerous · TDV murad-iv, kosem-sultan. ⑦ 28 July 1808 · Mustafa IV → his cousin Selim III and his brother Mahmud · cousin, brother · as Alemdar Mustafa Pasha was marching on the palace, he ordered both to be killed; Selim was killed, while Mahmud escaped wounded and was raised to the throne · TDV mustafa-iv, selim-iii. ⑧ 17 November 1808 · Mahmud II → his brother Mustafa IV · brother · during the revolt that broke out after Alemdar was killed, he was strangled on his brother's order · TDV mustafa-iv.", "c1351433"], "kisa": ["It is said that ‘fratricide was abolished’ after 1603; this list shows that the practice continued at intervals until 1808. All of these events are explicitly recorded in TDV.", "b6236d89"], "not": ["TDV's article 'Selim III' records that the principle of seniority gave rise to good relations between uncles and nephews, but that the events of 1808 showed that this was not enough in extraordinary circumstances. In the articles on the sultans that were read (from Abdülmecid to Mehmed VI), no explicit record was found, after 1808, of a member of the dynasty being killed on the order of another member of the dynasty; since the death of Abdülaziz is disputed, it is in the third list.", "cab666c3"]},
"liste-hanedan-ici-katl-3-rivayet-ve-supheli":{"oneri": ["Events that the source calls a ‘report’, a ‘rumour’ or ‘suspicious’, and those in which not a member of the dynasty but…", "4123b380"], "ad": ["Killings and executions within the dynasty (3) — reports, suspicious deaths and rulers killed by hands outside the dynasty", "583bd284"], "metin": ["A · reported or suspicious. ① c. 1299 · Osman Bey → his uncle Dündar · uncle-nephew · according to Neşrî's account, in a disagreement over the policy of the beylik he shot him dead with an arrow; TDV's article 'Şehzade' traces the beginning of the practice to this event · report (Neşrî) · TDV osman-i, sehzade. ② After the Interregnum · Orhan, son of Emîr Süleyman, was blinded · nephew · TDV's article 'Mehmed I' mentions the fact but does not give the perpetrator or the date in that sentence · perpetrator unclear. ③ Date unclear · Oğuz Han, son of Cem Sultan, was killed · TDV mentions the elegy that Cem wrote for him, but does not give the perpetrator · perpetrator unclear. ④ 25 February 1495 · The death of Cem Sultan · TDV writes that it was very probably poisoning, and that according to one report the pope, who had been forced to give up the hostage, had him poisoned; it is not attributed to a member of the dynasty · suspicious. ⑤ 10 June 1512 · The death of Bayezid II · allegation involving father and son · TDV 'Bayezid II' writes that according to some local and foreign records he may have been poisoned, while 'Selim I' writes that the rumour that his son had him poisoned cannot be confirmed from other sources · suspicious. ⑥ 20 January 1639 · The death of Mustafa I · allegation involving uncle and nephew · TDV 'Murad IV' writes that according to one report Murad IV also had his uncle killed · report. ⑦ 1640 · On his deathbed Murad IV ordered the killing of his last brother İbrahim, and Kösem Sultan prevented it · TDV 'Murad IV' and 'İbrâhim' relay this as a report that cannot be definitely confirmed (citing Du Loir) · report. ⑧ Reign of Mehmed IV · when his sons were born he wished to have his brothers Süleyman and Ahmed killed, and his mother Turhan Sultan prevented it · a report based on Evliya Çelebi · TDV mehmed-iv · attempt, report. ⑨ 22 December 1756 · The sudden death of Prince Mehmed, son of Ahmed III · cousin · two TDV articles diverge: 'Mustafa III' writes that Osman III tried to do away with him and that he was probably poisoned, while 'Osman III' writes that the death was from illness and that the reports of poison must very probably be considered unfounded · disputed. ⑩ 4 June 1876 · The death of Abdülaziz · allegation involving uncle and nephew · the official announcement was suicide; the investigation that Abdülhamid II had opened in 1881 reached the conclusion that the death was murder, and Murad V was accused of having given the order; TDV states that the matter has been debated down to the present day · disputed · TDV abdulaziz. B · by hands outside the dynasty. ⑪ 20 May 1622 · Osman II · rebel Janissaries and sipahis deposed him and strangled him at Yedikule · TDV osman-ii. ⑫ 18 August 1648 · Sultan İbrahim · on a fatwa obtained after his deposition on 8 August, he was killed by the hand of the executioner in the presence, according to Kâtib Çelebi, of the Shaykh al-Islam, the grand vizier and the agha of the Janissaries · TDV ibrahim--padisah. ⑬ 2 September 1651 · The Grand Valide Kösem Sultan · connected to the dynasty by marriage; she was killed in the Harem by Başlala Uzun Süleyman Agha, whom Turhan Sultan had charged with the task, and his men (2-3 September 1651) · TDV kosem-sultan. Note: TDV 'Kösem Sultan' writes, relying on Karaçelebizâde, that it is stated that Kösem also had a hand in the strangling of Sultan İbrahim.", "f5e79cf5"], "kisa": ["Events that the source calls a ‘report’, a ‘rumour’ or ‘suspicious’, and sultans killed not by decision of a member of the dynasty but by decision of rebels or statesmen — deliberately not included in the first two lists.", "be53a56d"], "not": ["No line in section A should be transferred to the first two lists as established fact: either the source does not give the perpetrator, or it treats the event as a report, or two TDV articles contradict each other.", "4cf18e8d"]},
"menkibe-kuyucu-lakabi":{"baslik": ["Where does the nickname 'Kuyucu' (the Well-Digger) come from?", "6db090ee"], "metin": ["The first tradition: Murad Pasha had the corpses of the rebels he put to death in the Celali campaigns thrown into deep wells that he had dug at his halting places; this is why his nickname became 'Kuyucu' (from kuyu, 'well'). This account is related together with the claim that he eliminated 60-70,000 Celalis.\n\nThe second tradition: The nickname comes from a much earlier event. In the Tabriz campaign of 1585, while he was retreating as beylerbey of Karaman from a clash with the Safavids that lasted until the dark of night, he fell with his horse into a deep well and was taken prisoner by the Iranians; he was held for about five years in the castle of Alamut.\n\nThe second event itself (his fall into a well and capture) is documented by Selaniki's record; what is disputed is from which event the nickname arose. The TDV relates both traditions and chooses neither. The nickname 'Koca' (the Old), on the other hand, comes from his age of more than ninety.", "eb93fb07"], "kisa": ["There are two traditions, and both are tied to a well — but one puts his victims in the well, the other puts the man himself there.", "76994887"]},
"mora-despot-kardesler-1460":{"oneri": ["What delivered the Morea to the Ottomans was the hatred two brother despots bore each other; one a monk in Edirne…", "f1ee72f9"], "sebep.b": ["The despot brothers Thomas and Demetrios Palaiologos, who shared the Morea after 1449, constantly fighting each other, and Thomas openly refusing the tribute in 1458", "bd9424bc"], "sonuc.b": ["The Conqueror putting an end to the Despotate of the Morea in the campaign of 1460 by taking the whole peninsula except Venice's coastal fortresses", "d825ef35"], "bag": ["According to the TDV article on the Morea, following the refusal of tribute the Conqueror came with a great army in 1458, took the north of the peninsula (Patras, Vostitsa, Kalavryta, Corinth) and established Ottoman administration there. The quarrel between the brothers went on; according to the article on Mehmed II, Thomas, supported by the Westerners, gained control of the peninsula. In the spring of 1460, while Pope Pius II was preparing a Crusade, the sultan marched on the Morea again before an army could be assembled.", "80b8b7ec"], "metin": ["Here the paths of the two brothers parted. Demetrios, who chose to surrender, was left Lemnos, Imbros (Gökçeada) and Samothrace in the Aegean and Enez on the coast of Thrace; he spent the end of his life in retirement in a monastery and died in Edirne in 1470. Thomas, who put his trust in the West, took refuge in Italy, lived on a generous pension granted by the pope and died there in 1465. His son Manuel and other members of the Palaiologos family entered Ottoman service and became Muslims; the family was still in office even in the time of Bayezid II. But the expression \"the whole of the Morea\" is incomplete: Coron, Modon, Navarino, Nauplion (Anabolu) and Monemvasia remained with Venice. These were taken in the war of 1499-1502 and in the later wars that lasted until 1540; only then did the peninsula pass entirely to the Ottomans. According to the TDV, this was followed by a period of peace lasting more than two centuries; the tax registers (tahrir) show that up to the first quarter of the sixteenth century the population more than doubled, and the article describes the claim that the Greeks fled to the mountains from Turkish oppression as \"a historical fallacy\". The TDV dates the conquest only as \"the spring of 1460\".", "49e54601"]},
"nedim-lale-devri":{"baslik": ["Nedîm comes to the fore as the pioneer of Tulip Era poetry", "d3c40dbb"], "metin": ["Throughout the Tulip Era of 1718-1730, Nedîm developed a new style of poetry with his simplicity of expression and witty manner. This style, which grew up in the circle of Nevşehirli Damad İbrahim Pasha, is considered the greatest representative of the \"localization\" current in divan poetry — in place of abstract conceits, the daily life of Istanbul and its culture of entertainment entered poetry.", "608e220a"], "sanatci.ad": ["Nedîm (original name Ahmed)", "60c38afb"], "sanatci.hayat": ["He was born in Istanbul, probably in 1092 (1681); his father was Kadı Mehmed Efendi, grandson of a kazasker (chief military judge), and his mother belonged to the Karaçelebizâde family. He died in 1143 (1730).", "040eee15"], "sanatci.onem": ["In TDV's description, \"the poet who created a style in divan poetry known by his own name\" — with his plain and witty manner he became the best-known name of the Tulip Era.", "8d247a98"]},
"nedim-olumu":{"baslik": ["The death of Nedîm during the Patrona Halil revolt", "06bf909e"], "metin": ["Nedîm lost his life during the Patrona Halil revolt of 1730; according to the sources, he died either after falling ill from the terror of the revolt or by falling from the roof of his house while trying to escape — the sources diverge on the details of how he died. With his death the brightest voice of the Tulip Era in poetry also fell silent; the era itself ended with the same revolt.", "d255366d"], "sanatci.ad": ["Nedîm (original name Ahmed)", "60c38afb"], "sanatci.hayat": ["He was born in Istanbul, probably in 1092 (1681); his father was Kadı Mehmed Efendi, grandson of a kazasker (chief military judge), and his mother belonged to the Karaçelebizâde family. He died in 1143 (1730).", "040eee15"], "sanatci.onem": ["In TDV's description, \"the poet who created a style in divan poetry known by his own name\" — with his plain and witty manner he became the best-known name of the Tulip Era.", "8d247a98"]},
"nefi-idami":{"baslik": ["Nef'î is executed because of his satires", "5e459c60"], "metin": ["Nef'î, known for his satires, was executed in 1635 on the orders of Murad IV because of his collection of satires, Sihâm-ı Kazâ (Arrows of Fate). Grand Vizier Bayram Pasha handed him over; Nef'î was strangled in the palace woodshed and his body thrown into the sea — the enmities he had earned with his pen brought about his end.", "8999f9d1"], "sanatci.ad": ["Nef'î (original name Ömer)", "6231c83f"], "sanatci.hayat": ["He is estimated to have been born around 980 (1572) in the Pasinler (Hasankale) district of Erzurum; he came from the family of a sanjak governor (sanjakbey). He died in 1044 (1635).", "21fd5d24"], "sanatci.onem": ["In TDV's description, \"a divan poet famous for his satires and odes\" — with his powerful and assertive style he is considered the most remarkable name in Ottoman satirical literature.", "d42cd3f1"]},
"ruslara-niye-direnilemedi-1806-1812":{"baslik": ["Why could the Ottomans not hold out against the Russians in 1806-1812 — was it the army, technology, logistics or internal strife?", "25c658f9"], "ozet": ["In the summer of 1810 Sohum was lost, and in the autumn Rusçuk (Ruse). There is no one-sentence answer to the question \"Was the Russian army larger, or technically more advanced?\": the sources consulted point to at least five separate causes, and these feed one another. The card does not declare any of them to be the sole cause.", "3f5aaac8"], "metin": ["Cause ① — the throne changed hands twice while the war was going on. The war began in November 1806; in May 1807 the Kabakçı Revolt overthrew the sultan, in July 1808 a counter-coup overthrew the second sultan and brought in a third, and in November 1808 the grand vizier was killed. The TDV article `mustafa-iv` records how this was reflected at the front: \"The events in Istanbul spread here too and caused an upheaval; the Janissaries rebelled, the grand vizier was chased away, the army's senior officials fled, the agha of the Janissaries, Pehlivan Agha, who was handling these affairs, was killed by his own comrades, and Çelebi Mustafa Pasha was appointed grand vizier, but order could not be restored. The turmoil within the army led to a great weakness in the face of the Russians.\" Moreover, the time gained was spent not on the enemy but on internal score-settling: the one-year armistice made at Slobozia on 24 August 1807 was \"used not for taking the measures necessary to bring the war to a successful conclusion, but for the internal struggle caused by the change of sultan.\"\n\nCAUSE ② — the only modern army available was not committed to the war, and was then destroyed. Selim III had his New Order (Nizâm-ı Cedîd) army; although there were more than 10,000 trained soldiers in Istanbul in 1807, the sultan did not use them even against the revolt and abolished the corps with his own hand (TDV `kabakci-isyani`). A study examining the organisation of the campaign through the sharia court registers identifies the same gap: \"Considering that this war came on the eve of the New Order system introduced by Selim III, one would expect New Order soldiers to have taken part in it. However, Selim III did not use these soldiers in this war\" (Karakaya 2020, p. 116). As for its successor, the new Sekban corps (Sekbân-ı Cedîd) founded in 1808, it lived for three months: on 16 November its barracks were burned, its aghas were killed and the survivors scattered (TDV `sekban-i-cedid`). The TDV article `yeniceri` states the real structural deadlock in a single sentence: between the new and old armies a \"relentless enmity\" had developed, \"so much so that even in times of need it was never possible to use these two forces together.\"\n\nCAUSE ③ — who raised the soldiers: not the centre but the provinces. The manpower as measured by the same study: \"In the research carried out on the 1806-1812 Ottoman-Russian war, it is seen that the average number of soldiers remained around 60,000\", and at the front \"alongside the soldiers of the standing army (kapıkulu), it is seen that the troops of the ayan in particular were used in the war\" (Karakaya 2020, p. 116). The contribution of one Anatolian district (Safranbolu) is entered in the court registers in the form of demands for soldiers, avârız and imdâd-ı seferiyye taxes (extraordinary and campaign-aid levies), mules and sheep — that is, the army was assembled not through a central system of mobilisation but from soldiers and provisions gathered district by district. This is also the reason for the political weight of the ayan: the state depended on them for taxes, public order and soldiers (TDV `ayan`).\n\nCAUSE ④ — money. The revenue of the New Revenue Treasury (Îrâd-ı Cedîd Hazinesi), set up to finance the reform, had reached 64,500 purses in 1797-98; but because the growth of the new army \"increased the burden on the population of Anatolia and Rumelia, and the people were crushed under heavy taxes\", it fed the opposition, and when the New Order was abolished, \"one of the first things done was the plundering of the revenues transferred to the New Revenue Treasury\" (TDV `nizam-i-cedid`). In other words, in the middle of the war the army's source of finance was broken up.\n\nCAUSE ⑤ — several fronts and diplomacy at the same time. The war was not on a single front: the pilgrimage routes had been closed by the Wahhabi revolt, the British fleet had appeared before Istanbul in February 1807 (TDV `kabakci-isyani`), and in the Caucasus local powers had changed sides — in Abkhazia, Sefer Bey, the son of Keleş Bey and a Russian subject, wrote letters to the tsar in 1808 and 1809 stating that he wished to submit to him, and on 17 February 1810 the Russians proclaimed that Abkhazia had been joined to Russia (TDV `sohum`).\n\nAND the outcome can be read in two cities. Sohum Fortress \"fell into Russian hands on 11 July 1810 because sufficient military support could not be provided at that time\" (TDV `sohum`). Rusçuk, by contrast, \"withstood a long and bloody siege carried out by the Russian armies under the command of Count Kaminski; on 26 September 1810 it surrendered on condition that the garrison and the civilians could leave the city in safety\" (TDV `ruscuk`). Read together, the two do not yield the picture that \"Russian soldiers were better\": Rusçuk held out for months, and the following year the Russians were forced to withdraw \"after the heavy defeats and setbacks suffered in June 1811\". The issue, then, is not technical superiority in a single battle but the capacity to sustain a war: an uninterrupted supply of soldiers, money, command and political stability.\n\nWhat THIS card does not say. No quantified comparison of the Russian army with the Ottoman army of the period in terms of muskets, artillery, training and the education of officers was found in the sources consulted. The statement \"their weapons were better\" is too strong a claim to be written without being measured; the five causes above rest on measurable records, while the sixth (technical superiority) could not be measured.", "04d3cab8"], "bag": ["View: The cause was military — the old army was untrained and mutinous, and the new army was destroyed almost as soon as it was established. Counter-view: The cause was political — a state that went through two depositions, one killing of a ruler and an assassination of a grand vizier within six years could not possibly have maintained continuity at the front; the army was a result of this instability, not its cause. Third reading: both are secondary, and the real issue was financial and logistical capacity — soldiers were being gathered district by district, money was dissipated through the plundering of the reform treasury, and distant fortresses (Sohum) were falling because they could not get support. The sources present these three readings not as substitutes for one another but as intertwined.", "0b65dbbc"]},
"savas-93-harbi-1877-1878":{"baslik": ["The War of '93 — the Russo-Ottoman War of 1877-1878", "8a0cf459"], "tarih_metin": ["Declaration of war 24 April 1877 · Fall of Plevna 10 December 1877 · Treaty of San Stefano 3 March 1878", "8df6f08b"], "yer": ["Balkan (Danube) front — Plevna, Shipka Pass; Caucasus front — Kars, Erzurum", "59d469ce"], "oncesi": ["After the Treaty of Paris (1856), Russia, pursuing a Pan-Slavist foreign policy, encouraged the Balkan peoples to revolt against the Ottomans; the allegation that tens of thousands of Bulgarians had been killed in the suppression of the Bulgarian Uprising that broke out in May 1876 gave the war a religious/humanitarian legitimacy in the eyes of European public opinion. In addition, the growing burden of the Ottoman foreign debt had also turned European public opinion against the state. Russia declared war on 24 April 1877.", "bbea8a50"], "akis": ["On the Danube front, the Ottoman forces under the command of Gazi Osman Pasha established a fortified position at Plevna and resisted for months; in September 1877 the repeated joint assaults of the Russian and Romanian forces came to nothing. But at the end of the long siege provisions and ammunition ran out, and Osman Pasha was forced to surrender on 10 December 1877. This resistance delayed the course of the war but could not change its outcome; the Russian army crossed the Balkans and advanced as far as Edirne, and on the Caucasus front as far as Erzurum.", "7c436b2a"], "sonuc": ["The guns fell silent with the Armistice of Edirne of 31 January 1878, and on 3 March 1878 the Treaty of San Stefano (Ayastefanos) was signed: a \"Principality of Greater Bulgaria\" with wide borders was established, the independence of Serbia, Montenegro and Romania was officially recognised for the first time, a large territory in eastern Anatolia including Erzurum was ceded to Russia, and the Ottomans agreed to pay Russia a war indemnity of 802,500,000 francs. The human cost of the war was heavy: hundreds of thousands of Muslim refugees (muhacir) fleeing from Bulgaria and the Caucasus sought shelter in Istanbul and Anatolia. Britain, disturbed by such a great Russian expansion, exerted diplomatic pressure to soften the harsh provisions — in return it took over the administration of Cyprus; some of the harsh provisions were revised a few months later at the Congress of Berlin (1878).", "742b09c1"], "tartisma": ["Only the original provisions of San Stefano have been described in this card; the extent to which the Treaty of Berlin changed San Stefano (such as the reduction of Greater Bulgaria) has not been separately verified, as the body of the TDV text that was read does not give this detail.", "56bc0f69"], "taraflar.0.ad": ["Ottoman State", "86202f73"], "taraflar.0.komutan": ["At Plevna, Gazi Osman Pasha", "a89259b4"], "taraflar.0.kuvvet": ["no figure given in the source", "5fd4623f"], "taraflar.1.ad": ["Russia, Romania, Serbia, Montenegro", "b57f43c4"], "taraflar.1.komutan": ["no figure given in the source", "5fd4623f"], "taraflar.1.kuvvet": ["no figure given in the source", "5fd4623f"]},
"savas-bursa-1326":{"baslik": ["The Surrender of Bursa (6 April 1326)", "2789bfbd"], "tarih_metin": ["2 Jumada al-Awwal 726 · 6 April 1326", "814cf92f"], "yer": ["Bursa (Prusa), at the northern foot of Mount Uludağ", "9f2737c8"], "oncesi": ["When the alliance of the tekfurs (Byzantine lords) of the Bursa plain was defeated at the Dimbos (Dinboz) pass, the city was left on its own. Instead of attacking the walls, Osman Gazi chose to cut the city off from its surroundings: he had two blockade towers named Aktimur and Balabancık built to keep watch on Bursa, gave one of them to his nephew Aktimur and withdrew his forces. The Turkmen and Kızık villages on the slopes of Uludağ are thought to have been founded during these long years of blockade. Unable to obtain help from outside, the townspeople were gradually driven into hunger and poverty.", "35db8ed6"], "akis": ["The decisive outcome was brought not by a pitched battle but by negotiations for surrender. Orhan, who had taken over the headship of the beylik in the spring of 1326, came before the walls with all his forces and called on the city to surrender. According to the TDV article on Orhan, the agreement (ahidname) reached with the tekfur had three conditions: the soldiers entering the city would not plunder or take captives; those who wished to leave could take their property and depart under the protection of Ottoman soldiers; and 30,000 gold coins would be paid to Orhan. As Aşıkpaşazade relates it, when the tekfur left the city, Ahi Hasan was the first to climb onto the tower of the walls, and then the Muslims settled in the city.", "53986afa"], "sonuc": ["The Byzantine commander was allowed to go to Istanbul; his chief adviser, who had brought about the surrender, stayed in the city and entered Ottoman service, and the metropolitan of Bursa also continued in his office. The Greek population was moved to the quarters below the citadel, and only Turks were settled inside the citadel. Orhan converted the monastery within the citadel into a mosque, had the Bey's Palace (Bey Sarayı) built on the Tophane ridge and in 1327 had the first silver akçe of the beylik struck here. The complex consisting of a mosque, soup kitchen, madrasa, bathhouse and inn that he founded east of the citadel in 1339-1340 became the nucleus of the city's new market centre. Bursa was made the centre of the beylik, and the tombs of the early rulers from Osman Gazi to the Conqueror also rose here.", "b333ed51"], "tartisma": ["The beginning of the blockade is given differently in two TDV articles: the article on Osman I places the battle of Dimbos in 1303 and says that the city remained under siege for twenty-three years; the article on Bursa, however, places the tekfur's defeat at the Dinboz pass and the beginning of the siege in 1308 and speaks of a blockade lasting \"more than ten years\". The day of the surrender (6 April 1326) is the same in both articles.", "4617aae7"], "taraflar.0.ad": ["Ottoman Beylik", "d8cb2121"], "taraflar.0.komutan": ["Orhan Bey", "6726cdef"], "taraflar.0.kuvvet": ["no figure given in the source", "5fd4623f"], "taraflar.1.ad": ["The fortress of Bursa, subject to Byzantium", "624fdda3"], "taraflar.1.komutan": ["The tekfur of Bursa (his name does not appear in the sources)", "5b66491e"], "taraflar.1.kuvvet": ["no figure given in the source", "5fd4623f"]},
"savas-canakkale-bozgunu-1656":{"baslik": ["The Dardanelles Rout (26 June / 13 July 1656)", "818d581e"], "tarih_metin": ["26 June 1656 (the day the naval battle began) — the losses/withdrawal continued until 13 July 1656 (the atlas's date of change)", "832807db"], "yer": ["The Dardanelles, the narrow passage between Kilitbahir and Kal'a-i Sultaniyye", "08080fdf"], "oncesi": ["In the tenth year of the Cretan War (1645-), Venice was continuing its strategy of regularly blockading the Dardanelles in order to cut off Ottoman supplies to the island. In 1654 Kapudan Pasha (Grand Admiral) Kara Murad Pasha had defeated the Venetian fleet in the narrow passage between Kilitbahir and Kal'a-i Sultaniyye and temporarily lifted the blockade — but this victory did not last; Venice gathered its strength and massed once more before the strait.", "b08492aa"], "akis": ["On 26 June 1656 the two fleets met in the strait. The TDV article `canakkale-bogazi` does not give the details of the engagement (tactics, duration); it records only the result: the Ottoman fleet of eighty-six vessels was routed by the Venetians. In the period that lasted until 13 July, the card's own date of change, the fleet dispersed, Istanbul's sea link to the Mediterranean was in effect cut, and Venice occupied Tenedos (Bozcaada) and Lemnos.", "9b1a0869"], "sonuc": ["When the Strait came under Venetian blockade, the flow of provisions (especially grain) reaching Istanbul by sea was cut off; prices rose in the city and unrest grew among the people. In this atmosphere of military and administrative crisis, on 26 September 1656 Turhan Sultan, mother of Mehmed IV, brought Köprülü Mehmed Pasha, an elderly and stern administrator, to the grand vizierate — according to the TDV article `koprulu-mehmed-pasa`, this appointment was an attempt to halt the systematic weakening of the Ottomans against Venice. As soon as Köprülü took office, he also launched a counter-operation at sea: in 1657 a new naval battle of the Dardanelles lasting three days ended in an Ottoman victory (after the enemy flagship blew up when hit by a cannonball fired from Kumburnu), the blockade was lifted, and in the same year Tenedos and Lemnos were retaken.", "7f08b982"], "tartisma": ["The TDV article that was read (`canakkale-bogazi`) does not give the tactical detail of the battle (how the rout came about); it reports only the result. For this reason the card does not answer the question \"how was it lost\"; it relates only \"what happened and what followed\".", "88e6e97c"], "taraflar.0.ad": ["Ottoman State", "86202f73"], "taraflar.0.komutan": ["Kapudan Pasha (Grand Admiral) Sarı Kenan Pasha", "8dd1e8c4"], "taraflar.0.kuvvet": ["eighty-six ships: fifty galleys, nine galleasses (mavna), twenty-seven galleons", "a51e2ab6"], "taraflar.1.ad": ["Republic of Venice", "f30b04e7"], "taraflar.1.komutan": ["not named in the TDV article that was read", "dfd87a77"], "taraflar.1.kuvvet": ["no figure given in the source", "5fd4623f"]},
"savas-kirim-savasi-1853-1856":{"baslik": ["The Crimean War (1853-1856)", "9a0abab0"], "tarih_metin": ["Declaration of war 4 October 1853 · Raid on Sinop 30 November 1853 · Treaty of Paris 30 March 1856", "d0edee21"], "yer": ["Danube front, Caucasus front, the Crimean peninsula (siege of Sevastopol), the Black Sea", "c80acecb"], "oncesi": ["Russia, using as a pretext the question of control over the Christian holy places in Jerusalem (against Catholic France), claimed a right of protection over the Orthodox subjects in Ottoman territory, and when this demand was rejected it declared war on 4 October 1853.", "ae3755e2"], "akis": ["The first great blow of the war came at sea: on 30 November 1853 the Russian fleet bombarded and burned the Ottoman squadron in the harbour of Sinop; more than 2,000 soldiers were killed (as martyrs), twelve ships were sunk and the civilian quarters of the city were also damaged (see the card on naval misfortunes). This raid caused a great stir in European public opinion and led Britain and France to enter the war on the Ottoman side. The main theatre of the war shifted to the Crimean peninsula, to the port of Sevastopol besieged by the allies; the siege ended with the fall of the city on 8 September 1855, and this accelerated the peace process.", "b535b93e"], "sonuc": ["The war ended with the Treaty of Paris of 30 March 1856. The treaty abolished Russia's right of protection over the Principalities (Wallachia and Moldavia), established an international regime of navigation on the Danube, declared the Black Sea neutral and demilitarised, and officially deemed the Ottoman State \"an equal member of the European state system\" — thereby also officially ending the Russian superiority established by Küçük Kaynarca in 1774. But the victory did not come without a price: the financial burden of the war forced the Ottomans to sign the first foreign loan contract in their history on 24 August 1854 — this borrowing was the first link in the chain that half a century later would lead to the Ottoman Public Debt Administration (Düyun-ı Umumiyye) (1881) (see the card on the Public Debt Administration).", "36044b90"], "tartisma": ["The TDV source that was read (the article on the Treaty of Paris) gives no total figure for the military and civilian losses of the war; the losses at Sinop are documented with concrete numbers, but no total casualty figure has been given in this card for the Sevastopol front as a whole (none has been invented).", "3a5fba92"], "taraflar.0.ad": ["Ottoman State, Britain, France (from 1854), Piedmont-Sardinia", "b62d6c53"], "taraflar.0.komutan": ["no figure given in the source", "5fd4623f"], "taraflar.0.kuvvet": ["no figure given in the source", "5fd4623f"], "taraflar.1.ad": ["Russia", "4af42a63"], "taraflar.1.komutan": ["no figure given in the source", "5fd4623f"], "taraflar.1.kuvvet": ["no figure given in the source", "5fd4623f"]},
"savas-otlukbeli-1473":{"baslik": ["The Battle of Otlukbeli (11 August 1473)", "309a3556"], "tarih_metin": ["16 Rabi al-Awwal 878 · 11 August 1473 (Wednesday)", "cdda1351"], "yer": ["The hills of Otlukbeli (Başkent), at the locality of Üçağızlı near Tercan", "726b54c1"], "oncesi": ["Having taken Trebizond and established his dominance over the Karamanids, the Conqueror had as his most powerful rival in the east the Aq Qoyunlu state, centred on Diyarbekir and Tabriz, which held most of Iran and Eastern Anatolia. Uzun Hasan sought an alliance with Venice, which was at war with the Ottomans, and also with Hungary, the Knights of Rhodes and Cyprus; he persistently asked Venice for cannon and muskets, the greatest deficiency of his army, but because of Ottoman precautions the weapons sent never reached him. In 1472 an Aq Qoyunlu army plundered and burned Tokat; the TDV interprets this as a response to the new customs duty that the Conqueror had imposed at Tokat on the silk road. As the price of peace Uzun Hasan demanded that Cappadocia and Trebizond be left to him; in a harsh letter the Conqueror challenged him to war in the spring. After seeking peace with Venice and Hungary in the west and coming to terms with the Mamluks, he set out from Üsküdar in March 1473 and entrusted Istanbul to his young son Cem.", "1c9b6598"], "akis": ["Although the army marched in battle order for forty days, it did not encounter Uzun Hasan; when communications were broken, the rumour reached Istanbul that the Aq Qoyunlu had won, and Prince Cem set about establishing his own authority. On 4 August, on the bank of the Euphrates, Uğurlu Mehmed drew Has Murad Pasha into a narrow pass with a feigned retreat; the pasha, who did not heed Mahmud Pasha's warning, was drowned while trying to cross the river, about 4000 soldiers died and many notables were taken prisoner. When, seven days later, the demoralised army encamped at Üçağızlı, it was forced to fight on unfavourable ground against an enemy that had already occupied the hills of Otlukbeli. Davud Pasha's fierce attack prevented Gâvur İshak from coming down and cutting the roads and enabled the Ottoman units to gain a foothold on the hill. Prince Mustafa came out onto level ground and bore down on Zeynel Mirza's wing; when Zeynel was killed by the azebs, the Aq Qoyunlu right wing collapsed. On the other wing Uğurlu Mehmed held a stream valley and stopped Prince Bayezid. Seeing that the break-up had begun, the Conqueror threw still more of the Janissaries into the battle. Realising that resistance was impossible, Uzun Hasan left in his place Alpagot Pir Mehmed Bey, who closely resembled him, and fled; the Ottomans took the latter prisoner thinking he was Uzun Hasan, and only later understood that they were mistaken.", "f5d75c6c"], "sonuc": ["According to Angiolello, in the battle, which lasted eight hours, the Aq Qoyunlu lost 10,000 men and the Ottomans only 1000; the TDV stresses that superiority in cannon and muskets played a large part in the victory. Among the prisoners were mirzas of Timur's line and Uzun Hasan's nişancı (chancellor) and imam; most of the captured Turkmen soldiers were killed, but the fleeing army was not pursued. The Conqueror took Bayburt and on the way back also seized the fortress of Şarkîkarahisar. The victory gave a great boost to the morale of the Ottomans, who had feared a danger from the east ever since the defeat inflicted by Timur, and showed that the classic Turkmen armies could no longer cope with regular units bearing firearms. The Aq Qoyunlu never recovered; the vacuum they left was filled by the Safavids, who would become a far more serious rival to the Ottomans.", "069f8e19"], "tartisma": ["Has Murad Pasha's failure to obey orders is attributed in the sources to two different causes: according to some, those in his retinue goaded him not to leave the glory of victory to Mahmud Pasha; according to others, he fell victim to his own inexperience. The strength of the Ottoman army is given within a wide range, between 70,000 and 100,000; the casualty figures in the TDV, for their part, rest on a single source (Angiolello).", "9d42976e"], "taraflar.0.ad": ["Ottoman army", "e562e57a"], "taraflar.0.komutan": ["Sultan Mehmed the Conqueror (centre) · Prince Bayezid on the right wing · Prince Mustafa and Koca Davud Pasha, beylerbey of Anatolia, on the left wing · Grand Vizier Mahmud Pasha", "97a84f6a"], "taraflar.0.kuvvet": ["between 70,000 and 100,000", "f06466e3"], "taraflar.1.ad": ["Aq Qoyunlu army", "968b6c58"], "taraflar.1.komutan": ["Uzun Hasan (centre) · his son Zeynel Mirza on the right wing · his son Uğurlu Mehmed on the left wing · Gâvur İshak in the vanguard", "2f0384fd"], "taraflar.1.kuvvet": ["about 70,000 cavalry (40,000 of them armoured and armed with lances)", "501151e7"]},
"savas-rodos-1522":{"baslik": ["Siege of Rhodes (28 July – 20 December 1522)", "dba5cb3f"], "tarih_metin": ["The fleet put to sea on 4 June 1522 · the sultan crossed to the island on 28 July · surrender on 1 Safar 929 / 20 December 1522", "d270e65f"], "yer": ["The city and island of Rhodes", "e0a71429"], "oncesi": ["The Knights had settled on Rhodes in 1309, turned the city, with its walls enclosing 81 hectares, into the strongest fortress of medieval Europe, and from here carried out corsair raids on Turkish ships and coasts. In 1480 Mesih Pasha's fleet of 160 ships besieged the island for three months; when the last great assault on 28 July failed, he withdrew, after which the walls were renewed from end to end. After Sultan Selim the Grim took Syria and Egypt in 1516-1517, the island became a Christian outpost in the middle of the busy sea route between Istanbul and Cairo. Having taken Belgrade, Suleiman the Lawgiver chose as his second target Rhodes, which again his great forebear had failed to capture; the shipyard Selim had built and the great fleet he had had prepared in his last years left hardly any need for further preparation for this campaign.", "d87e3bf9"], "akis": ["The fleet sailed on 4 June; on 18 June the sultan set out overland from Üsküdar at the head of his army, reached Marmaris on 26 July by way of İznik, Kütahya, Denizli, Çine and Muğla, and crossed to the island on 28 July. Throughout the siege he watched the fighting, often rode around the island and held frequent councils with Pîrî Mehmed Pasha. Life in the camp was not confined to war: twenty-five qadis found to have committed abuses in Anatolia were dismissed, and the news from Hürrem Sultan of the birth of his son Mehmed also arrived at this time. The sources describe the fighting as \"hard and bloody\"; the details of the assaults are not known. The Knights surrendered on 20 December.", "e81a5723"], "sonuc": ["L'Isle-Adam, who was received at the divan that was convened, was given leave to quit the island; according to one account, the sultan consoled the aged knight and even proposed inviting him to Islam and giving him a suitable office. Under the terms of the agreement the Knights boarded their ships and departed; the Grand Master left the island on 1 January 1523. In those same days Cem Sultan's son, who had been living on Rhodes, was found and put to death, and Cem's daughters and wife were sent to Istanbul. The sultan entered the city on 29 December and on 2 January and had the khutbah read in his name. The ruined walls were repaired and a strong garrison was installed; in the upper part of the city a mosque, a madrasa, a soup kitchen and a bathhouse were built, and the tax revenues of the small surrounding islands were assigned to this endowment. The Knights' Gothic cathedral of Saint John was converted into a mosque in 1523. The island remained Ottoman until the Italian occupation of 1912.", "7f5fe217"], "tartisma": ["The campaign calendar differs between two TDV articles: the article on Suleiman I says that the sultan left Üsküdar on 23 Rajab (18 June), while the article on Rhodes says that he set out on campaign on 21 Rajab 928 (16 June). The Rhodes article gives the garrison of 1524 as 1,343 men in one place and 1,378 in another. The date of the surrender is 20 December 1522 in both TDV articles.", "e3dffcdb"], "taraflar.0.ad": ["Ottoman army and fleet", "a9f3c5bb"], "taraflar.0.komutan": ["Sultan Suleiman the Lawgiver · Grand Vizier Pîrî Mehmed Pasha", "fe9d7206"], "taraflar.0.kuvvet": ["no figure given in the source", "5fd4623f"], "taraflar.1.ad": ["Knights of Saint John (Hospitallers)", "6a12a491"], "taraflar.1.komutan": ["Grand Master Philippe Villiers de l'Isle-Adam", "facb37aa"], "taraflar.1.kuvvet": ["no figure given in the source", "5fd4623f"]},
"savas-trabzon-1461":{"baslik": ["The Surrender of Trebizond (1461)", "85472e39"], "tarih_metin": ["Summer 1461 · TDV: \"August or September 1461\"", "f2b10e65"], "yer": ["Trebizond, on the eastern Black Sea coast", "9f1237f1"], "oncesi": ["The TDV attributes the long life of this small empire, which had stood since 1204, to several factors: the eastern Black Sea mountains that separate the coast from the interior and allow no passage, strong walls, lively trade with the Venetian and Genoese colonies, and a marriage diplomacy that was prepared to accept vassalage to powerful neighbours (the Seljuks, the Mongols, Timur). After the conquest of Istanbul, Emperor Kalo Ioannes began to pay tribute to the sultan. In 1456 Sheikh Junayd, who had won the Turkmens of Canik to his side, attempted to take the city and establish a state of his own; he came right up to the walls but did not succeed. According to the article on Mehmed II, Trebizond was regarded as being under the protection of Uzun Hasan of the Aq Qoyunlu; the campaign was therefore also seen as a move against Uzun Hasan. Before setting out on the campaign, the Conqueror signed a truce with the Knights of Rhodes.", "5d8e1fda"], "akis": ["First Hızır Bey carried out a reconnaissance operation with the fleet. Then the sultan, at the head of the army, reached the city overland across difficult mountain roads and began the siege; the fleet of Kasım Bey, sanjak governor of Gallipoli, gave support from the sea. According to the sources the siege lasted four or six weeks, and the city surrendered under an agreement. The Conqueror stayed a few days in Trebizond and left the administration of the city to Kasım Bey.", "704cd050"], "sonuc": ["Under the agreement David Komnenos and his whole family were put on ships with their movable goods and sent to Istanbul; palace officials and wealthy families followed them. The emperor was given extensive lands in the district of Serres, and most of the nobles were assigned timars. Of some 1500 young people selected from the city and its surroundings, 800 were sent to Istanbul to be trained as Janissaries. The property of those who remained in the city was recognised; a unit of 400 Janissaries and azebs (light infantry) was stationed there, and Turks were installed in the vacated houses. According to the survey register of 1486, Muslims who had come from Anatolian towns, either by deportation or of their own accord, made up about a fifth of the population. The Chrysokephalos cathedral, where the emperors had been crowned, was turned into the Câmi-i Atîk (Old Mosque) in commemoration of the conquest. For Uzun Hasan the matter was not closed: as a condition of peace he demanded that Trebizond be given to him, and in 1472 he sent a nephew of the former emperor against the city — this was one of the knots of the conflict that would end two years later at Otlukbeli.", "30e71434"], "tartisma": ["The TDV article on Trebizond gives the surrender with two possibilities, as \"August or September 1461\"; the duration of the siege too varies between four and six weeks according to the sources. As for the fall of Amasra, the TDV article on Mehmed II places it in the summer of 1459, immediately after the Smederevo campaign.", "2905820c"], "taraflar.0.ad": ["Ottoman army and fleet", "a9f3c5bb"], "taraflar.0.komutan": ["Sultan Mehmed the Conqueror · Hızır Bey with the reconnaissance fleet · Kasım Bey, sanjak governor of Gallipoli", "2897fe54"], "taraflar.0.kuvvet": ["no figure given in the source", "5fd4623f"], "taraflar.1.ad": ["Empire of Trebizond (Komnenos dynasty)", "8f519803"], "taraflar.1.komutan": ["Emperor David Komnenos", "5af9c587"], "taraflar.1.kuvvet": ["no figure given in the source", "5fd4623f"]},
"savas-viyana-karlofca-1683-1699":{"baslik": ["The Second Siege of Vienna and the Great Turkish War (1683-1699)", "22a62e94"], "tarih_metin": ["Siege 14 July - 12 September 1683 · the war as a whole 1683-1699 · Treaty of Karlowitz 26 January 1699", "f85e5d28"], "yer": ["Vienna (siege) — the war in general on the fronts of Hungary, the Morea, Podolia and Dalmatia", "429c2485"], "oncesi": ["When Protestant Hungarian leaders asked for Ottoman protection against Austria, Kara Mustafa Pasha, in contrast to the inclination of the previous grand vizier Fazıl Ahmed Pasha to maintain the 1664 Treaty of Vasvár, decided on a direct campaign against Vienna. The army set out in the spring of 1683 and arrived before Vienna on 14 July; according to the TDV article `merzifonlu-kara-mustafa-pasa`, the fact that the great siege guns had not been taken on the campaign was a deficiency from the outset.", "1271666c"], "akis": ["The siege, which began after the city rejected the offer to surrender, lasted two months, and the shortage of food came to the surface. Kara Mustafa Pasha's personal enmity with the Crimean khan Murad Giray was a mistake that led the khan to remain neutral throughout the campaign. The Polish king Sobieski arrived with an army of 100,000 men and 200 guns, held the hill of Kahlenberg (\"the German mountain\") and got behind the Ottoman army. In the battle that began on 12 September, İbrahim Pasha's unit collapsed first, and the expected support from Murad Giray did not come; when Sobieski attacked the centre, Kara Mustafa Pasha was forced to withdraw after resisting for five or six hours.", "49e1a64c"], "sonuc": ["After the rout, Kara Mustafa Pasha's enemies at the palace (Yusuf Agha, Sarı Süleyman Agha) maligned him to the sultan and had him executed on 25 December 1683. The defeat led to the formation of the Holy League, which brought together Austria, Poland, Venice and the Papacy, and the war continued for sixteen years on four fronts (Hungary, the Morea, Dalmatia, Podolia) — in Ottoman history this period is also referred to as the \"Great Turkish War\". The war ended on 26 January 1699 with the Treaty of Karlowitz: the Ottomans ceded the greater part of Hungary (including Transylvania) and the Morea to Austria and Venice, and Podolia to Poland. Karlowitz was the first treaty of great territorial loss that the Ottomans signed in Europe, and within the framework given by TDV it is regarded as a turning point at which Austria was able for the first time to force its rivals into such a comprehensive peace.", "c2980fed"], "tartisma": ["The tactical details of the siege (at what point İbrahim Pasha collapsed, the exact nature of the enmity with Murad Giray) are given only in summary in the TDV article that was read; the full sixteen-year, four-front course of the war has not been detailed in this card, and only the beginning, the rout and the outcome have been narrated.", "1be643a3"], "taraflar.0.ad": ["Ottoman State", "86202f73"], "taraflar.0.komutan": ["Grand Vizier Merzifonlu Kara Mustafa Pasha", "205cd1b1"], "taraflar.0.kuvvet": ["no total figure given in the source", "f6e6426c"], "taraflar.1.ad": ["Holy Roman Empire (Austria) + Poland-Lithuania + (from 1684) the Holy League: Venice, the Papacy, later Russia", "75cec463"], "taraflar.1.komutan": ["At Vienna, the Polish king John III Sobieski (relief army of 100,000 men, 200 guns)", "bab8f153"], "taraflar.1.kuvvet": ["no total figure given in the source", "f6e6426c"]},
"savas2-atina-1687-parthenon-patlamasi":{"baslik": ["The loss of Athens and the explosion of the Parthenon (21-26 September 1687)", "128acdac"], "tarih_metin": ["The siege began on 21 September 1687 · explosion of the Parthenon 26 September 1687 · Venetian withdrawal and Ottoman return April 1688", "954a578a"], "yer": ["Athens (the Acropolis) and the Parthenon (at that time the mosque of Athens)", "a54f0de0"], "oncesi": ["As part of the Morea campaign (one front of the War of the Holy League), the Venetian fleet under Morosini's command, after seizing the whole Morea peninsula between 1685 and 1687, turned north towards Athens. In the Ottoman period the Parthenon had been used as a mosque for centuries, and the garrison had stored gunpowder and ammunition inside it, probably because it was the most solid structure in the fortress — this decision became the direct cause of the coming disaster.", "12a936d4"], "akis": ["In the siege that began on 21 September 1687, Venetian artillery pounded the Acropolis. On 26 September a cannonball fired under Königsmarck struck the Parthenon, which had been reported to have ammunition stored inside. The explosion completely destroyed the middle part of the building: fourteen of the forty-six columns surrounding it were toppled, the columns of the interior were shattered and the roof was entirely blown open — a building that had stood almost undamaged since AD 438 (as a church and then as a mosque) was turned into a ruin in a few seconds.", "624bd7f2"], "sonuc": ["The city surrendered shortly afterwards; 560 soldiers and 3000 Turks left Athens, while Venice forcibly deported 6000 Christian inhabitants to the Morea. But the Venetian presence in Athens did not last long: because of epidemic disease and the difficulty of defending the city, the Venetian troops withdrew by their own decision in April 1688, and the Ottomans returned at once — that is, the city remained in Venetian hands for only eight months. The Parthenon was never restored to its former state: the returning Ottoman administration contented itself with building a small mosque within the ruins, and no restoration of the great structure was undertaken.", "a4c8808e"], "tartisma": ["Some online/secondary sources write that the Ottomans did not retake Athens until 'twenty years later' (about 1707); the TDV article `atina` that was read did not give this detail, whereas the site's own chronology entry (1688-04-01, 'The recovery of Athens — the Venetian withdrawal') gives April 1688, and this is closer to the standard account of the Morean War, consistent with Venice's general military and health difficulties in the Morea (epidemic, supply). This card has taken the date 1688 as its basis; the 'twenty years later' claim could not be verified from TDV, and its source is unclear.", "70931988"], "taraflar.0.ad": ["Republic of Venice", "f30b04e7"], "taraflar.0.komutan": ["Francesco Morosini · artillery commander, the Swedish Count Otto Wilhelm Königsmarck", "0b3e960a"], "taraflar.0.kuvvet": ["no figure given in the source", "5fd4623f"], "taraflar.1.ad": ["Ottoman State (Athens garrison)", "b5115c94"], "taraflar.1.komutan": ["not named in the TDV article that was read", "dfd87a77"], "taraflar.1.kuvvet": ["560 soldiers (based on the number who left the city)", "f3c5d431"]},
"sebep-sonuc-1595-on-dokuz-sehzade":{"oneri": ["The nineteen deaths of 1595 were not a decision of the moment: a century-old order of succession, a crowded…", "fad6b7d7"], "sebep.b": ["Murad III leaving behind a large number of sons within an order of succession in which the throne was bound by no rule, every prince was considered a candidate and the law code permitted fratricide", "bbdcf91b"], "sonuc.b": ["The strangling of nineteen brothers on the night of Mehmed III's accession — and the reaction aroused by the funerals preparing the ground for the end of the practice", "1c3bd383"], "bag": ["Not all the links are sourced: the number of children, the encouragement within the harem and the reaction of 1595 are recorded in TDV; linking these to one another as ‘causes’ is this card's own interpretation.", "d34951ed"], "metin": ["① The absence of a rule. TDV's article 'Ahmed I' writes that until Ahmed I accession was not bound by any rule, and its article 'Şehzade' that the succession of the eldest son to the throne was an unofficial tradition; the princes were sent out to sanjaks to gain experience in government. The Conqueror's law code laid down that whoever came to the throne could kill his brothers for the sake of the ‘order of the world’ (nizâm-ı âlem). ② The absence of a rule fed war. TDV's article on Bayezid II writes that this provision gave the struggle among the princes the character of a fight for survival; the article on Prince Bayezid also states that fear that the same provision would be applied played a part in the rebellion of 1559-62. The wars between brothers during the Interregnum, Cem and Bayezid II, Selim the Grim and his brothers, Prince Mustafa in 1553 and Prince Bayezid in 1562 are the links of this chain (details are in the lists ‘Killings and executions within the dynasty’). ③ A crowded harem. Selim II was the first Ottoman ruler who went on no campaign at all (TDV). For the reign of his son Murad III, TDV records that, encouraged by his mother Nurbânû Sultan and his sister İsmihan Sultan, the sultan turned to concubines. TDV regards the large figures given for the number of his children as ‘exaggerated reports’; what can be gathered from the sources is that forty-nine of his children were alive at his death. TDV's article 'Mehmed III' also speaks of circles that reacted against his father's shutting himself up in the palace and becoming ineffective in government. ④ The night of 1595. At the accession of Mehmed III nineteen princes, four of them grown and the rest very young, were strangled; the mothers of the dead, the daughters of Murad III and women of the palace were sent to the Old Palace. In 1574 Murad III too had had five brothers killed in the same way, but the number in 1595 was almost four times as large. ⑤ The result. According to TDV, the nineteen coffins that left the palace the next day aroused great reaction in every section of society, and this reaction probably opened the door to the end of the practice. In 1603 Mehmed III also had his elder son Mahmud killed on suspicion of coveting his throne. At his death his son Ahmed was fourteen and did not yet have a son; for this reason his brother Mustafa, the only other surviving male member of the dynasty, was not touched (TDV 'Mustafa I').", "43a7d313"]},
"sebep-sonuc-amasya-1555":{"oneri": ["The Shah never gave battle", "670cbc9a"], "sebep.b": ["During the Nakhchivan Campaign, Shah Tahmasp's avoidance of a pitched battle and devastation of the army's route, Suleiman the Lawgiver's threat to Ardabil, held sacred by the Safavids, by wintering in Amasya, and the shah's request for a truce", "3fa28be9"], "sonuc.b": ["Treaty of Amasya: the first treaty between the two states, in which the Safavids accepted Ottoman rule in Iraq and Eastern Anatolia", "dad38f4f"], "bag": ["Significance: The TDV describes this peace as the first treaty concluded between the Ottoman State and Iran. Although it had the appearance of a friendly exchange of letters, it made the other side accept Ottoman rule along the line stretching from Baghdad to Kars and kept the eastern frontier calm for a quarter of a century.", "338561c7"], "metin": ["The raids that the Safavids made into Eastern Anatolia after the Tabriz Campaign sent Suleiman the Lawgiver on campaign once again. Knowing that he could not withstand the great army, Tahmasp did not come out to face it; he burned the places the army was to pass through, had the water poisoned and moved the population away. Because of supply shortages and the approaching winter, the Ottoman army withdrew from Nakhchivan. While Suleiman the Lawgiver was in Erzurum, the shah's request for a truce was accepted (26 September 1554). The sultan's wintering in Amasya and his threat to destroy Ardabil alarmed Tahmasp: his envoy Ferruhzad Bey arrived in Amasya on 17 May 1555, was received in the divan on 22 May and presented the letter seeking peace.", "ac004014"]},
"sebep-sonuc-belgrad-1739":{"oneri": ["The 1739 Treaty of Belgrade — the recovery of lost Belgrade", "62d1d3de"], "sebep.b": ["Austria's defeat in the war of 1736–1739, which Russia and its ally Austria waged against the Ottomans on two fronts, and the operation carried out to retake Belgrade", "9da8e650"], "sonuc.b": ["Treaty of Belgrade: Belgrade and Northern Serbia were recovered; Azov was to be demolished and become an area from which both sides would stay away", "3929618a"], "bag": ["Significance: According to the TDV, Austria's eastward expansion came to a halt and its frontier in the Balkans changed little over the following 140 years or so; Russia, for its part, was kept away from the Black Sea until the Treaty of Küçük Kaynarca of 1774.", "951e406e"], "metin": ["The peace opened a long period of calm between the two empires. When Austria was hard pressed in the reign of Maria Theresa by the wars of succession and the wars with Prussia, the Ottomans chose not to take advantage of this weakness; they held aloof from the insistent offers of alliance made by King Frederick II of Prussia. Russia, having waged the war far more effectively than its ally, began to emerge as a serious rival to the Habsburgs in the Balkans. The price of the return to Belgrade was also heavy: according to the survey of 1741, 721 of the 1,546 villages in Northern Serbia had been abandoned. Belgrade Fortress was rebuilt and made the largest fortress in the country.", "76b0567f"]},
"sebep-sonuc-berlin-1878":{"oneri": ["Who undid San Stefano, and why did the Ottomans again leave the table with losses?", "ac172c9e"], "sebep.b": ["The objections of Britain, Austria and the Balkan states to the Greater Bulgaria that Russia had created at San Stefano", "d520e887"], "sonuc.b": ["Treaty of Berlin: the division of Bulgaria into three, the independence of Serbia, Montenegro and Romania, Bosnia-Herzegovina handed over to Austrian occupation, and Kars, Ardahan and Batum ceded to Russia", "c00b2d63"], "bag": ["Significance: San Stefano was a treaty ending a war that concerned only two states; the Congress of Berlin made the question the common concern of Europe's great powers. According to TDV, the principle of Ottoman territorial integrity laid down in the 1856 Treaty of Paris was openly violated, and territory was given even to Iran and Greece, which had had nothing to do with the war. The congress, which was expected to secure a general peace, did not produce the desired result and became the source of later disputes.", "e1a4c38d"], "metin": ["San Stefano settled the Eastern Question solely in Russia's interest, without taking the European balance of power into account. Britain could not accept Russia's gaining so much influence over Ottoman territory, nor could Austria accept the birth of a large Bulgaria in the midst of its own Balkan designs. The new Balkan states were also discontented: Serbia wanted Bosnia-Herzegovina and part of Macedonia, and Romania, although it was to receive Dobruja, did not want to cede to Russia Bessarabia, where Romanians lived. When no European state would consent to Russia's reaching down to the Aegean and the Adriatic, Russia accepted the congress it had at first not wanted. Before the congress, Britain asked for Cyprus as a base in the Eastern Mediterranean against Russia and made it known that it would take the island by force if it were not given; the Sublime Porte, hoping for British support at the congress, handed over the island on 4 June 1878, and a supplementary convention dated 1 July stated that if Russia returned the places it had occupied in the east, Britain would withdraw, and that ownership belonged to the Ottomans. The congress met on 13 June under the presidency of Bismarck. The Ottoman delegation was headed by Field Marshal Mehmed Ali Pasha; with him were the Minister of Public Works, Karatodori Pasha, and the Ottoman ambassador in Berlin, Sadullah Bey. The Ottoman delegation expected the terms of San Stefano to be softened, but each state protected its own interest, and even the British representatives, from whom help was expected, expressed views against the Ottomans. The sixty-four-article treaty signed a month later also shaped the following years through articles not included on the provisions card: the application of the 1868 regulation in Crete, with Europe to be kept informed; the cession of Kotur to Iran; the closure of the Danube to warships while it remained open to merchant ships; reforms in the places inhabited by Armenians; and the retention of the status of the Straits laid down in the London treaty of 1841 and the Paris treaty of 1856.", "622cb3da"]},
"sebep-sonuc-bolge0073-misir-sudan-siyaseti":{"sebep.b": ["Mehmed Ali of Kavala's decision to create a new army on the European model, and that army's need for men and natural resources", "caae44b6"], "sonuc.b": ["Sudan's passing under Egyptian administration with the surrender of Sennar (the Funj), and the beginning of the sixty-one-year \"Turkiyya period\"", "01363b09"], "bag": ["Significance: The Sudan campaign was the first step in Egypt's transformation from an Ottoman province into a power pursuing its own policy of expansion. Sudan's present-day borders are likewise the borders drawn by the administration that began with this campaign.", "a126072b"], "metin": ["① The four causes listed by the source. The article on Sudan in the TDV Encyclopaedia of Islam lists one by one Mehmed Ali's aims in 1821: to gain possession of the gold and emerald deposits, to make greater use of the sources of the Nile, to employ Sudanese soldiers in the army, and to neutralise the last Mamluk remnants who had taken refuge in the region. For this purpose the campaign was directed at the Dongola and Funj regions. So, according to the source, the answer to the question \"soldiers, gold or trade route?\" is \"all of them\"; but there is an order among them. ② The real driving force: the new army. The TDV article on Mehmed Ali Pasha of Kavala links the cause directly to the army. After the Hejaz campaign, the pasha turned his eyes to Sudan in order to obtain the human and natural resources that would form the basis of the new army he was thinking of creating to preserve his authority and continue his expansionist policy. The campaign organised in 1820 lasted two years. According to the same article, Mehmed Ali, having seen the inadequacy of his army in the Hejaz and Sudan campaigns, decided to create a new army called the Nizam-i Cedid and entrusted this task to the French colonel Joseph Sève (Süleyman Pasha). In other words, Sudan had been conceived both as a reason for the new army and as its first source. ③ And this calculation did not work out. This is the most striking sentence in the source: \"Although the necessary manpower was initially obtained from Sudan, it was not sufficient, and Egypt and Syria thus came to the fore\" (TDV). When the idea of an army formed of Sudanese soldiers failed, Mehmed Ali was obliged to take his own Egyptian peasants under arms; compulsory military service became the greatest source of discontent in the following years. ④ The gold and the trade route really did exist. The source lists the foreign trade of the Funj Sultanate item by item: the main trade goods were slaves, ivory, tamarind, butter, cream, musk and gold ingots; in Cairo, Asyut, Esna, Daraw, Qus, Aswan and Naqada there were commercial agencies that organised the coming and going of the caravans, and Egyptian merchants bought large numbers of slaves from here in particular (TDV, Funj). Sennar was the nodal point of this trade, and Egypt seized this node directly. ⑤ The Mamluk question. At a banquet he gave in Cairo on 1 March 1811, Mehmed Ali had had all but a few of the Mamluk beys killed; the survivors had fled south. According to the TDV article on the Funj, after the conquest the Mamluks of Sennar fled further south, left no trace and vanished from the stage of history. In this respect the campaign was as much the completion of an elimination as a territorial gain. ⑥ How long it lasted. From 1821 to 1882 Sudan remained a country governed by Mehmed Ali and his sons with the fullest powers in the name of the Ottoman State; in Sudanese history this period is called the \"Turkiyya\" (TDV). In 1841 Sudan was formally entrusted to Mehmed Ali by a firman, and the pasha was counted as governor of the provinces of Nubia, Sennar, Kordofan and Darfur. The period was completely brought down in 1885 by the movement that Muhammad Ahmad al-Mahdi launched in 1881. ⑦ So did Abyssinia, Djibouti, Eritrea and Somalia ever come onto the agenda? They did — but not in 1821, and none of them as the target of a campaign. For the time of Mehmed Ali, the source mentions no military plan for these regions; the Red Sea coast passed to Egypt by the sultan's firman. Massawa in present-day Eritrea and Sawakin on the Sudanese coast were given to Mehmed Ali by Sultan Abdülmecid in 1846 as a sâlyâne (a holding in return for a fixed annual payment); on the pasha's death (1849) Massawa returned to the control of the governor of the Hejaz (TDV, Massawa and the Province of Habesh). That is, twenty-five years after Sennar, and by appointment, not by conquest. ⑧ The Horn of Africa was opened a generation later. The real expansion came in the time of his grandson, Khedive Ismail Pasha: by a firman dated 27 May 1866, the district governorships (kaymakamlık) of Sawakin and Massawa were placed under the control of the governorship of Egypt; by another firman dated 1 June 1875, Zeila, on the coast of present-day Somaliland, and its environs were added, and Egypt agreed to send Istanbul 15,000 gold pieces for Zeila (TDV, Zeila). In 1874–1875 Ismail Pasha seized the coastal regions of Somalia in the name of the Ottoman State; his effort to spread into the interior was blocked by Britain, and in 1883 Egyptian troops withdrew from Somalia because of the Mahdist uprising (TDV, Somalia). ⑨ Abyssinia: war, yes; a plan, in 1821, no. According to the TDV article on Ethiopia, in 1872 Khedive Ismail Pasha, also encouraged by the British, wished to annex the sources of the Blue Nile to Egypt, but failed; after 1874 he organised three military campaigns against Ethiopia, which laid claim to Massawa, and in 1876 the fighting stopped when King Yohannes IV chose to come to an agreement. This was half a century after the surrender of Sennar. ⑩ Djibouti: the source does not speak of this. The TDV article on Djibouti mentions no Egyptian venture in the 19th-century history of the region; Obock there was bought by the French from a local chief in 1859 (TDV, Zeila and Somalia). This is an answer in itself: Djibouti never came onto Egypt's agenda at all. ⑪ The short answer. In 1821 Egypt's eyes were on the upper reaches of the Nile: soldiers, gold and the node of the Sennar trade. The African coast of the Red Sea — Massawa, Sawakin, then Zeila and the Somali shore — came into Egypt's hands a generation later and largely by Istanbul's firmans; the reckoning with Abyssinia belongs to the 1870s; Djibouti never came onto the agenda.", "56d31553"]},
"sebep-sonuc-bucas-1672":{"oneri": ["Why did the peace that won Podolia not last even a year?", "40c4c95f"], "sebep.b": ["Mehmed IV's Polish campaign: the capture of Kamianets (Kamaniçe) after a ten-day siege and the pressure put on Lviv (İlbav)", "c48f0660"], "sonuc.b": ["Treaty of Buczacz: Podolia became an Ottoman province (eyalet), and Poland agreed to pay an annual pişkeş (tributary gift)", "74e7b1a2"], "bag": ["Significance: With Kamianets and Podolia, the Ottomans established themselves in a region regarded as the key to the Crimea; Ukraine under Doroshenko became an autonomous entity attached to the Ottomans, and the way to emigration was opened to the Lipka Tatars. But it was precisely this gain that was unacceptable to Poland: the Polish Diet found the treaty too harsh, rejected it and imposed new taxes. From 1673 the war began again and lasted until Karlowitz; the intervening treaties of 1676 and 1678 took Buczacz as their basis. Poland, unable to recover Kamianets by war, regained it only in 1699 through an article added at Karlowitz.", "affa5e12"], "metin": ["When Kamianets fell on 27 August 1672, the Ottoman side began to press Poland towards a lasting peace: on 4 September the Polish king was informed that the campaign would continue unless he accepted that Podolia belonged to the Ottomans, and on 6 September the army marched on Lviv, where the king was staying. On 19 September the king asked for peace; arguing that Lviv lay outside Podolia, he offered money in return for the lifting of the siege. When the army laid siege to the palanka of Buczacz on 3 October, the Polish envoys arrived and on 14–16 October held talks with a delegation headed by the kaymakam Kara Mustafa Pasha. The treaty was signed on 18 October; Fazıl Ahmed Pasha sealed the Turkish text and the Polish envoys the Latin one. At the envoys' request, the palanka of Buczacz, which lay outside Podolia, was returned to Poland, and that same evening a pardon (aman) for the Polish king was proclaimed by firman. However, Poland held on to some of the castles and palankas it had undertaken to return and turned to a hostile policy towards the Ottomans; the Ottoman government decided on a second campaign.", "8a5ad46f"]},
"sebep-sonuc-bukres-1812":{"oneri": ["What was saved at the table while Napoleon marched on Moscow?", "bd02bd79"], "sebep.b": ["The breakdown of the Ottoman-Russian alliance formed against the French when the Russians incited the Greeks of the Morea and the islands to revolt, and the war that resumed in 1806", "76aa0d95"], "sonuc.b": ["Treaty of Bucharest: Bessarabia passed to Russia, and the Serbs were granted partial autonomy", "f12f7097"], "bag": ["Significance: With the Prut frontier, Bessarabia entered a period of Russian rule that was to last 106 years. According to the TDV, together with the autonomy granted to the Serbs, a period began in which the Orthodox and Slav peoples were openly incited and supported against Ottoman rule.", "33a756ee"], "metin": ["The autonomy provision was not carried out at once: the revolt of the Serbs, who wanted broader rights, was suppressed in 1813, Belgrade was retaken, and the leader of the revolt, Karađorđe, fled to Austria. But in 1815 a second Serbian uprising broke out under the leadership of Miloš Obrenović; although it too was suppressed the same year, from then on the privileges granted to the Serbs began to be broadened, and the question came back to the table with the Akkerman Convention of 1826.", "4aa66331"]},
"sebep-sonuc-celali-isyanlari":{"oneri": ["The name of one sheikh became the name of every uprising in Anatolia for a century.", "4e2a47a7"], "sebep.b": ["The mass of unemployed levend (irregular soldiers) and sekban (musket-bearing mercenaries) that built up in Anatolia during the years of the Long War, sipahis who had lost their revenue grants, the debased akçe and the oppression of provincial administrators", "f924eb41"], "sonuc.b": ["The 'Great Flight' in Anatolia: peasants abandoning their homes and fleeing to fortresses, to Istanbul and to Rumelia; followed by Kuyucu Murad Pasha's bloody purge", "691f118c"], "bag": ["Significance: The Celali movement began as a cause bound up with sect and the claim to rule; by the seventeenth century it had turned into a movement against the centre by people cut off from the state apparatus. While the Long War went on, Anatolia was left defenceless; the revolts meant that sufficient forces could not be assigned to the Austrian and Iranian fronts, which at times led to defeats. The population and the productive order of Anatolia were deeply shaken.", "956a4271"], "metin": ["The name comes from Sheikh Celal of Bozok, who rose in revolt around Tokat in 1519 claiming to be the Mahdi. His revolt was harshly suppressed, but from then on the people began to call every armed rebel who did them harm a 'Celali'; the Ottoman conception of the state, for its part, regarded these movements as rebellion against the sultan (huruj ala al-sultan). In the first half of the sixteenth century most revolts arose from Turkmen groups of Shi'ite leanings nourished by Safavid propaganda. In the second half of the century the ground shifted: timar holders and 'yevmlü' (day-wage) soldiers who had gathered around Prince Bayezid and then scattered, suhtes (madrasa students) who could find no position from the madrasa, peasants who abandoned their farms because there was not enough land, and companies of levends turned out of the households of the beys began to roam about in armed bands. After 1596 the movement became organised around great chiefs. Karayazıcı Abdülhalim seized the citadel of Urfa and sent out decrees under the name 'Halim Shah'; after his death his brother Deli Hasan overran Kütahya and marched on Afyonkarahisar; Tavil Ahmed, Canbolatoğlu Ali and Kalenderoğlu Mehmed followed them. Villagers took refuge within city walls, and the wealthy migrated to Istanbul and Rumelia. The state restored calm first by giving the chiefs offices, then by force through Kuyucu Murad Pasha's campaigns of 1607-1608. The remnants later joined the revolts of Abaza Mehmed Pasha and Abaza Hasan Pasha; after the time of Köprülü Mehmed Pasha the sources began to say 'bandit' (eşkıya) more often than 'Celali'.", "ab5aa12c"]},
"sebep-sonuc-edirne-1829":{"oneri": ["With the Russian army in Edirne, was there any room left to bargain?", "4b1ee7cf"], "sebep.b": ["The intervention of Britain, France and Russia in the Morean revolt of 1821, the sinking of the Ottoman fleet at Navarino and Russia's declaration of war in 1828", "deab6cdf"], "sonuc.b": ["Treaty of Adrianople: the independence of Greece was accepted, and the mouth of the Danube and the Caucasian fortresses passed to Russia", "d1dd5d41"], "bag": ["Significance: According to the TDV, the Ottomans, unable to halt the Russian advance, could achieve no success in diplomacy either and were forced to accept the terms dictated by the victor; the payment of 11.5 million Hungarian gold pieces that they accepted was heavier than the treasury could bear.", "aeb7bf97"], "metin": ["On 22 August 1829 the Russian army entered Edirne without meeting resistance and also occupied Kırklareli and Lüleburgaz; while Russian ships attacked the Bosphorus, a squadron blockaded the Dardanelles. The Ottoman delegates who went to Edirne through Prussian mediation could not soften the Russian terms, apart from a small change in the commercial indemnity. The burden was later reduced: in 1830 the indemnity was lowered to 8 million Dutch gold pieces, and a further 1 million was written off on condition that the full independence of Greece be accepted; in January 1834 it was reduced to 5 million. The Russian withdrawal, which was tied to the indemnity, took place from Wallachia and Moldavia only in 1834, and from Silistra under the convention of 8 April 1836.", "6c6fcef5"]},
"sebep-sonuc-hunkar-iskelesi-1833":{"oneri": ["Why did a Russian fleet enter the Bosphorus while the Egyptian army was at Kütahya?", "64a31e5a"], "sebep.b": ["The forces of Mehmed Ali Pasha defeating the Ottoman army at Konya and threatening Istanbul; the acceptance of Russian help by the Sublime Porte, which found no support from France and Britain", "1dedc447"], "sonuc.b": ["The defensive alliance of Hünkâr İskelesi and the secret article providing for the closure of the Dardanelles in Russia's favour", "980eeac1"], "bag": ["Significance: According to the TDV, the secret article turned the Egyptian question into an international question of the Straits; it caused a crisis in Europe, gave rise through Metternich's mediation to the Münchengrätz Agreement (18 September 1833) and, when the eight-year term expired, was replaced by the London Straits Convention of 1841.", "c64e9a2b"], "metin": ["When İbrahim Pasha's army defeated Grand Vizier Mehmed Reşid Pasha at Konya on 21 December 1832 and reached Kütahya on 2 February 1833, both the capital and the dynasty came under threat. While calling on Russia for help, the Sublime Porte also wished to use this invitation as a card to spur France and Britain into action; Russia, however, gave it no opportunity: it anchored its fleet at Büyükdere (20 February) and then landed its ground forces at Beykoz (5 April). Through the Kütahya talks, the provinces of Egypt and all Syria, together with Adana, were left to Mehmed Ali's side (firman of 6 May). The Russian forces still did not withdraw; the alliance proposal brought by Orlov was signed on 8 July as the heavy bill for the help, and two days later the Russian fleet and ground forces left the Bosphorus. The TDV also sees behind the indignation felt in Istanbul at the Russian alliance the propaganda that Mehmed Ali Pasha conducted in Europe.", "f9d73540"]},
"sebep-sonuc-karlofca-1699":{"oneri": ["How did the side that lost the war manage to sit bolt upright at the negotiating table?", "35286fc6"], "sebep.b": ["The sixteen-year war on many fronts that began with the 1683 defeat at Vienna: Venice's advance northward from the Morea, the fall of Azov in 1696 and the defeat at Zenta in 1697", "b6bdcf0e"], "sonuc.b": ["Karlowitz: Hungary and Transylvania ceded to Austria, Podolia to Poland and the Morea to Venice; with Russia, only a truce", "f248d009"], "bag": ["Significance: TDV regards Karlowitz as a turning point in Ottoman history — for the first time the state accepted by treaty a loss of territory on such a scale, the peace was made at the table of mediating powers (England and the Netherlands), and the Ottoman-Austrian equality that had been accepted at Zsitvatorok but could not be put into practice was written anew. Even so, according to TDV the Ottoman delegation showed none of the abjectness of a defeated party: by holding on to Temeşvar and to fortresses in Bosnia such as Bihać, it preserved the possibility of recovering the lost places in the future. Ottoman statesmen saw the treaty more as a truce than as a lasting peace — an outlook that found expression in 1711 at the Pruth and then in the war for the Morea declared on Venice.", "c5337a74"], "metin": ["On the road to peace both sides were weary. On the Ottoman side, the army had withdrawn to the Danube after Zenta; Venice, advancing northward from the Morea, had taken Athens and many fortresses in Dalmatia and Bosnia; and the Russians had captured Azov in 1696. Grand Vizier Amcazade Hüseyin Pasha persuaded Mustafa II, who would consent to peace only once part of the losses had been recovered; his arguments were that the people were being crushed under extraordinary taxes, that villages were emptying, that finding soldiers and munitions had become impossible, that public order had broken down in Anatolia and that there were revolts in Rumelia. On the other side, Austria wanted peace because of the question of the Spanish succession, which was expected to erupt; Venice fell in with it, but Poland and Russia, not having obtained what they wanted, favoured continuing the war. The preliminary protocol signed at Edirne on 27 January 1698 accepted the principle of 'alâ hâlihî' (each side keeps what it holds). The talks that began at Karlowitz on 13 November 1698 ran to thirty-six sessions over seventy-two days in a large tent with four doors. The reisülküttab (chief secretary) Rami Mehmed Efendi studied the texts of earlier treaties and obtained reports from experts; even the Austrian delegate Marsigli wrote that the Christian delegates were at times reduced to a pitiable state. The longest wrangle was with Venice: agreement was reached when Austria declared that if the Ottoman demands were not accepted, Venice would carry on the war alone. Because the Russian delegate did not recognise the protocol, only a two-year truce was made with Russia; peace was signed in Istanbul on 14 July 1700. The terms were ill received in Vienna, Warsaw and Venice. The fact that the second delegate, Mavrokordato, later received a decoration from the emperor gave rise to accusations that he had acted against Ottoman interests; TDV says these accusations must be treated with caution.", "8373db96"]},
"sebep-sonuc-kasr-i-sirin-1639":{"oneri": ["How did three days of talks make a frontier for two centuries?", "e2bd586e"], "sebep.b": ["Murad IV's recapture of Baghdad, the withdrawal of the Safavid army to Qasr-e Shirin, and the exhaustion of both sides from the wars fought on the Azerbaijan and Iraq fronts since 1630", "4cf61a2e"], "sonuc.b": ["Treaty of Qasr-e Shirin (Zuhab): Iraq remained with the Ottomans and Yerevan with the Safavids", "f48a09af"], "bag": ["Significance: According to the TDV, it put a definitive end to the Ottoman-Safavid dispute and was taken as the basis in later centuries: the Treaty of Kerden of 1746 renewed it, and the Erzurum treaties of 1823 and 1847 settled frontier questions on its basis.", "81507167"], "metin": ["The peace also brought economic returns. Shah Abbas's transfer of his capital from Tabriz to Isfahan and his founding of New Julfa for the Armenian merchants had shifted the trade routes southward; in the climate of peace, Iranian silk and textiles were carried via Baghdad to Aleppo, and via Mosul to Diyarbekir and the cities of Anatolia. According to the figures reported by the TDV, Iran's silk exports rose by about half between 1640 and 1670. This balanced period lasted until Nader Shah, founder of the Afshar dynasty, came to power in 1736.", "35eec015"]},
"sebep-sonuc-kucuk-kaynarca-1774":{"oneri": ["Why was a peace signed in a besieged army camp misread for a century?", "9811fc1f"], "sebep.b": ["The war of 1768, declared without preparation: the occupation of the Crimea and of Wallachia and Moldavia, the raid at Çeşme, the collapse of the Bucharest talks and, after the rout at Kozluca in 1774, the encirclement of the army headquarters at Shumen (Şumnu)", "757943f7"], "sonuc.b": ["Küçük Kaynarca: the political separation of the Crimean Khanate from the Ottomans, freedom for Russian ships in the Black Sea and the Mediterranean, and an indemnity of 15,000 purses", "f5c01ade"], "bag": ["Significance: According to TDV, this war both laid bare Ottoman weakness and testified to Russia's emergence as a great power. In the official French translation that Russia printed in 1775, the church to be built in Istanbul was rendered not as a 'Greek' (Rum) church but as a 'Russian' one; this falsification gave Russia a claim to a general protectorate over the Ottoman Orthodox and became one of the causes of the Crimean War. The claim was formally ended only by the Reform Edict of 1856 and the Treaty of Paris. On the other hand, Russia's recognition of the caliphal tie with the Crimea is counted as an important gain, even if it did not make up for the loss.", "b9b6b770"], "metin": ["The war was declared on account of Russia's interference in Poland's internal affairs and its attacks on the Crimean frontier — with just cause, but without any preparation. The Russians soon occupied the Crimea and Wallachia and Moldavia and crossed the Danube. In the Ottoman army, bad leadership, indiscipline, the collapsed menzil (post station) system and shortages of provisions heralded a general disintegration. The Russians were not at ease either; the Pugachev revolt that was shaking the country turned them towards peace talks — Frederick the Great had described this war as a 'war of the one-eyed against the blind'. In the talks held at Focșani and Bucharest in 1772-1773, granting independence to the Crimea was considered contrary to Islamic law, and the surrender of fortresses and an indemnity were also rejected amid the sharp reaction of the ulema in Istanbul; the war resumed in March 1773. In the spring of 1774 the Russians inflicted a heavy defeat on the Ottoman forces at Kozluca (on 25 June according to TDV) and besieged the headquarters at Shumen. Because even the interpreters had fled in the rout, Marshal Rumyantsev's peace letter went untranslated for several days. With the camp, reduced to 7-8,000 men, under siege, Rumyantsev insisted on negotiating while the war continued, without declaring a truce. The Ottoman delegates, Ahmed Resmi Efendi and Münib Efendi, held talks at Küçük Kaynarca on 16, 17 and 21 July; when the approval of the gravely ill grand vizier arrived, the treaty was signed on the evening of 21 July 1774. Catherine II found the terms more satisfactory than expected.", "494c3856"]},
"sebep-sonuc-mondros-1918":{"oneri": ["How did an armistice open the door to occupation?", "c3bb9cbb"], "sebep.b": ["The collapse of the Palestine–Syria front, and Bulgaria's signing of a ceasefire and withdrawal from the war, which left Thrace and Istanbul open to attack", "585fe0aa"], "sonuc.b": ["Armistice of Mudros: the disbanding of the army and navy, and the right of occupation given to the Entente powers by Articles 7 and 24", "26993965"], "bag": ["Significance: The TDV regards Mudros as the herald of the end of the Ottoman State's six-century history; the occupations that began with the abuse of Articles 7 and 24 gave rise to the War of Independence led by Mustafa Kemal Pasha and to the founding of the Republic of Turkey.", "6091cf80"], "metin": ["The British offensive that began on the Palestine–Syria front on 19 September 1918 routed the Ottoman forces; with Bulgaria's withdrawal on 29 September, the land link with the allies was cut. Two peace offers made by the Ottomans through Spain (5 and 12 October) went unanswered; the government of Talat Pasha, having received a vote of no confidence, was replaced by that of Ahmed İzzet Pasha (14 October), and Admiral Calthorpe was reached through the captive British General Townshend. Trusting in Wilson's principles, the government sent its delegation in the expectation that there would be no interference in the administration and no landing of foreign troops in the country; the text signed, however, dismantled the Ottoman defence system almost entirely. The British occupation of Mosul within a few days on the basis of Article 7 confirmed the suspicions; the Committee of Union and Progress dissolved itself on 1 November, and Enver, Cemal and Talat Pashas secretly left the country.", "3df03dd8"]},
"sebep-sonuc-paris-1856":{"oneri": ["Admission to Europe on paper", "a9a49644"], "sebep.b": ["Russia's demand for an internationally valid right of protection over the Orthodox population in the Ottoman Empire, the dispute over the 'holy places' and the Crimean War, which began in 1853", "e7a6090e"], "sonuc.b": ["Treaty of Paris: the Ottomans were accepted as an equal member of the European states system, and the Black Sea was neutralised", "cbf265be"], "bag": ["Significance: According to the TDV, it ended the superiority that the treaties of Küçük Kaynarca, Akkerman and Adrianople had given Russia, changed the Straits regime of 1841 and was interpreted as having checked Russia's expansion towards the Ottomans, which had been under way since Catherine II. Because of the Reform Edict, it also drew the state into an important constitutional change.", "afbe2766"], "metin": ["The guarantees given soon ceased to work. When France was defeated by Prussia in 1870–1871, Russia announced, before that war had ended, that it would no longer abide by the Black Sea clause; the TDV regards this as the first sign of the great Ottoman-Russian war that was approaching. The guarantee of territorial integrity could not prevent the great dismemberment in the war of 1877–1878. The burden of debt left by the war and the social conflicts that arose in the implementation of the Reform Edict shaped the period that followed. The TDV reports that at the Congress of Berlin (1878) Bismarck rebuked the Ottoman delegates, who were trying to defend their rights, by telling them that European law was not for them — a sign of how far the status won in 1856 had remained on paper.", "71cfb416"]},
"sebep-sonuc-pasarofca-1718":{"oneri": ["The 1718 Treaty of Passarowitz — the Morea stayed, Belgrade was lost", "809d732f"], "sebep.b": ["The desire to avenge Karlowitz: the war declared on Venice; Austria's alliance with Venice in 1716 and its entry into the war; the defeats at Petrovaradin and Belgrade", "bddbeceb"], "sonuc.b": ["Passarowitz: the Morea remained Ottoman; the Banat, Oltenia (Lesser Wallachia), northern Serbia including Belgrade, and northern Bosnia passed to Austria", "c5eb1153"], "bag": ["Significance: According to TDV, the treaty served Austria more than Venice; with its great territorial losses the Ottoman state not only withdrew from Central Europe but also began to cease being a strong state in European eyes. The commercial treaty signed in the same days granted Austrian merchants free trade at a 3% customs duty and the right to maintain consuls and dragomans in all ports; in return the Ottomans too would be able to open consulates (şehbenderlik) in Austrian territory, and a consul (şehbender) was sent to Vienna. Belgrade and northern Serbia were recovered only by the 1739 Treaty of Belgrade.", "c503d80b"], "metin": ["Ottoman statesmen regarded Karlowitz as a truce. After halting Russia at the Pruth, they declared war on Venice, which they claimed had violated the treaty; the Ottoman army and navy had the upper hand in this war. Grand Vizier Damad Ali Pasha hoped Austria would remain neutral, but Prince Eugene favoured war, and on 13 April 1716 the Austrian-Venetian alliance was signed. In Istanbul some statesmen proposed placating Austria by returning a few unimportant islands to Venice; when the grand vizier threatened them with death they fell silent, and war was declared. Ali Pasha fell in battle at Petrovaradin, the Banat and Temeşvar passed to Austria, and in 1717 Belgrade fell as well. The question of peace set two poles against each other at court: at the head of the war party were Numan Pasha and the Köprülü family, and tradesmen who were profiting from high prices also wanted the war to go on; the people, however, were exhausted, and the sultan favoured peace. Charles VI's harsh terms, demanding Bosnia, Serbia and Wallachia, enraged Ahmed III, but through the mediation of the English and Dutch ambassadors it was agreed to negotiate on the basis of the existing situation. In the negotiations that began on 5 June 1718, Austria first made the surrender of Rákóczi and his men a condition; the Ottomans refused, and Austria backed down. In truth neither side was in a condition to fight: Austria was on the brink of a new conflict with Spain. The Ottomans asked for a fifteen-year peace; Austria's proposal of twenty-four years was accepted. After seventy days, the treaties with Austria and Venice were signed on 21 July 1718.", "ef9861d9"]},
"sebep-sonuc-sevr-1920":{"oneri": ["Debated for nineteen months at the partition table, forced on Istanbul for signature, never applied: what killed Sèvres…", "1720b20a"], "sebep.b": ["Paris Peace Conference: the victorious powers began to discuss the future of the Ottoman lands", "d3f8317e"], "sonuc.b": ["The Treaty of Sèvres was signed — the Ankara government did not recognise it, and the text could not be applied", "d11a8e42"], "bag": ["Significance: Sèvres was the written form of the solution Europe devised for the 'Eastern Question'. According to TDV, the 1923 Treaty of Lausanne preserved the distribution of the Arab mandates made at Sèvres, while introducing important changes in all matters concerning the new Turkish state.", "d797533f"], "metin": ["The decision-making body of the conference was the Council of Ten, composed of the prime ministers and foreign ministers of Britain, France, Italy, the USA and Japan; it determined the regions to be wholly separated from the Ottomans — Arabia, Syria, Mesopotamia, Palestine, Armenia and, on a British proposal, Kurdistan. The Allies could not agree: France and Italy wanted the secret wartime agreements to be implemented, whereas the USA wanted Ottoman rule to continue in regions with a Turkish majority, in accordance with Wilson's twelfth point. The Greeks, supported by Britain, France and the USA, landed at İzmir on 15 May 1919; this landing led to the rapid organisation of the national resistance in Anatolia. Faced with the demand of Indian Muslims that the caliph remain in Istanbul, the British government on 6 January 1920 rejected the plan to expel the Turks from Europe. On 16 March 1920 Istanbul was officially occupied; on 23 April the Grand National Assembly began its work in Ankara. The text was given its final form at San Remo (18-26 April 1920) and communicated to Tevfik Pasha on 11 May; Tevfik Pasha stated that he could not accept the terms. Lloyd George set the Greek army in motion on 22 June; after Bursa fell on 8 July and Thrace on 20 July, the Council of the Sultanate convened by the sultan on 22 July, in the face of reports that Istanbul would be occupied by the Greeks, voted in favour of signature, with the exception of Lieutenant General of Artillery Rıza Pasha. What followed came quickly: after Sakarya, France made an agreement with the Ankara government on 20 October 1921 and recognised the new state, and even Lloyd George declared on 10 August 1921 that Sèvres had now been torn up. The victory of 30 August 1922 was followed by the Armistice of Mudanya on 11 October, the abolition of the sultanate on 1 November and the Treaty of Lausanne on 24 July 1923.", "a078f4bf"]},
"sebep-sonuc-usi-1912":{"oneri": ["The last Ottoman province in Africa was given up at a table in Switzerland; but the war did not end there, and in the Aegean…", "4fa24612"], "sebep.b": ["Italy's declaration of war on the Ottoman State on the grounds of protecting its citizens in Tripoli", "8d96d53b"], "sonuc.b": ["Treaty of Ouchy: the Ottoman State accepted that it had ceded Tripoli to Italy", "af6dab0c"], "bag": ["Significance: The loss of the Ottoman State's last territory on the African continent. The treaty's loose end was tied up at Lausanne: Rhodes and the Dodecanese, which Italy was supposed to evacuate under the Treaty of Ouchy, remained in its hands, and Turkey formally renounced its rights over Tripoli.", "b90736c2"], "metin": ["As the colonial race in Africa gathered pace in the second half of the 19th century, Italy's interest in Tripoli grew. According to TDV, Italy declared war in September 1911 on the pretext of protecting its citizens living in the city from the Ottoman government, and this was the beginning of the province's transformation into an Italian colony; during the occupation a significant part of the city's population perished. By the treaty signed in 1912 at Ouchy in Lausanne, Switzerland, the Ottoman State accepted that it had abandoned to Italy this last province of its in Africa; the caliph, however, would continue to be regarded as the religious leader of the Muslim community there. The treaty did not in fact end the conflict: Ottoman soldiers carried on the resistance together with the local people and fought against Italy under the name 'Africa Groups Command' until the Armistice of Mudros in 1918. Tripoli remained under Italian occupation until 1943, then came under British control, and independence was proclaimed in 1951. The other end of the treaty was in the Aegean: according to TDV's Lausanne article, Rhodes and the Dodecanese, which the Italians kept under occupation although they were obliged to return them under the Treaty of Ouchy, were left to them in 1923 by Article 15 of Lausanne; by Article 22, the rights over Tripoli were also renounced.", "a97d4df9"]},
"sebep-sonuc-vasvar-1664":{"oneri": ["Heavy losses at Saint Gotthard, gains at the table", "e178b653"], "sebep.b": ["Habsburg intervention in Transylvania, Zrínyi's building of a fortress opposite Kanizsa and Fazıl Ahmed Pasha's campaign of 1663, in which he took Uyvar", "c54bfafe"], "sonuc.b": ["Treaty of Vasvár: the withdrawal of both sides from Transylvania, the retention of Uyvar and Novigrad by the Ottomans, and a twenty-year peace", "739b5091"], "bag": ["Significance: The TDV describes this treaty as the text that defined the widest Ottoman frontiers in Hungary. It also carries an innovation in legal terms: whereas at Zsitvatorok the peace rested on the sultan's personal pledge, Mehmed IV's ahidname of ratification also bound the rulers who would descend from him — the principle of the continuity of the state. But the peace alienated the Hungarian nobles; this discontent led to the Thököly revolt and, in the second half of the 1670s, to the two empires confronting each other again.", "b98a8982"], "metin": ["The crisis began in Transylvania: after the death of George II Rákóczi, the Ottomans took Várad on 27 August 1660, while the Transylvanian nobles consented to the stationing of German troops in some castles. In the talks held for two years with the Habsburg envoy in Istanbul, Simon Reniger, the 1662 draft of the reisülküttab (chief of the chancery) Şamizade Mehmed Efendi already contained most of the articles later signed; when Vienna dragged its feet, Fazıl Ahmed Pasha set out on campaign in 1663 and took Uyvar after a six-week siege. In 1664, after the unexpected attacks of Zrínyi and Hohenlohe in January and April, the allies besieged Kanizsa at the end of April; the siege could be raised only at the beginning of June, and at the end of June the Ottomans took and burned Zrínyi's Yenikale. On 1 August 1664, Ottoman units attempting to cross the River Rába suffered heavy losses against Montecuccoli's army (Saint Gotthard). This defeat strengthened the desire for peace at the Ottoman headquarters; eight days later the texts were exchanged in Fazıl Ahmed Pasha's tent. Leopold I delayed ratification in the hope of better terms, but because of the condition of his army and the Imperial Diet's reluctance for war, he ratified on 9 September; the confirmation took place with a ceremony near Uyvar on 27 October 1664. A note on the date: the treaty was signed on 9 August 1664; because the date in the Turkish text was wrongly converted, it appears in Western historiography as 10 August.", "598b1e60"]},
"sebep-sonuc-yas-1792":{"oneri": ["The army refused to fight", "d48bcd0d"], "sebep.b": ["Russia's annexation of the Crimea in 1783 and the Ottoman declaration of war on Russia with the aim of recovering the Crimea; Austria's entry into the war on Russia's side as well", "fa10a91f"], "sonuc.b": ["Treaty of Jassy: the Dniester (Turla) became the frontier, and the hope of recovering the Crimea came to an end", "17bec341"], "bag": ["Significance: According to the TDV, the dream of recovering the Crimea was over, and with Russia's extension into Georgia the danger that would come from the Caucasus became apparent. The army's open declaration that it could not fight made the restructuring of the state in every field urgent; the peace was the starting point of the Nizam-i Cedid reforms.", "b7f66d88"], "metin": ["After the rout at Măcin (9 July 1791), the civil and military notables who gathered in the army camp acknowledged that the army no longer had the strength to fight and sealed a memorandum in favour of peace. The TDV describes this as a 'boycott', in the sense of the army's refusal to fight, without precedent in Ottoman history; Selim III, who wanted the war to continue, was left with no option but peace. Developments in Europe also played a part: Britain had opposed Ochakov (Özi) remaining with Russia with a firmness that went as far as the threat of war, and the danger that the French Revolution posed to monarchies had pushed Catherine II to end the Turkish war. The grand embassies sent by each side to the other after the peace were the last example of this tradition.", "1ecbc422"]},
"sebep-sonuc-zitvatorok-1606":{"oneri": ["Why did a title outweigh a fortress?", "95e3cb68"], "sebep.b": ["The financial straits into which the long war begun in 1593 had driven both sides; the Celali revolts in Anatolia, the Safavid counter-offensive in the east and the revolt of Prince Bocskay of Transylvania against the Habsburgs", "db194902"], "sonuc.b": ["Treaty of Zsitvatorok: a peace in which the Habsburg ruler was regarded as the sultan's equal and the annual tribute was abolished", "b998d332"], "bag": ["Significance: According to the TDV, this was the first treaty in Habsburg-Ottoman relations to be decided by joint negotiation rather than depending on the sultan's unilateral goodwill; the two rulers dealt with each other as equal parties, and in diplomatic practice the Ottomans' superiority over their Christian rival came to an end.", "315f14fa"], "metin": ["Peace soundings had been made since 1596 in the winter months, outside the campaigning season, but had produced no result. In November 1604 the revolt of István Bocskay, Prince of Transylvania, against the Habsburgs interrupted the talks; the Hungarian nobles became the decisive party between the two great powers. Grand Vizier Lala Mehmed Pasha at first preferred to continue the war, but he died in June 1606 while preparing for the campaign. The treaty was later extended six times, and its terms remained in force for sixty years.", "2ac692fb"]},
"tabi-devlet-vassallik":{"sebep.b": ["The fact that the direct annexation of Serbia in 1439 and its conversion into a sanjak made distant lands beyond the Danube untenable in terms of the cost of defense and administration", "c33d4b54"], "sonuc.b": ["The recognition of Wallachia, in the Treaty of Edirne-Szeged, in the status of a tributary (vassal) state governed by its own voivode — the model for Wallachia, Moldavia, Transylvania and the Crimea over the following three centuries", "d4f4d91a"], "bag": ["According to the TDV article on Wallachia (Eflak), this treaty established 'a dual sovereignty': the voivode paid tribute and remained free in internal affairs, and the land was not divided into timars — the exact opposite of the solution applied to Serbia, which in the same period was directly annexed and made into a province.", "28922bc8"], "metin": ["A tributary (vassal) state was the form of government in which the Ottomans, without annexing the territory they had conquered, left the local dynasty at its head in return for tribute and troops. Wallachia, Moldavia, Transylvania, the Crimean Khanate and the Sharifate of the Hejaz were different forms of this model; what they had in common was that the land was not divided into timars (military fiefs) and that local administration and religion continued. Because it secured the same control more cheaply than direct provincial rule, it was preferred especially in frontier regions that were expensive to defend.", "3eed6869"]},
"tartisma-ahmed1-kardes-katli-terki":{"oneri": ["The sources put forward four separate explanations: the lack of an heir, public reaction, centres of power in the palace…", "52522354"], "ad": ["Why did Ahmed I not have his brother Mustafa killed — and was fratricide really ‘abolished’?", "7a6e0c5c"], "metin": ["View 1 — There was no heir, and killing him would have endangered the dynasty. TDV's articles 'Mustafa I' and 'Şehzade' state that Ahmed came to the throne at the age of fourteen, that he did not yet have a son and that Mustafa was the only other surviving male member of the dynasty: for a young sultan who might or might not be able to leave an heir, killing his only brother would have meant risking the extinction of the line. According to this explanation, the decision was not a change of principle but a necessity. Indeed, after his sons were born, Mustafa's life was in danger again; the report of the Venetian ambassador Contarini of 1612 writes that Ahmed several times set about having him killed and then gave it up (the account of a foreign observer, relayed by TDV). View 2 — The reaction of 1595. The same two articles count as a second factor the hatred that Mehmed III's killing of his nineteen brothers aroused among the people and among administrators. View 3 — The actors in the palace. TDV's article 'Kösem Sultan' writes that there is no definite information about Kösem's role in this process, but that she had a relative share in bringing the practice to an end, even if it was in order to protect her own sons. The same articles also mention the role of the Shaykh al-Islam Hocazâde Esad Efendi and the deputy grand vizier (kaymakam) Sofu Mehmed Pasha in Mustafa's elevation to the throne in 1617, the promptings of the chief black eunuch (kızlarağası) Mustafa Agha according to the historian of the period Peçuylu, and the report that Kösem belonged to this group. View 4 — A sultan's decision, or a practice that took hold later? Here TDV's own articles place the emphasis differently. The article 'Ahmed I' says that the principle of seniority (ekberiyet) was adopted from this sultan onwards. The article 'Şehzade', however, writes that the three-century tradition came to an end in 1617, when Mustafa came to the throne on Ahmed's death, and that life in the cage (kafes) began from that year. The article 'Mustafa I' also reports that the decision of 1617 was taken by going ‘outside the prevailing order of succession’, that it was criticised by contemporaries and that Osman II, in a letter to the King of England, maintained that succession passed from father to son. Kayapınar (Belleten, 2004), reviewing Vatin and Veinstein's work ‘Le Sérail ébranlé’ (2003), reports that the authors see the confinement of the princes to the cage and the choice of 1617 as a decision of the religious and political notables of the state, and that fratricide, though it very probably did not disappear altogether because of the reactions, became rarer. Hitzel (BCAI, 2005), reviewing the same book, states that the authors treat the practice as one that preserved order, although it was never a recognised rule. THE LIMIT OF THE WORD ‘ABOLISHED’: after 1603 too, brothers or close relatives were killed — Osman II had his brother Mehmed killed (1621), and Murad IV his brothers Bayezid, Süleyman and Kasım (1635, 1638); Mustafa IV ordered the killing of his cousin Selim III, and Mahmud II in turn had his brother Mustafa IV strangled (1808). In other words, what changed in 1603 was not that the provision was deleted from the law code, but that the open contest for the throne among princes sent out to sanjaks gave way to princes waiting under surveillance in the palace.", "4492b8b0"], "kisa": ["The sources put forward four separate explanations: the lack of an heir, public reaction, centres of power in the palace and a practice of succession that took hold later. The word ‘abolished’ is itself disputed: the practice continued at intervals until 1808.", "5cc8d789"], "not": ["This card does not choose any view. The four explanations do not exclude one another; most of the sources count more than one of them together. Further reading: Leslie P. Peirce, The Imperial Harem (Oxford University Press, 1993) · Baki Tezcan, The Second Ottoman Empire (Cambridge University Press, 2010) · Nicolas Vatin – Gilles Veinstein, Le Sérail ébranlé (Fayard, 2003). For the scene of the event, see the card ‘An illness, a storm’ in the Magazine section; for a comparison of the practice with other dynasties, see the Curiosities section.", "8cb0ce42"]},
"tartisma-akdeniz-vehran-onemi":{"baslik": ["Oran (Vehrân): A Port Taken and Lost for Two Centuries — Why Was It Indispensable to the Regency of Algiers?", "b7f260c9"], "metin": ["① Geography itself defended the city. Oran was founded in 290 (903) by members of the Maghrawa tribe; its name means 'two lions' or 'lions' den' in the Tuareg language. The old city was built on both banks of the Ra's al-Ayn valley, and the 375-meter Aïdour hill to its west protected the harbor from westerly winds — in the words of the TDV Encyclopaedia of Islam, 'its geographical structure has given the city a position that is sheltered against attacks from outside and strategic'. At a point very close to both Morocco and Spain, it was situated so as to control the sea route in the western Mediterranean. ② It was already a base in the Middle Ages. It suffered destruction repeatedly in the struggle between the Fatimids and the Andalusian Umayyads (297/910, 342/954), and passed successively into the hands of the Banu Ifran, the Zirids, the Almoravids (473/1080, Yusuf ibn Tashfin) and the Almohads; the Almohad ruler had a fleet built here and set out from here on his campaigns to take all of the Maghreb and al-Andalus. Under the Abd al-Wadids (Zayyanids) the city became the port of Tlemcen, the great trading center of the interior; for this reason the Marinids, who besieged Tlemcen in the 14th century, landed troops on the coast of Oran and had a fortress built here. When Granada fell to the Spaniards in 1492, Muslims and Jews from al-Andalus took refuge here and enlarged the city further. ③ 1509: the Spanish landing. On 17 May 1509 the Kingdom of Castile occupied Oran, killed most of its people and took the rest captive; no Muslims or Jews remained in the city. The Spaniards enlarged the walls and fortresses and turned it into a garrison town. ④ Five Ottoman attempts, four of them unsuccessful. Oruç Reis, master of Algiers, blockaded the city in 1516; but because the local rulers were uneasy at the Ottoman presence and cooperated with Spain, Oruç Reis and his brother İshak Reis were killed in 1518. Barbaros Hayreddin Pasha's attempts at recovery were not enough to retake the city either. In 1556, under Salih Pasha, beylerbey (governor-general) of Algiers, Piyale Pasha's expedition came to nothing because of the beylerbey's death. In 1563 Hasan Pasha besieged the city again but was forced to withdraw when help arrived from Spain. In the period up to 1708 the Spaniards had to contend with famine and plague as well as Berber attacks. ⑤ 1708: the first definitive Ottoman conquest — and its loss in 1732. After a long siege, the forces of Mustafa bin Yusuf Bey, who rebuilt Mascara, the center of the Western Beylik of Algiers, and who was popularly known as 'Bouchelaghem' (the big-mustached), received the surrender of Oran in 1708 in the name of the Ottomans; the news aroused great joy in Istanbul, and the bey moved his seat to the city. During the twenty-four years of Ottoman rule maritime trade revived. But in 1732 the city was retaken by Spain; with Spanish rule trade died down again and the population declined. ⑥ The earthquake of 1790 and 12 September 1792: the definitive conquest. When the great earthquake of 1790 devastated the town, the people founded a new settlement on the eastern side of the valley. On 12 September 1792 the Ottoman governor in Mascara, Kuloğlu Mehmed bin Osman, succeeded in finally retaking Oran from the Spaniards; this second conquest too was greeted with great satisfaction in Istanbul, and the people gave Mehmed Bey the title 'Mehmed (Muhammad) el-Kebir' (the Great). In total Spain held the city for 199 (1509-1708) + 60 (1732-1792) = 259 years; the 24-year Ottoman period in between falls outside this continuity. ⑦ Administrative and political importance: it became the capital of a beylik. Mehmed Bey made Oran the new center of the Western Beylik (one of the three sanjaks of the Regency of Algiers, the others being the Eastern/Constantine and Titteri/Médéa) — the TDV article on Algeria (Cezayir) records this as 'the West (center Mascara, Oran after 1792)'; moving the seat of the beylik from Mascara to Oran made the city no longer merely a port but an administrative capital. Mehmed Bey, who had the city rebuilt, settled the Muslim and Jewish population of the surrounding area here, restored to their original use the mosques that had been converted into churches, and built the mosques of Mustafa Pasha and of Mehmed Bey (Jami al-Basha), as well as a palace. ⑧ Economic importance: the gateway to Tlemcen, then the center of European trade. In the Ottoman period the city returned to its position as 'the port of Tlemcen', and maritime trade revived. After the French occupation (4 January 1831) it changed identity: while the Muslim population migrated to the interior, tens of thousands of French and other Europeans settled in the city, and Oran became the most important center of trade with Europe in Algeria. Today, too, its port competes with that of the capital, Algiers; according to 2009 data the population of the city is 852,000, and that of the province of the same name is 1,453,158.", "29bbef91"], "kisa": ["The city changed hands three times from 1509 to 1792 — behind Spain's persistence, amounting to 259 years in total, and the five separate campaigns the Ottomans undertook to win it back lay the same reason: the western gateway of the Maghreb.", "2f34ebac"], "bag": ["Its importance: because of its position, Oran was a gateway that was both easy to defend and valuable to seize — a port close to Morocco and Spain at the same time, controlling the sea route of the western Mediterranean and the trade of Tlemcen. That is why Spain held it at great expense for two and a half centuries, and the Ottomans tried to retake it in five separate campaigns. The definitive conquest of 1792 raised the city from a mere port to the capital of a beylik; only then was the coastline of the Regency of Algiers completed from end to end — and thirty-eight years later it became one of the first landing points of the French.", "aa3d7b1c"]},
"tartisma-bolge0073-1821-osmanli-ordusu-nerede":{"baslik": ["Where was the Ottoman army while the rebels were taking the towns one by one? The four simultaneous fronts of 1821–1822", "088eff3b"], "metin": ["① The measurable part of the question: how many fronts were there at the same time? Nauplion (Anabolu) fell on 12 December 1822. In that month the military situation of the Ottoman State was as follows. ② The eastern front — Erzurum under siege. According to the TDV article on Iran, using as a pretext the search by Ottoman officials of the caravan that included the shah's wife, who was going on pilgrimage, the Crown Prince Abbas Mirza crossed the border with a large military force in the autumn of 1821, seized the provinces of Kars and Bayazıt and laid siege to Erzurum. ③ The Iraqi front — Baghdad was occupied. According to the same source, Iranian troops simultaneously launched a large-scale attack on Northern Iraq and the province of Baghdad; Sulaymaniyah and Baghdad were occupied and Kirkuk was besieged. What ended the war was not an Ottoman victory: Ottoman resistance and an epidemic that engulfed Azerbaijan forced the Iranians to withdraw; the peace treaty concluded at Erzurum in 1823 stipulated that the Iranian forces would withdraw within sixty days. That is, in the two most critical years of the Greek revolt, the eastern half of the empire was a war zone. ④ The Albanian front — and the detail here is absolutely crucial. Mahmud II dismissed Ali Pasha of Tepelena, the governor of Ioannina, who was behaving semi-independently (March 1820), and sent forces against him. The commander entrusted with this task was Hurşid Ahmed Pasha — and, as the TDV records, Hurşid Pasha was at the same time the man who had been appointed governor of the Morea. That is, when the revolt broke out, the man who should have been governing the Morea was besieging a fortress at Ioannina. Ali Pasha's resistance at Ioannina lasted nearly two years, and in the words of the TDV, during this resistance the Greek revolt became generally widespread. Ali Pasha was killed on 14 February 1822, and his head was brought to Istanbul on 24 February. ⑤ The force he faced was no small one. Ali Pasha had an army of 30,000–40,000 men, 7,000 of them standing troops, and a small navy; the population under his rule is estimated at 1.5–2 million, and his annual revenue amounted to 4–5 million kuruş (TDV). This was not a hunt for bandits but a civil war. The TDV article on Greece states the outcome plainly: the rebellion of Ali Pasha, suppressed in 1822, made the Greek uprising easier and contributed to its success. ⑥ The army itself — it was still the Janissary corps. The Janissary corps stood until 1826, and according to the TDV article on the Janissaries, the series of wars that began in 1768 \"had laid bare that the Janissary army no longer had any military value\". The corps had become an obstacle standing in the way of renewal. The Nizam-i Cedid army founded by Selim III had been disbanded in 1807, and as the TDV records, such implacable enmity had developed between the two systems that even in times of need it had never been possible to use the two forces together. In 1821 the Ottoman State did not have an army trained on the European model. ⑦ And what about Arabia? The Wahhabi front was closed by this date, so it is not included in the list: Diriyah had been besieged on 6 April 1818 and was captured on 9 September 1818 by Egyptian forces under the command of İbrahim Pasha (TDV). But that campaign had been carried out not by the Ottoman army but by the Egyptian army — and this is the clue that leads to the real answer to the question. ⑧ The solution again came from Egypt. The island of Crete was given to Mehmed Ali Pasha so that he would suppress the revolts there, and Egyptian forces landed on Crete in June 1822. As the revolt continued in the Morea, in 1824 the sultan asked Mehmed Ali for help; the Egyptian fleet under the command of İbrahim Pasha landed in the Morea in 1825, made Modon its headquarters and harshly suppressed the revolt (TDV, Greece). The state had become dependent on the army of its own province. ⑨ The short answer: was it weak? According to the sources, the issue was not sheer strength but simultaneity and STRUCTURE. In 1821–1822 the Ottoman State was fighting a state in the east, fighting one of its own governors in the west, grappling with a revolt in the south, and doing all this with a corps which, by its own admission, no longer had any military value. The piling up of these three explains why the towns fell \"one by one\". And the fact that the sultan dared to destroy this corps in 1826, precisely at a time when the external threat was at its height, although it seemed ill-timed, is, in the words of the TDV, an indication of how well he followed developments.", "22e241d0"], "kisa": ["When Nauplion fell, the Ottoman State was occupied in four places at once: Erzurum was under Iranian siege, Baghdad had been occupied, the governor of the Morea was besieging a rebel pasha at Ioannina, and the army itself was still the Janissary corps that was to be abolished in 1826.", "b892b5e7"], "bag": ["Significance: The loss of Nauplion was not merely the loss of a fortress; it was the result of a year in which the commander who should have been governing the Morea was tied down at Ioannina, and the eastern army at Erzurum and Baghdad. Shortly afterwards the town was to become the first capital of the new Greek state.", "30043a3c"]},
"tartisma-bolge0073-cezayir-ordusu-1830":{"baslik": ["Did Algiers have no army? Two separate forces and two separate collapses in 1830", "21831f43"], "metin": ["① The first question: what military force of its own did the Regency of Algiers have? It had one, and for a long time it was formidable. Algiers was part of the system of the western garrison regencies (Garp Ocakları) in North Africa, and this garrison corps (ocak) formed a military aristocracy. The core of the military class consisted of Janissaries sent from Istanbul, headed by an agha. According to estimates, Turkish military strength was around 15-20,000 in Algiers, 6-8,000 in Tunis and 6,000 in Tripoli; in 1634 there were some 20,000 Janissaries in Algiers alone. Those enrolled in the corps received pay and were subject only to their own leaders, and any soldier with ability could rise to the highest office. ② The second pillar: the fleet. One of the most important strengths of the western regencies was their powerful fleets, and it was Algiers that possessed the largest. The seamen lived on the coast, did not mix with the Janissaries and pursued their profession as a separate corps. This naval power was taken so seriously that France, although it had a trade treaty with the Ottoman state, had been obliged to conclude a separate treaty with the dey of Algiers at Marseilles on 21 March 1629; seven years later England and the Netherlands did the same. ③ The third pillar: the tribes — and this pillar was weak from the outset. The corps' territory was extensive, and it was difficult to maintain order away from the coast. The Turks solved this by exempting certain privileged tribes, called the Makhzan, from tax and using them to keep the tax-paying tribes in obedience; they also took advantage of rivalries among the tribes. Since it was hard to collect taxes from nomads, military units were sent out every spring and autumn to collect them — these expeditions were called the \"mahalle\" or \"mahalle-i mansure\". In other words, merely to collect taxes in its own interior, the corps had to mount a campaign twice every year. ④ So why could it not hold out? The decline did not begin in 1830. Toward the eighteenth century, as the French and British navies grew stronger and brought the Mediterranean under their control, the maritime activity of the Algerians was crippled; as Algiers lost its sources of income and its importance, its population began to fall. In the second half of the century the fleet shrank and the number of Janissaries fell from around 20,000 to as low as 5,000. As revenue fell, taxes were raised, and as taxes rose, revolts broke out. The administration too was unstable: of the twenty-eight deys who came to power after 1671, half were killed by the populace and the soldiery. ⑤ And the measure of the force that faced it. On 16 June 1827 France declared war on Algiers and blockaded its coasts; the blockade lasted three years. On 14 June 1830 it sent a great fleet and a fresh force of 37,000 men under the command of General Bourmont. That is, a corps reduced to 5,000 men was confronted by an entirely fresh army seven times its size. The city of Algiers was occupied on 5 July 1830. ⑥ The second question: why did Istanbul not help? On this point the source has no hesitation. When in August 1827 France sent a note to the Sublime Porte asking for permission to punish the dey, the Porte, preoccupied with the Greek revolt, sent Tahir Pasha as mediator, also at Britain's wish; the French seized the pasha's ship and took him to Toulon. The verdict of the TDV is this: the Sublime Porte thought that Algiers was strong enough to fight the French on its own and did not wish to become actively involved in the war — in fact, it had no means of sending forces to Algiers either. ⑦ The calendar of that impossibility. In the months when Algiers fell, the Ottoman state had neither a fleet nor a settled army, and this is not an interpretation but the sequence of dates: the Janissary corps had been abolished on 15 June 1826, and the Asakir-i Mansure-i Muhammediyye founded in its place was only four years old. On 20 October 1827 the combined British, French and Russian fleet burned the Ottoman fleet at Navarino, and France landed troops in the Morea. The following year the Ottoman-Russian war began, and it ended on 14 September 1829 with the Treaty of Adrianople (Edirne). The independence of Greece was accepted in February 1830. When the French army landed at Sidi Ferruch (14 June 1830), it had been three years since the empire lost its fleet and four since it disbanded its army, and it had just emerged defeated from a war. ⑧ Resistance did not end; there was simply no centre. After Hüseyin Pasha, the dey, surrendered and was sent into exile on 9 August 1830, the tribes of the west gathered around Emir Abd al-Qadir and proclaimed him sultan in 1832; in the east Ahmed Bey, the emir of Constantine — himself a kuloğlu (son of a Turkish soldier and a local woman) — carried on the struggle until 1837. It took the French until 1847 to seize the whole country. In 1830 the unmarried Janissaries were sent by ship to Anatolia, the islands, Izmir and Crete. The Ottoman government contented itself with protesting against the occupation, and in 1847, recognising the French occupation, it declared that its rights over Algeria had ended. ⑨ The short answer. Algiers had an army, but it was not the army of half a century earlier: a naval power that drew its income from corsairing, whose number of soldiers fell to a quarter once that income was cut off. As for the Ottoman state, it truly had no power to prevent this, and the source says so openly. The two collapses took place separately; 1830 is only the moment at which both fell in the same year.", "e8ff764a"], "kisa": ["The question is really two questions, and their answers differ. The corps had an army, but within half a century it had shrunk to a quarter; Istanbul, for its part, had no strength at all to send help — and the source says so in its own words.", "4bd6e489"], "bag": ["Significance: 9 August 1830 is the day on which a colour three hundred years old was erased from the map. To understand why that colour could not be defended, one must look not at Algiers but at the Morea, Navarino and Edirne at the same moment — the western end of the empire broke away in the four years when the centre was at its most defenceless.", "24ddf1da"]},
"tartisma-bolge0073-hurmuz-bogazi-el-degistirme":{"baslik": ["Whose was the Strait of Hormuz? Six changes of hands in five hundred years", "07d47cc0"], "metin": ["① First the city moved (1300). Hormuz, which gave the strait its name, was originally a port on the mainland. During the Mongol raids, the ruler Qutb al-Din Tahamtan moved the population to the island of Jarun north of the strait, and this place was called New Hormuz. The ruins of Old Hormuz are today 80 km from Minab. The new port soon became the keystone of the maritime trade route between the Persian Gulf and India, and kept this position until the 17th century (TDV). Ibn Battuta describes it as a large, beautiful and busy trading city; the Russian traveller Afanasy Nikitin, who saw the island in 1472, says that people and goods from every part of the world were to be found there. ② Portugal (1507–1622). Afonso de Albuquerque besieged the island in 1507 but turned back without taking it. When he besieged it again seven years later, taking advantage also of the fact that Shah Ismail, the sultan's overlord, was in those years engaged in a struggle with the Ottoman State, he seized the island and made the sultan, who was a vassal of Iran, subject to Portugal (1514). The Portuguese built a strong fortress surrounded by a moat at a commanding spot on the island and brought the whole strait, the entrance to the Gulf and the Iranian coast opposite under their control; they stopped ships bound for the Hejaz and tormented would-be pilgrims (TDV). But their influence was limited: they could not have much effect on the transit trade going from the coast into the interior of Iran, and had to content themselves with the trade of the island's port. ③ The Ottoman attempts — and neither succeeded. A fleet under the command of Piri Reis besieged the island in 1552 but could not take it. Two years later Seydi Ali Reis, while taking the Ottoman fleet at Basra to Suez, encountered Portuguese ships near Hormuz and defeated them. Another campaign was planned in 1570 but could not be carried out (TDV). The Ottoman State established itself in the Gulf by way of Basra and al-Ahsa, but it never got its hands on the key to the strait. ④ Safavid Iran (1622) — and the port was moved across the water. Hormuz was recovered in the time of Shah Abbas I, 108 years after the Portuguese occupation (23 April 1622); the operation was carried out with the support of the fleet of the English East India Company. By the shah's order the port installations on the island were demolished, Gombroon on the opposite shore was built up with their stones, and all commercial activity was shifted to this new town, named Bandar Abbas after the shah; the military installations were left untouched (TDV). From this date Hormuz declined rapidly and turned into today's small fishing town. The centre of gravity of the strait passed from the island to the mainland and never returned. ⑤ Ya'rubi Oman (from the 1620s). The expulsion of the Portuguese from Muscat and its environs took place in the time of Imam Nasir b. Murshid of the Omani Ya'rubi dynasty (1620); for some time longer the Portuguese continued to collect taxes from Hormuz, Julfar and Qalhat. In 1633 Nasir b. Murshid captured Julfar (present-day Ras al-Khaimah) (TDV, Ras al-Khaimah). The naval power of the Ya'rubis extended as far as East Africa: the colonies of Zanzibar and Mombasa attained autonomous administration in this period. ⑥ The Qawasim (18th century). The Qawasim tribe, which had played a major role in freeing the region from the Portuguese, settled on the Julfar coast. When the Ya'rubis went into decline, the tribal sheikh Rahma al-Matari began to act freely and declared his independence at Ras al-Khaimah, which he had founded beside old Julfar. Matari, who built up a strong navy and land army at Ras al-Khaimah and Sharjah, consolidated his control over the traffic of the Strait of Hormuz after Nader Shah was killed (1747) and Iran lost its influence in the Gulf; in the words of the TDV, this situation continued for about a century. At the beginning of the second half of the 18th century, some of the people of Hormuz, troubled by the Qawasim, migrated to Sharjah. ⑦ Busaidi Oman (after 1741) and the leasing of the island. After the death of Sayf b. Sultan II (1743), the period of the Al Bu Said dynasty began with the seizure of power by Imam Ahmad b. Said. In 1762 Ras al-Khaimah, the centre of the Qawasim, agreed by a treaty to submit to Imam Ahmad, and the two sides tried together to counter the Iranian threat (TDV, Oman). Omani interest in Hormuz began with Imam Ahmad; in 1793 his son Sultan b. Ahmad, by fighting, seized a 150-kilometre strip of the Iranian coast, including the islands of Qeshm and Hormuz. Under the treaty made with Iran, the island was leased to the Sultanate of Muscat and Oman from 1798 (TDV, Hormuz). ⑧ Britain (1798–1820) — not the one who held the lock, but the one who decided about the lock. Britain opened an agency in Muscat (18 January 1798), and Omani–British relations began with this agreement. From the first years of the 19th century it signed non-aggression treaties with the Arab emirates, which it brought to consent by using force through the East India Company; in 1819, in an attack, it seized Ras al-Khaimah, held it under occupation for a short time and destroyed the ships in the harbour. Thereupon Emir Sultan b. Saqr, who had withdrawn to Sharjah, was also obliged to sign a non-aggression treaty (1820, TDV Ras al-Khaimah). Both shores of the strait were now within the British maritime order. ⑨ Iran takes it back (1868). In 1855 the lease of Hormuz was taken up again and its term limited to twenty years. In 1868 Shah Nasir al-Din, who had been looking for an opportunity to break the treaty, withdrew the powers and privileges granted by earlier shahs concerning Bandar Abbas and Hormuz; Iranian rule was re-established on the island in place of the Sultanate of Oman (TDV). ⑩ And the last Ottoman claim. At the beginning of the 20th century the Ottoman State was asserting that Hormuz was among its own territories and was preventing Iran's attempt to build a quarantine station there (TDV). This was the very last Ottoman claim over a strait that it had never been able to seize in four hundred years. ⑪ A summary of five hundred years. The Strait of Hormuz changed hands not through the holding of a small island or port on its shore, but according to whose ships the trade passing over that water depended on. Portugal held it with a fortress; Iran took it by pulling down the fortress and moving the port to the opposite shore; Oman and the Qawasim held it with their fleets; and Britain bound them all by treaty without governing any of them.", "a0f318a2"], "kisa": ["The key to the strait never stayed in the same hands for long: the Kingdom of Hormuz, Portugal, Safavid Iran, Ya'rubi and Busaidi Oman, the Qawasim and finally Britain. Each change of hands came together with a change in the direction of a trade route.", "613f8e60"], "bag": ["Significance: This strait, the only outlet of the Persian Gulf, is the narrowest passage in the atlas where the colours of five different states appear in turn. The Ottoman State entered the Gulf from Basra but could never hold the strait; this is also the geographical logic of today's disputes over Hormuz.", "fbd322af"]},
"tartisma-bolge0073-misir-osmanli-modernlesme":{"baslik": ["What did Egypt do that the Ottomans could not? The modernisation of a province and that of an empire", "3ded2070"], "metin": ["① First let us correct the wrong question. It is not that the Ottoman State \"could not\" modernise, but that it was able to do so only late. According to the TDV article on Mahmud II, the abolition of the Janissary corps (1826) opened the way for the reforms of the final period of the sultan's reign, which was to last thirteen years, and in this new period the old order and its institutions were granted no right to survive whatsoever. The real question is this: why was Mehmed Ali of Kavala able to do this fifteen years earlier? ② The elimination of the rival caste: 1811 and 1826. Mehmed Ali continued the struggle against the Mamluk beys, whom he saw as the greatest obstacle to his rule; in 1808–1810 he neutralised the beys in Upper Egypt, and on 1 March 1811, at a great banquet he gave in honour of his son Tosun, he had all but a few of the Mamluk beys killed (TDV). Mahmud II was able to do the same only on 15 June 1826, and before that he had to spend years in preparation: he made careful appointments to the leading posts of the army, created a new drilled unit under the name of the Eşkinci corps (29 May 1826), and by announcing that these soldiers would begin drill in European-style uniforms on 11 June, he all but drove the Janissaries to revolt; in the words of the TDV, there is no doubt that the last Janissary revolt was not accidental and that it was the sultan's handiwork. ③ The composition of the resistance was different. The TDV describes the first period of Mahmud II as \"the domination of the anti-reform front, which saw its interest in the preservation of the traditional order, was represented chiefly by the military and learned (ilmiye) classes, and was supported by the ayan in Anatolia and Rumelia\". In other words, facing the sultan was a three-legged alliance: the Janissary corps, the ulema and the provincial notables. Nor was the Janissary body simply an army: according to a study based on the Surety Registers of 1791–1793, about 40 per cent of the 1,110 owners of businesses and shops in Istanbul had Janissary connections; the funds of the ortas (Janissary companies) lent money at interest to small tradesmen, and the transport and construction sectors were largely controlled by Janissaries (TDV, Janissaries). To disband an army meant at the same time to dismantle the credit and labour market of the capital. ④ Mehmed Ali also broke the ulema early. The pasha, who increased his control over land through the centralised economic system applied in Egypt, largely broke the power of the ulema, whom he saw as a danger to his rule, by subjecting endowment (waqf) lands to taxation (TDV). What is interesting is that Mahmud II used the same instrument in 1826: an attempt was made to finance the new army through the rational use of endowment wealth, and for this purpose the Ministry of Imperial Endowments was established and all endowment wealth was gathered under its administration. The same remedy, fifteen years apart. ⑤ Scale: a province and an empire. By 1821 Mehmed Ali had registered and taxed all the cultivable land of Lower and Upper Egypt; he abolished the tax-farming (iltizam) system and nationalised the endowment lands, and almost all the land of Egypt became state property (TDV). The Nile valley is a country stretching along a single river, which it is possible to survey and control. To carry out the same operation in an empire stretching from Rumelia to Baghdad, with a separate dynasty of ayan in each of its regions, was an entirely different task. The TDV sums this up as follows: although the case of Mehmed Ali remains the single exception where he failed, Mahmud II succeeded in making the weight of central authority felt over the Ottoman lands to a degree that had not been possible in earlier periods. ⑥ Finance: this is where the real difference lies. Mehmed Ali's system earned the state money — but by taking it from the people. Through the monopoly system, crops were bought very cheaply, and together with heavy taxes this order was \"enriching the administration while impoverishing the people\"; during the pasha's rule the cultivable land doubled, the variety of cotton was improved, and Egypt became one of the world's leading cotton producers (TDV). On the Ottoman side the picture is the reverse: according to the TDV, weakness in the financial sphere was the weakest side of the reign of Mahmud II. The war indemnity paid to Russia was 400 million kuruş — far above the state's total revenues for one year. As a result of devaluation carried out ten times, the silver content of the kuruş fell by 80 per cent. So one side was finding the money for reform, while the other in the same years was losing the value of its money. ⑦ Two programmes side by side. Egypt: in 1809 students began to be sent to Europe; in 1810 the foundation of the navy was laid at Bulaq; in 1816 a school of geometry; in 1820 the first military school in Aswan and the first printing press in Bulaq; in 1827 a medical school; in 1828 the newspaper Vekāyi-i Mısır (Egyptian Events); in 1834 an engineering school; in 1837 the Ministry of Education (TDV). The Ottomans: the Asâkir-i Mansûre-i Muhammediyye (Victorious Soldiers of Muhammad) army in 1826, the Ministry of Imperial Endowments in 1826, Takvîm-i Vekāyi‘ in 1831, the Mekteb-i Maârif-i Adliyye school, the Military Academy in 1835, the Redif (reserve) organisation, the Supreme Council of Judicial Ordinances (Meclis-i Vâlâ-yı Ahkâm-ı Adliyye), quarantine (TDV). The list is almost the same; the dates are shifted by ten to fifteen years. ⑧ And the proof came on the battlefield. This is the TDV's clearest sentence: the fact that the regular Egyptian forces, trained by modern methods, brought under control in a short time a rebellion which Ottoman forces lacking any discipline and training had been unable to suppress for years had a significant psychological effect, especially on public opinion in Istanbul; the success of the trained Egyptian forces was perceived as a development that restored the reputation of Selim III and acknowledged that he had been right about reform. In other words, Egypt's success was not an obstacle to Ottoman reform but its trigger. ⑨ Mehmed Ali was not an Egyptian nationalist. The TDV is clear on this point: the pasha's cultural outlook was an Ottoman one, the language he spoke was Turkish, and his view of the world was a typically Ottoman view; to regard him as the forerunner of Egyptian nationalism is a mistaken approach. The two modernisations were not two rival civilisational projects but applications of the same tradition on two different scales. ⑩ The short answer. It was not a single thing that held the Ottomans back: ① the economic and social roots of the rival military caste in the capital, ② the alliance of the Janissary corps, the ulema and the ayan, ③ the impossibility of controlling land and taxation on the scale of an empire, ④ public finances that collapsed under war indemnity and currency depreciation, ⑤ and the fact of fighting on four separate fronts in the same years. What Egypt had was a single valley, a single ruler, and a rival whose elimination had been completed in 1811.", "93eb986f"], "kisa": ["The sources do not give a single cause. Mehmed Ali eliminated his rival military caste in 1811, whereas Mahmud II was able to do the same only in 1826 — and the moment he did so, he launched a reform programme very similar to Egypt's. The fifteen years in between are the difference in scale and resistance.", "72be9f4e"], "bag": ["Significance: Here lies the root of the Egyptian crisis stretching from Kütahya to Nizip. When an empire that disbanded its army in 1826 came face to face with a province that had done so in 1811, the fifteen-year gap came back in the form of the defeats at Konya in 1832 and Nizip in 1839.", "a05f091f"]},
"tartisma-bolge0073-timar-dunyadaki-benzerleri":{"baslik": ["Was the timar peculiar to the Ottomans? Six different answers to the same question", "21b16865"], "metin": ["① The source's answer: no, it was not peculiar to them — and the reason is no coincidence. The article on the timar in the TDV Encyclopaedia of Islam frames the matter as a structural problem: in a state where the cash economy and the central treasury have not developed sufficiently and where the army consists largely of cavalry, a system of assigning revenues inevitably emerges. The cavalryman's needs could be met only in a rural setting, and the tax levied on the produce of the soil could be turned into cash only in the local market. The outcome the source records is that this system prevailed in ancient Iran, in the Byzantine Empire, in Western Europe, in the Islamic states and in the Turco-Mongol states. So the question is not \"who invented it\" but \"how did everyone solve the same problem\". ② Byzantium — the pronoia. The resemblance is not superficial but direct. In the words of the TDV, the Turkish word \"timar\" (care) is the exact equivalent of the Greek word pronoia. That the number of auxiliary troops a holder of a revenue grant had to bring to the battlefield was proportional to the size of the grant is likewise a practice found both in the Ottoman timar and earlier in the Byzantine pronoia. During their conquest of the Balkans the Ottomans let many pronoia holders carry on in their own pronoias as Ottoman timar holders; being a Muslim was not a precondition for being a sipahi (timar-holding cavalryman), and in some sanjaks the proportion of Christian timar holders reached 35 per cent of all sipahis. This shows that the two systems did not merely resemble each other, but that one could be directly converted into the other. ③ The Islamic world — the iqta. Certain basic features of the timar are seen in Iran under Seljuk rule and in Egypt under Mamluk rule; there too the force the holder of an iqta had to bring was proportional to the size of the iqta. Since the Ottomans came into direct contact with the Mamluks, they very probably knew the iqta system. In Seljuk Anatolia there existed appanage-type timars and military assignments resembling the Ottoman timar. ④ Safavid Iran — the tiyul. In the Iranian lands the word tiyul replaced iqta from the fifteenth century onward; it is first seen in texts of the Karakoyunlu period. In the Safavid era tiyul meant the granting to a person of the right to collect taxes in a particular area, and tiyuls took the place of the iqta system. Tiyuls were granted from state (miri) lands, dynastic estates and private properties, and were regulated by the Divan-i A‘la; their holders were emirs, tribal chiefs and army commanders. The point the source stresses is this: although the term was new, the system was a continuation of the tradition of meeting military expenses with land because of a shortage of cash. ⑤ The soyurghal — and here the real distinction appears. The TDV defines the soyurghal in a single sentence: a kind of land-based grant that carried tax immunity and could pass to heirs. Minorsky defines the tiyul as \"authority to collect taxes for a certain period\" and distinguishes it from the hereditary soyurghal. According to Ortaylı, the soyurghal was a privilege lavishly granted to the old ruling class; a state with a weak bureaucracy preferred to ally with the old provincial elite rather than destroy it and create a vacuum of authority. In other words, two separate models stood side by side in the same region: the one that was time-limited and revocable (tiyul), and the one that was permanent and passed on by inheritance (soyurghal). ⑥ India — the jagir. In the Turkish states of India the iqta was mostly known as the \"jagir\". The jagir method, applied from the time of the Delhi sultanates onward, was systematised under the Mughals: lands, towns and villages belonging to the state were given to statesmen and commanders according to their office and rank, in proportion to the salary assessed for them; the official who was given land was obliged to maintain a certain number of soldiers, to join campaigns with them when required and to keep order in his area. This is almost identical to the scheme of obligations of the Ottoman timar. ⑦ Western Europe — and the one sharp difference. Murakami, who has studied the soyurghal, says that for revenue grants of this kind the terms \"fief and lehen\" are used in Western Europe and the term \"iqta\" in the eastern Islamic world. But the TDV article on the timar states plainly the fundamental difference between the Ottoman system and Western feudal practice: the timar did not pass to heirs by inheritance. The law recognised for the son of a deceased sipahi a share on the scale of an initial timar; but the revenue grant itself did not become family property: it reverted to the state and was redistributed. The second difference concerns the reaya (tax-paying subjects): the Ottoman regime was in principle opposed to any personal bond being formed between the military class and the farmer; the sipahi could not force the peasant to work at his own will, nor could he collect more tax than the law code (kanunname) specified; most of the forced labour services inherited from pre-Ottoman times had been converted into obligations subject to a fee. ⑧ A correction: nor did the timar exist throughout the empire. The typical Ottoman province was one where the timar was in force, but the provinces whose revenues were not distributed as timars were salyaneli (provinces on an annual-remittance basis); the list the source gives is this: Egypt, Baghdad, Yemen, Habesh, Basra, al-Ahsa, Algiers, Tripoli and Tunis. So the system was neither peculiar to the Ottomans nor spread across the whole Ottoman realm. ⑨ Scale. According to the budget of 1527-1528, 46 per cent of Rumelia's total revenue of 198.2 million akçe and 56 per cent of Anatolia's 129.6 million akçe were allotted to has, zeamet and timar holdings; there were 17,288 and 16,468 revenue grants in the two provinces respectively. The sipahi class was the basic unit of the Ottoman military system until, during the Austrian wars of 1593-1606, the need for musket-bearing troops made the employment of salaried soldiers unavoidable. ⑩ The end. Attempts at reform (the regulations of 1777 and 1791) produced no result. In 1827 some 5,200 timar-holding sipahis from fifty-three sanjaks in Rumelia and Anatolia were turned, together with their timar revenues, into cavalry of the Asakir-i Mansure (the new army). However, the timar revenues did not cover their livelihood; some had their timars taken away and were put on monthly pay, while those who wished to retire kept their timars untouched in return for the substitute fee for an armed retainer (cebelü bedeli). A four-hundred-year-old backbone thus gave way to a salaried army and cash taxation.", "7f2fd520"], "kisa": ["According to the source, the timar was not an Ottoman invention but the inevitable solution of every state with an undeveloped cash economy and an army based on cavalry. The real question is not whether the system existed but in which detail it diverged — and the divergence turns on a single point: does the revenue grant pass by inheritance?", "9f9ee9f0"], "bag": ["Significance: On the map the timar is not a colour, but it is how the colour was held. The opening of a region to the timar meant sipahis, registers and permanent administration there; remaining salyaneli meant a province far from the centre, held with salaried garrisons. Part of the difference between Algiers breaking away easily in 1830 and Rumelia not breaking away for centuries lies here.", "0728ecb6"]},
"tartisma-celali-sebepleri":{"baslik": ["What gave rise to the Celalis? — there is no single cause, there are eight explanations", "695d7717"], "metin": ["① Safavid incitement and sect: The first wave (Şahkulu, Nur Ali, Sheikh Celal, Kalender Shah) arose from Turkmen groups nourished by the propaganda of Shah Ismail; this explanation is strong for the early sixteenth century and weak for the later great revolts.\n② Population pressure: In the sixteenth century the population of Anatolia grew rapidly, while the cultivated land did not increase at the same pace. Young men who abandoned their farms had to seek a place in a bey's household, in a fortress or in a madrasa; those who could find none were left adrift (the TDV bases this view on M. A. Cook).\n③ Money and prices: In the financial crisis at the end of the century, debased akçes flooded the market, prices rose, and the sale of grain from the coasts to Europe despite the ban heightened the fear of famine.\n④ Oppression by administrators: Provincial posts were obtained for money, and administrators appointed for short terms collected illegal levies (salgun) from the people. According to a couplet of the age, with offices openly on sale it was impossible for Celalis not to appear. The justice decrees (adaletname) issued from 1565 onward were a response to these complaints.\n⑤ The breakdown of the timar order and the 'victims of office': Almost all the great chiefs were officials of the state who had been dismissed or wronged; those whose revenue grants had been taken away because they had fled a campaign or arrived late also joined them.\n⑥ Turkmen reaction against centralising administration: The TDV says that the Celali movement of the sixteenth century can also be read as the resistance of freely living Turkmen communities to the centre's effort to control them.\n⑦ The spread of the musket: After Prince Bayezid's musket-bearing units, firearms multiplied among the people; contemporary documents refer to the musket outright as a 'cause of disorder', and the state forbade the people to carry muskets.\n⑧ The Haçova deserters: Some sources link the revolts to Cigalazade Sinan Pasha's cancellation in 1596 of the revenue grants of those who had fled the battle. The TDV article on the Celali revolts finds this explanation inadequate: the soldiers who lost their grants swelled Karayazıcı's ranks, but the cause must be sought in the masterless levends, the wronged sipahis, the unemployed suhtes and the unprotected peasantry.", "f352ac3b"], "kisa": ["Sect, population, money, oppression, the timar, the musket… The sources bring different causes to the fore; the TDV article openly rejects the most widespread 'single cause' explanation.", "ccffaaef"], "not": ["According to the TDV, apart from a few exceptions, the Celali chiefs did not aim to wrest Anatolia away from the Ottomans; most sought to obtain a sanjak or a beylerbeylik (governorate-general).", "13184f9b"], "bag": ["The explanations do not exclude one another: ①-⑥ describe the ground of the movement, ⑦ and ⑧ the factors that accelerated it. The real debate is which of them weighed most and how this changed from one period to another.", "a0d2d1f6"]},
"tartisma-dunya-hint-okyanusu-portekiz":{"baslik": ["From the Red Sea to the Indian Ocean: why could the Ottomans not drive the Portuguese from Indian waters?", "6b56575d"], "metin": ["① The route changed. Vasco da Gama's rounding of southern Africa and reaching India in 1497 shook the monopoly on trade and pilgrimage routes that the Red Sea had carried for centuries. Using this new route, the Portuguese built forts at the key points of the Indian Ocean: in 1509 they forced the governor of Surat in Gujarat into the treaty they wanted, on 10 August 1511 they took and plundered Malacca, and in 1513 they landed on Dahlak at the entrance to the Red Sea. Malacca, having fallen into Portuguese hands, never regained its former commercial supremacy; Muslim merchants moved to the port of Aceh in Sumatra.\n\n② An inheritance was taken over. When the Ottomans took Egypt in 1517, they also inherited the struggle the Mamluks had begun against the Portuguese. On 18 April 1517 Selman Reis defended Jeddah against a Portuguese fleet of thirty ships; in the report he presented to İbrahim Pasha in 1525, he listed one by one the Portuguese forts at Hormuz, Diu, Goa, Calicut, Cochin, Ceylon and Sumatra and proposed that they be recovered. By taking Aden in 1538, Hadım Süleyman Pasha secured the southern mouth of the Red Sea.\n\n③ Three expeditions on the ocean, three retreats. That same year Hadım Süleyman's fleet crossed to India and besieged the Portuguese fort of Diu; the outer forts fell and the Portuguese withdrew into the inner citadel, but when the support expected from Gujarat did not come, the Ottoman troops withdrew on the night of 6 November 1538, leaving behind some of their heavy guns. In 1552 Piri Reis sailed from Suez and took Muscat, but could not capture the fortress of Hormuz; fearing a surprise attack by a superior Portuguese fleet, he left his main fleet at Basra and returned, and at the end of 1553 he was executed in Egypt. In 1554, while trying to take the ships at Basra to Suez, Seydi Ali Reis twice clashed with a Portuguese fleet beyond Hormuz, was driven by a storm to the coast of Gujarat, lost his ships, and could return to Istanbul only overland, by way of India, Central Asia and Iran.\n\n④ Why? Three causes stand out in the account of the sources. The sea: TDV says that Hadım Süleyman's sailors were 'accustomed to the conditions of the Mediterranean'; they struggled against the monsoon winds. The Portuguese fleet facing Seydi Ali Reis consisted entirely of galleons, whereas the Ottomans put out onto the ocean in oared galleys (kadırga and baştarda). BASES: the Portuguese held permanent, fortified bases at Goa, Diu, Hormuz and Malacca; the Ottoman footholds opening onto the ocean were Suez, Yemen and Basra, and they had no place on the Indian coast to resupply and winter. Allies: the sultans of Gujarat swung back and forth between the two sides — in 1535 Bahadur Shah gave the Portuguese permission to build a fort at Diu, and in 1538 the expected help did not come. In 1567 a defense treaty was made with the Sultanate of Aceh; most of the mission and soldiers sent there settled and mingled with the local people, and Malacca remained Portuguese.\n\n⑤ The outcome — a loss or a boundary? The Ottomans could not win the ocean, but neither did they lose the Red Sea: the Portuguese could not establish themselves at Jeddah or Aden, and the pilgrimage route and Egypt remained secure. As TDV notes, in this new period the Red Sea's character as a trade route weakened, while its character as a pilgrimage route continued. Portugal's loss of Malacca was the work not of the Ottomans but of another European power: in January 1641 the city passed into the hands of the Dutch.", "a26c9b81"], "kisa": ["The Ottomans held the Red Sea and Basra and protected the pilgrimage route; but in no expedition could they take the Portuguese forts at Diu, Hormuz, Goa and Malacca. There was no single cause: a navy built for the Mediterranean, the lack of a base on the Indian coast and unreliable allies.", "56d5bea0"], "bag": ["Significance: the fall of Malacca (1511) is a turning point showing that Asian trade was shifting from the Red Sea to the Cape of Good Hope. The Ottomans stopped this shift in the Red Sea but could not stop it on the ocean — and the cause of that boundary was not a single defeat but the sum of shortcomings in type of fleet, bases and allies.", "38f8dcbc"]},
"tartisma-isyanciya-makam":{"baslik": ["Calming revolt with office — why did the Ottomans make rebels pashas?", "1d3ceb43"], "metin": ["Examples (only what the source relates):\n\n• Karayazıcı Abdülhalim (1600) — when hard pressed at Urfa, he asked for quarter; in return for the promise of the sanjak of Amasya or Antep, he handed over Hüseyin Pasha, who was with him. The promise was not kept and forces marched against him, but under pressure from certain circles in Istanbul he was later given the governorship of the sanjak of Çorum. Troops were sent against him again on the grounds that he was oppressing the people.\n\n• Deli Hasan (1603) — the Celali chief who took Kütahya and marched on Afyonkarahisar was made beylerbey of Bosnia when he asked for pardon. In the source's words, the matter was settled 'amicably'.\n\n• Tavil Ahmed (1605) — following the example of Deli Hasan, he was offered the beylerbeylik of Shahrizor; he did not accept it and besieged Harput.\n\n• Canbolatoğlu Ali Pasha (1606-1607) — to keep him from growing stronger, a diploma appointing him governor of Aleppo was sent to him, but his revolt did not stop. After his defeat he took refuge with the sultan and was pardoned, and received the beylerbeylik of Temeşvar (and then of Belgrade); a short while later he was killed.\n\n• Kalenderoğlu Mehmed and Muslu Çavuş (1607-1608) — Kuyucu Murad Pasha gave one of them the sanjak of Ankara and the other that of İçel. This was not a pardon but a military ruse: to keep them occupied and prevent them from joining forces.\n\n• Gürcü Abdünnebi and Katırcıoğlu Mehmed (1649) — on condition that they disband, one was offered the office of agha of the Turkmens and the other a sanjak, but no agreement was reached. Katırcıoğlu, who asked for pardon after his defeat, was given the sanjak of Beyşehir and later the beylerbeylik of Karaman; he fought against Venice in Crete and fell as a martyr before Candia on 17 December 1668.\n\n• Pazvandoğlu Osman (1799) — the ayan (provincial notable) who defied the state at Vidin received, after the failure of the army sent against him and after the French landing in Egypt, first the rank of kapıcıbaşı (chief gatekeeper) and then, on 21 June 1799, that of vizier. When he did not submit, his vizierate was revoked in November 1800 and restored on conditions in August 1802.", "d36053aa"], "kisa": ["Deli Hasan became beylerbey of Bosnia, Katırcıoğlu beylerbey of Karaman, Pazvandoğlu a vizier. Was the state helpless, or was it acting with calculation?", "25863845"], "not": ["What the examples have in common is that the state was fighting on two fronts at the same time (Austria and Iran; France in 1799). The case of Kalenderoğlu and Muslu Çavuş must be kept separate: there the office was not a compromise but part of the plan of suppression.", "1a050a86"], "bag": ["The TDV article on the Celali revolts weighs both sides of this method together. The gain: pardoned chiefs were mostly given posts on the Rumelian frontier; besieged Anatolian cities were saved, and the forces and supplies to be allotted to the fronts were freed. The price: the state was reluctant to grant horsetail standards and sanjak banners, because very few of those pardoned remained obedient and most carried on their old habits in their new posts; moreover, the chiefs' men were entered in the registers of the standing army (kapıkulu) contrary to the law, and this hastened the deterioration of those corps.", "8185ab6f"]},
"tartisma-kadinlar-saltanati":{"oneri": ["The term was invented in 1916 and was a criticism from the outset; today's scholarship reads the same phenomenon the other way round.", "62fac1d6"], "ad": ["The “Sultanate of Women” — a period or an accusation?", "ba2da7fd"], "metin": ["The name for the period between the end of the 16th century and the middle of the 17th century, in which valide sultans and haseki sultans were markedly influential in state affairs, was given by the book “Kadınlar Saltanatı” (The Sultanate of Women), published by the historian Ahmed Refik Altınay in 1916. The term was born within the historiography of the time of its publication, which read the empire along a line of “stagnation and decline”, and it named the period as one of interference by women and the harem in the government of the state, that is, within a negative framework. TDV's 'Valide Sultan' article also refers by this name to the span between the last quarter of the 16th century and the middle of the 17th century, when valide sultans interfered directly in state affairs, and writes that the period closed in 1656 when Turhan Sultan transferred power to Köprülü Mehmed Pasha. Later academic studies began to question this naming itself; one of the most frequently cited works in this field is Leslie Peirce's book “The Imperial Harem: Women and Sovereignty in the Ottoman Empire” (Oxford University Press, 1993). The question debated in this literature today is not only “did women really rule” but also “why, and in whose eyes, did this period receive this name”. The debate over the phenomenon itself, that is, over the extent to which women ruled, is the subject not of this card but of the “Sultanate of Women” card in the 'Curiosities' section. This distinction is important today too: although in popular usage the term passes as a neutral name for a period, at its origin lies a critical point of view of the early 20th century.", "99c617bb"], "kisa": ["The term was invented in 1916 and was a criticism from the outset; today's scholarship reads the same phenomenon the other way round.", "62fac1d6"], "bag": ["This card tells only the history of the term. For three views on whether the period really was a form of government, see the “Sultanate of Women” card in the 'Curiosities' section.", "29f8f7d5"]},
"tartisma-karadeniz-etnik-genel":{"oneri": ["In 1500 the steppe was home to Turko-Tatar nomads, the coastal towns to Greek, Armenian and Jewish merchants,…", "7d5a9fde"], "ad": ["Whose homeland was the land north of the Black Sea? Four hundred years of population change on the steppe", "8018313f"], "metin": ["The steppe north of the Black Sea was the homeland not of a single people but of a layered population. Within the Crimean peninsula and on the steppe to its north lived the Crimean Tatars and the Nogai hordes loosely bound to the Crimean khan. The population of the coastal towns, however, was mixed: according to TDV's article on the Crimea, in the tahrir registers of 1542 the population of Caffa was about 16,000 and consisted mostly of Christians. In the towns the Greek, Armenian and Jewish (mostly Karaite) communities together outnumbered the Muslim population.\n\nIn the 16th century two Cossack communities appeared on the northern and eastern edge of the steppe. The Zaporozhian Cossacks lived below the Dnieper rapids, and the Don Cossacks along the lower course of the Don. The mountainous region between the Kuban and the Black Sea coast was the homeland of the Circassians (Adyghe). From the 17th century onwards the Kalmyks led a nomadic life on the steppe between the Don and the Volga.\n\nThis picture changed radically in the last quarter of the 18th century. As a result of the annexation of the Crimea (1783), the dispersal of the Nogai hordes, the migrations of the Crimean Tatars and Circassians to Ottoman lands and the Russian state's opening of the south to planned settlement, in the census of 1897 the majority of the population in all the provinces of this region spoke Ukrainian or Russian.", "591db57b"], "kisa": ["In 1500 the steppe was home to Turko-Tatar nomads, the coastal towns to Greek, Armenian and Jewish merchants, and the Dnieper and Don river lands to the Cossacks. By 1900, Ukrainians and Russians made up the majority of the population in the same lands.", "a3766d9b"], "not": ["When reading the map: what the atlas colours is political ownership, not population. That a region appears in the colour of the Crimean Khanate or of Russia does not tell us who lived there. The cards below separate the two.", "8413e16d"]},
"tartisma-kucuk-kaynarca-rivayetleri":{"baslik": ["Three stories told about Küçük Kaynarca — and the answer of the sources", "854b770e"], "metin": ["① 'The comfort of an elbow': It is told that the second delegate, İbrahim Münib Efendi, dozed off during the talks leaning on his elbow; that on waking and seeing everyone looking at him, he said, as though he had been following the discussion, 'let us come to the matter of the indemnity'; and that he thereby cost the state 4.5 million rubles in an indemnity the Russians had already given up. According to TDV, this has no basis beyond being an invented tale: the Russians never gave up the indemnity and had demanded 50,000 purses at Bucharest; the grand vizier had authorised the chief plenipotentiary to accept a demand of up to 20,000 purses without prolonging the matter and, if necessary, to go up to 40,000 purses. Since the indemnity was a separate article left to the very end, it was natural that it should be raised last.\n\n② 'Revenge for the Pruth': It is asserted that although the treaty was ready on 17 July, the Russians held it back until 21 July, the day on which the Treaty of the Pruth had been signed (Hammer, Zinkeisen, Jorga, Danişmend). According to TDV, the grand vizier's approval arrived on 21 July and the treaty was signed the same day; moreover, the Treaty of the Pruth too had been signed not on 21 but on 22 July and exchanged on the 23rd.\n\n③ 'In memory of Weissmann': The claim that the peace was made at Küçük Kaynarca in memory of the Russian general Weissmann, who had died there a year earlier, also appears only in Hammer; according to TDV, Rumyantsev's route and the fact that the peace could not have been known for certain in advance make such an intention implausible.\n\nNor is the account true that the treaty was 'dictated in a brief meeting of four to seven hours': talks were held for hours on three separate days, and the understandings previously reached at Bucharest were taken as the basis.", "71d21aab"], "kisa": ["A dozing delegate who brought up the indemnity, a date awaited to avenge the Pruth, a village chosen in memory of a general… TDV finds all three unfounded.", "51583f1f"], "not": ["The date of the treaty is also given differently in the sources: archival copies take 16 July, when the talks began, some primary sources take 17 July, and the Russian side takes 26 July, when the texts were exchanged. The date of signature is 21 July 1774.", "a379beab"], "bag": ["All three stories serve the same function: attributing a heavy defeat to an individual's lapse or to the enemy's symbolic calculation. TDV's objection, by contrast, rests on archival documents and contemporary Ottoman sources (Enveri, Vasıf, Ahmed Resmi).", "df4b9abe"]},
"tartisma-osman-gazi-olum-yili":{"baslik": ["Did Osman Gazi see the conquest of Bursa? — the debate over the year of his death", "ed71678c"], "metin": ["The TDV article on Osman I derives the year of his death not from the narrative of the chronicles but from a comparison of two documents. In the endowment deed (vakfiye) of Asporça Hatun, drawn up at the beginning of September 1323, Osman is alive; in the Mekece endowment deed dated 1324, he now appears to be dead. His death is therefore placed in the year 724 (1324). The same article also relates the Ottoman tradition: in that account, when Osman died Orhan was engaged in the siege of Bursa — and Bursa did not surrender until 6 April 1326. The chronicles write that because of the gout in his foot Osman withdrew, leaving affairs to Orhan, and that after 1305 his name no longer appears in the campaigns. The TDV article on Orhan goes one step further: it says that from the testimony of Ibn Battuta one may conclude that Osman's actual rule as bey ended around 1322. In accordance with his will, Osman was buried in the citadel of Bursa beneath the dome of the old monastery called Gümüşlükubbe (the Silver Dome); when this building collapsed in the earthquake of 1855, the present plain tomb was built by Sultan Abdülaziz in 1863.", "3649e4a7"], "kisa": ["The chronicles have him die while his son was before Bursa; two endowment deeds, however, show that he died two years before the city fell.", "ec818ca5"], "not": ["The detail that news of the conquest was brought to Osman on his deathbed does not appear in the TDV articles on Osman I, Orhan and Bursa; these articles relate only the tradition that \"when he died, Orhan was besieging Bursa\".", "a5d09f80"], "bag": ["The two accounts are not measuring the same thing. The endowment deeds provide a documentary date and support 1324; linking the death to the siege of Bursa comes from the chronicle tradition. For a bey who had fallen ill and long since left affairs to his son, to say \"he saw Bursa\" and to say \"he died without seeing it\" are each separate claims. The two entries in the chronology carry this distinction side by side: the entry on his death gives 1324, while the entry on the conquest of Bursa gives the tradition.", "f43b5e36"]},
"teknik-tashih-i-sikke-mecidiye-cift-metal":{"baslik": ["The mecidiye and the bimetallic system — why was the currency rebuilt in 1844, and why did it last seventy years?", "f8a41daa"], "metin": ["■ The problem: a currency with a corrupted standard\nFor more than a century the Ottoman kuruş had continuously lost value. According to Şevket Pamuk's calculation in TDV, the pure silver in one kuruş fell from 14.5 grams in 1720 to 2.4 grams in 1824 and to 1 gram in 1844. Over the same period, from the mid-1760s to 1844, food prices in Istanbul rose ten to fifteen times; the fastest increase was between 1808 and 1840. A great many coins of different types were in circulation on the market, some of them debased (mağşûş).\n\n■ DECISION AND IMPLEMENTATION: A CHANGE THAT TOOK FOUR YEARS\nThe new monetary system was launched by a decree dated 26 Safar 1256 (29 April 1840); the equipment needed for the mint was ordered from London. The old coins were definitively withdrawn from circulation on 25 Jumada al-Akhir 1259 (23 July 1843). The first mecidiye was struck on 22 April 1844, the half mecidiye on 18 May and the quarter mecidiye on 19 May 1844. Pamuk calls the operation the 'coinage correction (tashîh-i sikke) of 1844'.\n\n■ WHAT IS BIMETALLISM?\nBoth gold and silver circulating as full-value money, with the ratio between them FIXED by the state. Under the 1844 standard, 1 kuruş contained 1 gram of pure silver and 1 gold lira 6.6 grams of pure gold, and 100 silver kuruş equaled 1 gold lira. The ratio that follows from this: 100 / 6.6 = 15.1 — that is, 1 gram of gold was worth 15.1 grams of silver. In Pamuk's words, the Ottoman state thus passed to a 'bimetallic monetary system defined by a fixed exchange rate'.\n\n■ WHAT IS THE MECIDIYE?\nThe name of the new coins struck in Abdülmecid's name. According to TDV, the name came into general use especially for the 20-kuruş SILVER coin; gold mecidiyes were also struck. By Abdülmecid's death, 15,312,329 gold and 54,987,960 silver mecidiyes had been minted.\n\n■ SIGNIFICANCE: PERMANENCE\nAccording to Pamuk, the real significance of the reform is that the coinage standards adopted, unlike earlier ones, were lasting and could be maintained until 1914: six Ottoman sultans had money struck to the same standard. After a century of debasement (tağşîş, lowering of the standard), this was the first lasting arrangement that stabilized the value of money again.\n\n■ THE END: THE FALL OF SILVER\nThe bimetallic system rested on a fixed ratio; when silver became abundant in the world in the 1870s and its value fell, this ratio could no longer be sustained. In 1879 the Ottoman state also moved to abolish the bimetallic system.", "ae8e3818"], "kisa": ["The silver in one kuruş was 14.5 grams in 1720 and had fallen to 1 gram by 1844. The coinage correction (tashîh-i sikke) halted this slide: it tied the silver kuruş and the gold lira to a fixed ratio — and this standard did not change until 1914.", "a3d5d64b"]},
"varvar-ali-pasa-perihan-hanim-1648":{"soru": ["Which orders did Varvar Ali Pasha refuse before rebelling?", "b228f039"], "baslik": ["The pasha who would not send a woman to the palace died at the hands of her husband", "4f8ec1f0"], "metin": ["Varvar Ali Pasha, who as a Bosnian village boy was taken in the devshirme, rose in the palace through falconry and told the story of his life in a verse work of his own, received two orders from Istanbul in the autumn of 1647, while he was beylerbeyi of Sivas. The first demanded 30,000 kuruş under the name of 'holiday pocket money'; the pasha had the revenue of the province calculated and reported that this sum could not be paid. The second was that Perihan Hanım, the lawful wife in Sivas of İpşir Mustafa Pasha, beylerbeyi of Anatolia, should be sent to Istanbul to be presented to the sultan; according to Kâtib Çelebi, the pasha rejected this too. He was dismissed, did not let those sent to kill him into the city, proclaimed that the affairs of state were left in the hands of women, and decided to march on Istanbul. Evliya Çelebi claims that some circles in the capital, even Kösem Sultan, encouraged him with letters. Having defeated and captured Köprülü Mehmed Pasha, Varvar Ali was surprised on 20 May 1648 in a raid by İpşir Mustafa Pasha, whom he thought had come to join him; the man whose wife he had refused to send to the palace had him seized and executed.", "f9b087d7"], "not": ["The secret letters of support from Istanbul appear only in Evliya Çelebi's account.", "abc67e71"]},
"yeniceri-ocagi-kurulusu":{"sebep.b": ["The need of the principality's army, from Orhan Gazi onward, with the crossing into Rumelia and the growing conquests, for a standing, salaried infantry force tied to the center", "cfd3ee6b"], "sonuc.b": ["With the accession of Murad I, the establishment of the pençik system and of the Novice Corps (Acemi Ocağı) at Gallipoli — the nucleus of the Janissary organization", "88edaafc"], "bag": ["According to the TDV Encyclopaedia of Islam, the idea of taking one-fifth (pençik) of the prisoners of war, training them and adding them to the standing army matured gradually between the end of Orhan's reign and the time of Murad I; historians still debate the exact moment of foundation, and some point to the reign of Bayezid I the Thunderbolt.", "6ddd1be6"], "metin": ["The Janissaries were the infantry branch of the kapıkulu (the sultan's salaried household troops): youths recruited through the devshirme and pençik systems were trained in the Novice Corps and then enrolled in companies called 'orta'. The number of ortas reached 165 in the reign of Suleiman the Lawgiver and later 196; the corps was organized with the ranks of çorbacı and agha and was an institution receiving a regular salary (ulufe) from the center. Mahmud II abolished the corps by turning cannon on it in the Auspicious Incident (Vak'a-i Hayriyye) of 15-16 June 1826.", "b53f43ad"]},
"yenilesme-karsiti-ayaklanmalar-etiket":{"baslik": ["“Uprisings of Those Who Did Not Want Innovation” — What the Sources Say in Five Cases", "ff766e99"], "metin": ["In Ottoman history the uprisings that broke out right after a move toward modernization form a series, and they are often told with a single formula: a rising by those opposed to innovation. Below, five cases are listed with their reasons as far as the source states them; let the reader weigh for himself or herself how far the formula holds in each case. (The scope of this card is internal to the Ottoman state; for a world comparison see the card `kiyas-yenilige-tepki-ayaklanmalari`, and for a typology of the depositions of sultans the card `tartisma-osmanli-darbeleri-tipoloji` — neither has been repeated here.)\n\n① 1622 · The Incident of Young Osman (Osman II). The revolt began on 8 Rajab 1031 / 19 May 1622, and the sultan was strangled at Yedikule the next day. The trigger was the preparation the sultan was making to go on pilgrimage: a rumor spread that this preparation was a plan to gather troops from Anatolia and do away with the Janissaries. Here the TDV Encyclopaedia of Islam enters an important caveat: the reports that the sultan had given up on Istanbul, that he would march against the Janissaries with troops from Damascus and Egypt and that he would move the capital are “the propaganda of those opposed to him, which is reflected even in the sources of the period”; the account of Hüseyin Tuği, who narrated the event through the eyes of the kapıkulu troops hostile to Osman II, passed into later chronicles and led modern historiography to regard him as a ruler who was “a reformer, an innovator, wishing to carry out wide-ranging reforms”. In other words, both the portrait of the “innovative sultan” and that of a “revolt against innovation” may have been fed by the same hostile source. And the reason for the ulema's resentment was not reform but the cutting off of their arpalık (stipendiary revenue assignments).\n\n② 1730 · The Patrona Halil revolt. It began in Beyazıt on 12 Rabi al-Awwal 1143 / 25 September 1730; on the night of 1 October Damad Ibrahim Pasha was surrendered for execution, and on 2 October Ahmed III gave up the throne. The TDV Encyclopaedia of Islam's list of causes is long, and innovation is not at the head of it: a political, economic and social reaction going back to Passarowitz, the loss of Belgrade and Temeşvar, the failure suffered in Iran, the burden of the imdadiyye (emergency aid) taxes, the granting of mukataas (revenue farms) for life, the tax burden on the artisans' guilds, and the luxurious spending of the statesmen, verging on extravagance. The TDV Encyclopaedia of Islam states explicitly that activities such as the opening of the printing press, the translation committee, the libraries, tile-making, the paper and textile workshops and the fire brigade “did not present a situation of concern to the broad masses of the people”; what attracted attention was the pavilions in the French architectural style, the luxury and the dissipation. Moreover, the preparation of the revolt had been undertaken by the grand vizier's own son-in-law, the kaymakam Mustafa Pasha, who had his eye on office.\n\n③ 1807 · The Kabakçı revolt. This is the case in the series that best fits the formula of “opposition to modernization”: the spark is directly the Nizam-i Cedid — the bringing of the yamaks into this system and the incitement that they would be made to wear Nizam-i Cedid uniforms (TDV `kabakci-isyani`). Yet even here the deputy grand vizier and the Shaykh al-Islam stand behind the scene of the revolt; for details see the card `kabakci-isyani-niye-cikti`.\n\n④ 1859 · The Kuleli Incident. A conspiratorial society that was broken up on 14 September 1859, through an informer, before it had even gone into action. The causes counted by the TDV Encyclopaedia of Islam are: the discontent that the Tanzimat measures produced in some quarters, the financial shock after the Crimean War, the reaction against European-style manners among the upper class, and unease over the rights granted to non-Muslims by the Reform Edict of 1856 — but at the same time soldiers and officials who “had not been brought to the positions and offices they wanted”. Here too the label is not single: Namık Kemal interpreted the Kuleli Incident as a movement for liberty and found that the secret trial of the accused by the council of ministers was contrary to the legal principles of the Rescript of Gülhane. The TDV Encyclopaedia of Islam also writes that the movement later influenced the Young Ottomans and the Young Turks.\n\n⑤ 1909 · The Thirty-First of March Incident. 31 March 1325 in the Rumi calendar / 13 April 1909; it lasted eleven days, the Action Army took control of the city on 24 April, and on 27 April Abdülhamid II was deposed. The soldiers of the 4th Rifle (Avcı) Battalion who mutinied rose with a demand for the sharia; among the demands the rebels conveyed to parliament was also the full application of the provisions of the sharia. But the list of causes counted by the TDV Encyclopaedia of Islam is far broader: excessive politicization in the army, the conflict between officers risen from the ranks and academy-trained officers and the purge of the former, the loss of privileges by the old cadres, a sweeping reorganization and reduction of the bureaucracy, the struggle for power between the Committee of Union and Progress and the Ahrar (Liberal) Party, the repressive attitude of the Unionists, and assassinations by unknown perpetrators. And the TDV Encyclopaedia of Islam makes the label itself a matter of debate: it was the Unionists who applied the description “reactionary uprising”; according to the article, in both Unionist and Islamist literature this description “has been seen as an instrument of the desire to bring back the era of despotism”, and “in our recent history the reactionary aspect of the 31 March Incident has been brought to the fore as more important than the incident itself”, serving as historical evidence for later politics. Moreover, Islamist figures of the period such as Elmalılı Muhammed Hamdi, Manastırlı İsmail Hakkı and Mehmed Akif openly declared that what had happened was unacceptable from the standpoint of Islam and that constitutional government could not be given up.\n\n📌 three patterns that emerge from the five cases — not a verdict, but an observation:\n· None has a single cause. In every case grievances over pay, arpalık, office and taxes are found within the same crowd as the complaint about innovation; it is often impossible to separate the two.\n· The crowd's reasons and the plotters' reasons may not be the same: in 1730 the grand vizier's son-in-law, in 1807 the deputy grand vizier and the Shaykh al-Islam, and in 1909 party struggle stand behind the scene.\n· It is most often the victor who has applied the label. The descriptions “reaction” in 1909 and “revolt against a reforming sultan” in 1622 come from sources that were themselves parties to the conflict. This does not mean that the uprisings were not opposed to innovation; it means that the description and the reasons must be read separately.", "5d0d6e5c"]},
"zimmi-cizye-millet-duzeni":{"sebep.b": ["The fact that with the conquest of Istanbul the empire's most populous non-Muslim communities became subjects in a single center, and the need for an institutional framework to administer this population", "e3883d25"], "sonuc.b": ["The abolition of the jizya by the Reform Edict of 1856 (Islahat Fermanı) and the imposition of the obligation of military service on non-Muslims — the legal end of the four-century-old dhimma order", "117c6dd5"], "bag": ["The TDV article on the dhimmi (Zimmî) states that the classical order was “to a significant extent abolished after the Tanzimat”; the article on the jizya (Cizye) records that the Reform Edict of 1856 abolished the jizya and that it was replaced by the military exemption tax (bedel-i askerî), which lasted until 1907. According to the article on the millet, the Armenian millet had been established in 1461 when Mehmed the Conqueror appointed a patriarch in Istanbul; after 1856 lay elements entered communal administration and the powers of the patriarchates were reduced.", "9ca5302e"], "metin": ["A dhimmi is a non-Muslim who lives as a citizen in the abode of Islam and has been given a guarantee of protection: his life and property are inviolable, his worship is free, and he has the right to take disputes within his own community to his own court; in return he recognizes the sovereignty of the state and pays the jizya. The jizya was levied only on free, adult males who were able to work and able to pay — women, children, the elderly, the blind, the disabled, the poor and clergy who had withdrawn to a life of worship were exempt — and in the Ottoman state it was divided into three classes: âlâ, the highest (48 dirhams), evsat, the middle (24), and ednâ, the lowest (12). The communities were governed through the millet organization; this organization was based not on ethnic or linguistic affiliation but on religion and denomination, which is why Gregorian, Catholic and Protestant Armenians were counted as three separate millets. The patriarch and the chief rabbi collected taxes on behalf of the state, governed the community, applied the law and supervised education; but this authority was under the control of the state — in the 17th century alone the Greek Orthodox patriarch was changed fifty-eight times.", "4b065364"]}
});
